re, it's also about where they are from. You mentioned those seven battlegrounds that we're keeping our eyes all the way up until Election Day, now just about 35 days away. Well, take a look. Three of them are in this area. You heard Rick mention it, Wisconsin, Michigan, also Pennsylvania. You obviously have Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . He's from Minnesota. Right here you have Wisconsin. Right next to it, you have Michigan.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He has been to Wisconsin five times. He's been to Michigan four times. Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  has been to both of these states six times. Part of the reason why the Harris campaign, the Trump campaign selected both of these vice presidential candidates is because they're hoping they appeal to these voters right here in the Midwest, in these critical battleground states, these blue-collar voters. Also, of course, we have to mention that Senator JD Vance is from that critical battleground of Ohio. Right next to it, all-important Pennsylvania. David, you mentioned it, both candidates see this as a must-win.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right through the Rust Belt State, you can see the through line. Rachel Scott with us here tonight as. We're gonna come back to these polls a little later on. Because, again, it's what everyone wants to know, just how close is this race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 When we come back here tonight, Terry Moran standing by in Springfield, Ohio. Of course, that community came under intense national scrutiny during the Presidential Debate, dogs and cats and what JD Vance had once falsely claimed. So, we're gonna go live to Terry Moran there, what voters in that community expect to hear tonight in this vice presidential debate. And then, Donna Brazil, Reince Priebus, Democrats, Republicans, if they were in JD Vance's ear, if they were in \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's ear, what would they tell both of these gentleman they have