t right now it feels like it's being shoved in the faces of these vice presidential candidates. And as we are in the midst of this retaliatory cycle that seems to have no easy fixes or solutions, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, there's really no way to avoid it at this point, Matt Gutman, at least addressing it politically for both of these campaigns. Matt, thanks. Iran's attack, of course, came after Israel killed the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader in that devastating airstrike in Beirut. Let's get right to our chief foreign correspondent Ian Pannell. He's been in Beirut for many days now for us. Ian?\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, David, I mean, we've talked about the end of the strikes in Israel. There had been more strikes ongoing here tonight, but also for the last fortnight across Lebanon. We are, we've been focused on Israel, quite rightly, for the last few hours. But the bombardments in Beirut just hasn't stopped. There have been thousands of Israeli strikes here in the last couple of weeks, and no sign it's ending, especially with Israeli troops now on the ground with reporting on hundreds dead, including women and children.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, the crisis in the Middle East is gonna be one of the most, I think, pressing (inaudible) foreign policy challenges for whichever team is gonna win the election. As you say, the Vice Presidential candidates are likely to face those questions early on. The events of the last year, and especially just in the last few hours, kind of laid bare those deep divisions in this region. And I think, front and center is gonna be Iran and as Matt said, the proxies especially here in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the challenges to American power and influence, not just about Israel, for many people in the region, this is about the relationship with America. And it's gonna be a foreign p