h. In a moment, there's a picture of Governor Walz. Mary Bruce just showed it to me on her phone that's just come in tonight. The story behind it kind of surprising.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Little bit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just outside the door here in Central Park, that's the clue. We'll be right back, just minutes away from the Vice Presidential Debate. We'll carry it live here on ABC.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, let's get right to our political Powerhouse team. About two minutes, two and half minutes to go. Mary Bruce, there's a picture floating on the Internet that might be Governor Walz.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Yeah, we haven't confirmed that it is in fact him in this picture, but the campaign does tell me that he did go out for a jog this afternoon through Central Park. A reporter spotted him apparently there with the Secret Service behind him, undetected it appears by everyone around him, perhaps a chance to shake off those nerves. And David, I'm gonna outsource my final thought here because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has just tweeted. She says, "\uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , it is game time. I'm excited for the country to see you in action."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Game time. Oh, (inaudible) will time to put the suit back on. All right.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , what are looking for tonight?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I'm looking for does, does JD Vance do anything to appeal beyond the MAGA base? He is a Trump guy. That's why he was chosen. Does he try to appeal to the middle? And, you know, Walz has not done a single national television solo in - interview since he became the, the nominee. I'm looking to how he does now that he's finally answering questions after the convention.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they told us that he hadn't used a teleprompter before. But Martha Raddatz, you saw him at the convention. He did just fine with the prompter.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He, he did. And I'll say what I'm looking for tonight, is it the offensive line coach or the defensive line coach. Is he just blocking those tackles, or is he making a positive new case for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Linsey Davis?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Style and substance. You know, a quarter of Americans apparently say they don't know who they are. Tell us who you are, what you stand for, could you step in and be president?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 You sound like someone who's moderated a debate. Thanks to the whole team. We're gonna carry the Vice Presidential Debate right here on ABC. We're just moments away. Don't go anywhere. It's a big night in this race for president.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020241014eka100002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ASK THE EXPERTS QUESTIONS FROM SURVIVORS, THRIVERS & CAREGIVERS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STRIKE, DOCKWORKERS, VELOZ, DAGGETT, RHEE, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, OWENBY, NORWOOD, CRISWELL, RAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESPERATE SEARCH & RESCUE MISSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HELENE'S DEATH TOLL RISES WITH HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRACK, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TROPICAL THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE STORM FORMING THAT COULD HIT THE GULF COAST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BIDEN, HARRIS, HURRICANE HELENE, ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES RESPOND TO HURRICANE DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS BRIEFED BY FEMA OFFICIALS; PRESIDENT BIDEN BLASTS TRUMP COMMENTS\par \par \uc2 WALZ, VANCE, DEBATE, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE VICE PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO EXPECT WHEN WALZ & VANCE FACE OFF TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 HEZBOLLAH, ISRAEL, BIDEN, NETANYAHU, HAGARI\par \par \uc2 ROSE, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BASEBALL GREAT PETE ROSE PASSES AWAY AT 83\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MLB'S ALL-TIME HITS LEADER WAS BANNED FOR GAMBLING ON THE GAME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, JOHNSON, BONE CANCER, SHIP\par \par \uc2 KORS, MERGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUXURY MERGER LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FTC\uc2  FIGHTS PLAN TO BRING UPSCALE FASHION BRANDS TOGETHER\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SALES FORECAST, SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY SHOPPING SALES FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHEN & WHERE TO SCORE THE BIGGEST DEALS\par \par \uc2 KNUDSEN, BREAST CANCER, STATISTICS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS ON BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY RELEASES NEW STATISTICS REPORT\par \par \uc2 JONES, MARQUEZ, WEDDING, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWER IN PINK, 8:14\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DOCKWORKERS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 CARTER, 100, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, LIONS, SEAHAWKS, DOLPHINS, TITANS\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, STONE, TEROU, STOKELY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, KNUDSEN, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER AWARENESS MONTH\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, COMEN, JAMES, KNUDSEN\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have a beautiful audience with us. We are back now with Power in Pink. This is the first day of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. We have assembled a breast cancer panel with some of the top doctors in the field. They are Dr. Elizabeth Comen, give it up for her. She's a medical oncologist at NYU Langone Health. Dr. Monique James. The assistant clinical director of psychiatry at \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2 . And back with us is Dr. \uc2 Karen Knudsen\uc2 , the CEO of the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  and ACS CAN.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we also have a group of thrivers and caregivers here in the studio with us, who have questions for our doctors. We'll get to those questions in a second. But we're gonna give you a hand first.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. Thank you, thank you, thank you all for being here. We're happy you have questions for the doctors. The first question is from Amy, it's for Dr. Knudsen. So go ahead, Amy.\par \par \uc2 AMY (CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dr. Knudsen. I was diagnosed with breast cancer in September 2020. I went through chemotherapy, immunotherapy, hormone shots, and I will be taking pills for the next seven years. I just was recently speaking with my friend who stopped taking all cancer medications because she could not afford it. What do we do in these situations?\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, first of all, Amy, thank you so much for the question. You are why we are here. Unfortunately, what your friend is experiencing is all too common. We have a survivors tsunami coming in the United States, thankfully, as the mortality rate's gone down for cancer. More people are surviving cancer but then suffering from some of these knock on effects including what we call financial toxicity.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE "SURVIVOR TSUNAMI"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE PEOPLE DEALING WITH THE AFTERMATH OF SURVIVING CANCER\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 We know now that that's a real toxicity and that someone who is experiencing a hardship to pay for their cancer medicines has a worse outcome. They will often have medications or skip medications in order to make it happen. So it's really important to know that there are resources to ask early on with your oncology team about access to those resources with the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  and beyond to help offset some of those costs. Because there are things that actually can be done to offset financial toxicity. So important. I'm glad you asked.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There are so many resources, so many resources that are out there. Charmaine, where are you? Okay, You have a question for Dr. James?\par \par \uc2 CHARMAINE (CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Yes. My question is for Dr. James. My name is Charmaine. I am a seven year triple negative breast cancer survivor as of October 9th. My question is, through my journey of a double mastectomy and reconstruction, chemo, of the things that needed to be done, between friends and family, that you just kind of thought would be there when it mattered and they weren't. How do you manage that, especially when you're on the other side and you still have to walk this walk?\par \par \uc2 DR MONIQUE JAMES (ASST CLINICAL DIR OF PSYCH MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 Charmaine, thank you so much for asking that question. And thank you all so much for being here. First of all, this is so common. Unfortunately, it's very common. It's so common, that we have a term for it. We usually call it cancer ghosting. You might have heard that before. It can happen along the cancer continuum, all ages, all stages, friends, family, loved ones. So the first thing is that it's common. I usually tell my patients also, as much as you can, not to take it personally. So many people bring their own past experiences to you, whether it's their own experience, loved one's experiences.\par \par \uc2 DR MONIQUE JAMES (ASST CLINICAL DIR OF PSYCH MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 A lot of it is about their own fear, or their own love for you. If you feel comfortable, though, there are different kinds of supports. Emotional support, physical support, material support, transportation. So if you feel comfortable talking to that person about perhaps we can have a different kind of relationship right now. Perhaps we can go see a movie instead of having this talk over coffee. However, you know, to be prepared for if that person does not want to have this relationship for any reason. And so it's also okay to be sad. It's also okay to grieve that relationship that's different and that has changed right now. But however, right, to be open to new people coming into your life. New people fitting that emotional support relationship that you may have missed.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good answer. Where's Audrey? You have a question for Dr. Comen.\par \par \uc2 AUDREY (LOST MOTHER TO CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yes, hi, nice to meet you. My mom, Deb Hare (PH) was diagnosed with breast cancer in 2018 and unfortunately passed in 2022. Breast cancer did not run in my family before. So what kind of genetic testing should I do as a young woman? And what does that entail?\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (ASSOC PROF OF HEALTH NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for your question. And I am so sorry for the loss of your mother. I know that that is all too common and that grief is extremely traumatic. So thank you for the bravery of asking your question as well. So, many patients, most patients, in fact, diagnosed with breast cancer don't have a family history. But about 5 to 10% of breast cancers have a hereditary cause, which means that either from your mother's side or your father's side. A lot of times people forget that you can inherit 50% of your genes from your father as well.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (ASSOC PROF OF HEALTH NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 You can inherent a potential risk for breast cancer and maybe other cancers. So the first thing I would say is to the best of your ability, try to assemble an understanding of what cancers, not just breast cancer, but any type of cancer on your mother's side, including cousins as well, and your father's side. Bring that information to your doctor and ask to speak with a genetic counselor about what genetic testing may be appropriate for you, because there's lots of different types of genetic counseling and genetic testing out there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you for that. Next we have Amanda with a question for Dr. James.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA (CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Hello. I was diagnosed with breast cancer in December. I just finished chemo about eight weeks ago. I was diagnosed at 38, and I have two very small children. What's the best way to - explain cancer to kids who know something is wrong or off, but don't really, can't really comprehend cancer?\par \par \uc2 DR MONIQUE JAMES (ASST CLINICAL DIR OF PSYCH MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 Amanda, thank you for your question. It's a great question. And eight weeks out. That's amazing. So, you know, kids, talking to kids about cancer. So first of all, kids are truth tellers and they also say the darndest things. And so I think to be prepared, number one, that they will have questions. They are also observant. They're also resilient, but also very observant. So there's a few tips that we usually say. First is that if this child is in your life, to kind of prepare them first. So if you can say that these things are coming up, I'm going to be going to the doctor more often. I'm going to have special treatment.\par \par \uc2 DR MONIQUE JAMES (ASST CLINICAL DIR OF PSYCH MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 There might be parts of my body that change. Right? You don't have to use the word cancer if you don't want to. But you can as well. Some of it is about giving language. And of course, age appropriate. Then we also want kids always to hear this news in a familiar way. So if your family has always said news around dinner, then do that. If it's on a walk, if it's in play, then do that. This is not the time to kind of change things up so drastically. But the biggest thing is that you don't want them to have questions alone, right? You want them to have a place to go where they can get answers and to feel comfortable. But to giving them a warning sign that something might change with my hair and different parts of my body, would be the best.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. These have been all great responses and very, very helpful. And I'm glad that we're recognizing the beautiful thrivers that we have here. Dr. Knudsen, can I also ask us to talk about the caregivers that are with us, and those at home, that they need support as well.\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 No question. The science on this is very clear. That as the caregiver goes, the patients go. So patients who have a caregiver and who have a caregiver who's in good health, do better. So you are incredibly important caregivers as part of this cancer journey. It's important to connect with each other, as important as it is to connect patients. We know that connecting caregivers is really critical. We do offer at the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  some resources to that. I encourage you to go to cancer.org to take advantage of that, so we can help connect you to another caregiver who's walked in your shoes and is helping someone who has the cancer that you are assisting. Really important. And thank you for what you do.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have time for one more question. Let's get that from Jeanelle (PH) for Dr. Comen.\par \par \uc2 JEANELLE (CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Hello, Doc. So I'm 11 months in remission from triple negative breast cancer. And like many women, I had dense breasts. What exactly is the next step when a mammogram is inconclusive? Is it an ultrasound or an X-ray?\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (ASSOC PROF OF HEALTH NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 So, great question. This has really been in the news lately. As of September 10th, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has mandated that women be informed whether they have dense breasts or not. So what are dense breasts? They're very common. Almost half of all women over age of 40 have dense breasts. It's not something you can see or feel, or that a doctor could feel, but something a radiologist detects on a mammogram. If you have dense breasts, such as yourself, it's important to talk to your doctor about what additional screening may be necessary to pick up a breast cancer and pick up a breast cancer early.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (ASSOC PROF OF HEALTH NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 So that may be a breast ultrasound. In some instances it's a breast MRI. But it's a very individual discussion with your doctor about what are the next steps that are appropriate for you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wonderful. Wonderful. Doctors, thank you so very much. Thank you for being here and for being so reassuring, and for the wonderful, just your presence. Your presence, and just looking at them and just wanting to answer those questions. And to you all as well, you're absolutely beautiful. You're thriving. And we appreciate your bravery, your strength. (Inaudible) But wait, there's more. (inaudible) Everybody here in our studio, did you see Tory Johnson and those items she had in the Deals & Steals? Well, you're going to be going home with some of those products.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And coming up next, dietician \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2  is here with ways to add foods to your diet that can help reduce cancer risk as well. Bless you all. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241010eka10003i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING NEWS ON BREAST CANCER AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY RELEASES NEW STATISTICS REPORT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STRIKE, DOCKWORKERS, VELOZ, DAGGETT, RHEE, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, OWENBY, NORWOOD, CRISWELL, RAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESPERATE SEARCH & RESCUE MISSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HELENE'S DEATH TOLL RISES WITH HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRACK, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TROPICAL THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE STORM FORMING THAT COULD HIT THE GULF COAST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BIDEN, HARRIS, HURRICANE HELENE, ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES RESPOND TO HURRICANE DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS BRIEFED BY FEMA OFFICIALS; PRESIDENT BIDEN BLASTS TRUMP COMMENTS\par \par \uc2 WALZ, VANCE, DEBATE, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE VICE PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO EXPECT WHEN WALZ & VANCE FACE OFF TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 HEZBOLLAH, ISRAEL, BIDEN, NETANYAHU, HAGARI\par \par \uc2 ROSE, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BASEBALL GREAT PETE ROSE PASSES AWAY AT 83\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MLB'S ALL-TIME HITS LEADER WAS BANNED FOR GAMBLING ON THE GAME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, JOHNSON, BONE CANCER, SHIP\par \par \uc2 KORS, MERGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUXURY MERGER LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FTC\uc2  FIGHTS PLAN TO BRING UPSCALE FASHION BRANDS TOGETHER\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SALES FORECAST, SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY SHOPPING SALES FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHEN & WHERE TO SCORE THE BIGGEST DEALS\par \par \uc2 KNUDSEN, BREAST CANCER, STATISTICS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS ON BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY RELEASES NEW STATISTICS REPORT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now on "GMA" with the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  releasing its 2024 breast cancer statistics this morning. And we have it first on "GMA." Dr. \uc2 Karen Knudsen\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  and ACS-CAN, is joining us exclusively to talk about these exclusively to talk about these findings. Thank you so much for this opportunity. And the report, looking at it, it's a mixed bag. So, let's start with the good news first. Mortality rates are down.\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 They sure are. So, this is part of our commitment to patients and families across the country to report on progress against cancer. So, when we look at progress against breast cancer, we are delighted to report a 44% reduction in overall breast cancer mortality since 1989, which was the high-water mark for breast cancer deaths in this country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER MORTALITY RATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOWN 44% OVERALL SINCE 1989\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, that is the good news. There is some troubling statistics as well that it's targeting a certain group. Can you explain more about that?\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Well, that's right. So, when we look at incidents overall, so people being diagnosed with breast cancer, we see overall for all women about a 1% year over year rise in diagnosis. But when we unpack that and look at who is being disproportionately affected, it's women of a younger age, younger than 50.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSES\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 That increase in incidence is a little higher than the average, about 1.4% year over year. But a really unsuspected or unexpected trend in women of Asian descent or Asian American and Pacific islanders seeing a 2.5% rise in breast cancer incidence. So, we're watching that to try to understand what is driving these differences in different population.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT ON BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACS: MORE YOUNGER WOMEN & AAPI WOMEN BEING DIAGNOSED\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that is very alarming. We here on "GMA," there are more reports of younger generation. Are there any reasons behind this? Any early findings why that's the case?\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 So, there's some things we know now. So, for example, we know that there is an increasing incidence of obesity in the United States. And breast cancer risk includes obesity as a risk factor. We also know that alcohol use is associated with breast cancer risk and incidence. But there must be something more because it's not affecting populations equally. We see this rise in Gen X and millennials with breast cancer at a younger age compared to baby boomers. So, something more must be at play. And so, cancer research investment will be really important for us to understand what that is.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you'll continue that research. When I was diagnosed with triple negative breast cancer in 2007, I was told, as a black woman, that I was less likely to be diagnosed but more likely to die if I was diagnosed. Is that still hold true for black women? Are they disproportionately affected?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACS: BLACK WOMEN IMPACTED DISPROPORTIONATELY BY THE DISEASE\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, that's true. So, actually, if we go back to the 1970s before we knew as much about breast cancer as we know now, mortality rates for black and white women were approximately equal. Now, fast forward to today, where black women have a reduced incidence or lower incidence of breast cancer, but has 38% increase mortality rate compared to white counterparts.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER IN BLACK WOMEN\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 So, there's much to unpack. Part of that is triple negative breast cancer, which is more common in black women. But there must be more. Everything from access to early detection, which we know saves lives.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 All the way through access to the latest cancer research breakthroughs.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm being told to wrap, but I have to ask you about men, because they don't get enough coverage about this. And the numbers there are troubling as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROUBLING NEW NUMBERS ABOUT MALE BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 DR \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2  (CEO OF AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Please do. There is no screening plan for men. And male breast cancer is rare, but very serious. About 3,000 men this year in the United States will be diagnosed with breast cancer, and more than 500 will die of that disease. So, if you feel something, do something.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Important that we share that as well. Dr. Knudsen, thank you very much. I know you're going to stick around because we have much more to talk about. We have questions from thrivers. We have caregivers here in our studio as well. And that's coming up in our next hour, Michael. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are looking forward to that, Robin. Thank you. And coming up next, how far one dad went to make to it his daughter's wedding.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7:48, PLAY OF THE DAY!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You don't want to miss this story. It's our "Play of the Day."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241010eka100036\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEALS & STEALS POWER IN PINK\b0\par\par\uc2 1,374 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STRIKE, DOCKWORKERS, VELOZ, DAGGETT, RHEE, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, OWENBY, NORWOOD, CRISWELL, RAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESPERATE SEARCH & RESCUE MISSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HELENE'S DEATH TOLL RISES WITH HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRACK, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TROPICAL THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE STORM FORMING THAT COULD HIT THE GULF COAST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BIDEN, HARRIS, HURRICANE HELENE, ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES RESPOND TO HURRICANE DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS BRIEFED BY FEMA OFFICIALS; PRESIDENT BIDEN BLASTS TRUMP COMMENTS\par \par \uc2 WALZ, VANCE, DEBATE, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE VICE PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO EXPECT WHEN WALZ & VANCE FACE OFF TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 HEZBOLLAH, ISRAEL, BIDEN, NETANYAHU, HAGARI\par \par \uc2 ROSE, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BASEBALL GREAT PETE ROSE PASSES AWAY AT 83\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MLB'S ALL-TIME HITS LEADER WAS BANNED FOR GAMBLING ON THE GAME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, JOHNSON, BONE CANCER, SHIP\par \par \uc2 KORS, MERGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUXURY MERGER LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FTC\uc2  FIGHTS PLAN TO BRING UPSCALE FASHION BRANDS TOGETHER\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SALES FORECAST, SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY SHOPPING SALES FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHEN & WHERE TO SCORE THE BIGGEST DEALS\par \par \uc2 KNUDSEN, BREAST CANCER, STATISTICS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS ON BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY RELEASES NEW STATISTICS REPORT\par \par \uc2 JONES, MARQUEZ, WEDDING, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWER IN PINK, 8:14\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DOCKWORKERS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 CARTER, 100, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, LIONS, SEAHAWKS, DOLPHINS, TITANS\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, STONE, TEROU, STOKELY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, KNUDSEN, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER AWARENESS MONTH\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's time now for Deals & Steals. This morning, Tory Johnson is here with deals in honor of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And they are all from small businesses. This one is really special. They make inclusive bras and clothing for those going through treatment and for survivors.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. Their motto is one breast, two breast, no breasts, new breasts. All chests deserve support. And that's what AnaOno is really dedicated to. We have an enormous assortment from them of all of their exquisite bras designed specifically for pre-surgery, post-surgery, recovery. They pay such specific attention to the structure, the fabric, every single detail.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What women need at certain times.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ANAONO\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. They also have these really soft, feel that fabric.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I did already. Heavenly.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The lounge pants are just kind of like a recovery essential. You don't feel them. They're not going to bother pain points. The robes are phenomenal. So this is a special company. And everything today, 50% off.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. Thank you, AnaOno. Okay. Some great jewelry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AWE\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Some great jewelry. This is from Awe Inspired, wearable symbols of empowerment, started by a three time cancer survivor/thriver. She's all about giving women symbols that they can wear to offer hope, empowerment. I love the entire goddess collection, we have in rings and necklaces. We also have these really awesome mantra necklaces. I'm wearing one that they sent me that says I'm worthy of abundance. There's one that says inhale the future, exhale the past. We've got - these angel numbers in white topaz that are beautiful.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they really are.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The hat, come on, who doesn't love this hat? "In my goddess era." This is really just about reminding you that, for a lot of us, there's resilience, strength, hope that comes from within. Everything today 50 to 60% off, starts at $19 and free shipping from Awe Inspired.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And really beautiful quality.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These pieces, everyone is staring at them, gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love this company too, this is created by another thriver, and this is deodorant that's really healthy.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. It's all about 24 hour protection with no aluminum, no perfumes, no dyes, no parabins. I was texting with sweet Amber who introduced me to this line. She's like, yep, I still love it, still use it. Big endorsement there. We've got some of their newest products. Obviously we have the deodorant. We also have these brand new deodorant wipes. These are great for when a shower just simply feels too overwhelming. But they're also really great to travel with. Just a portable size. We have their deodorant sprays. But all of the products phenomenal from this company, and everything today 50% off.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FREEDOM\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amazing.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) On to skin care with the same sort of philosophy.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, so if you are focused on sensitive skin or especially mindful of what you are putting on your body, Goldfaden MD is designed to mimic the in-office dermatologist treatments but with good for you ingredients. All, in their case, natural, clean, plant powered products. And then we've got a variety I love some of the best sellers here. They've got this doctor scrub that's a really great everyday exfoliator. Perhaps one of my favorites is called the liquid face lift.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOLDFADEN MD\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I tried that. I liked it.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a - tiny little one here, with pretty quick results...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also the bright eyes. (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Bright eyes, yes, bright eyes is fabulous for that under eye area. It's ultralight but advanced.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And like really gives you that ding, that little sparkle...\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, little ding. And then also we've got great products for hydration. Everything from Goldfaden MD today slashed in half, and free shipping from them as well.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And 50 to 75% off on that, so check that out.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Big savings.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then these are vegan?\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So soft, yes. 100% cruelty free. Vegan faux fur. When somebody is going through a difficult time...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love a blanket. It's a great gift.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ... a cozy blanket is my go-to gift. These are ultra-soft. They also have this really unique elastic stretch in them that just envelopes you in a comfortable hug...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. I just want to climb into it.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So they're super comfortable, and one of the best things about this is zero shedding which is not always the case with faux fur. Zero shedding on this. We've got seven colors, three different sizes. They're machine washable. 50% off and free shipping from Lola blanket.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOLA\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's go. And then we're bringing it home with the beauty.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) With Blissy, we love Blissy. 100% silk, mulberry silk. Great, it's very gentle on skin and hair at a time when you especially want to be gentle on skin and hair...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Protect that hair, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...which can be every day, but especially when you're going through a difficult time, that matters a lot. We've got a huge variety of all of their beautiful colors. These also have a zipper on them so they stay in place and they're machine washable, and they're 50% off, and they have free shipping.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, that is fantastic. These are all really thoughtful, great gifts. Sourced by Tory, partnering with these companies. You can get them by heading to our website. Or check out that QR code. Coming up, we have an expert panel answering questions about breast cancer prevention, screening and treatment. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241010eka10003g\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2 'S CANCER FIGHTING KITCHEN RECIPES THAT MAY HELP REDUCE CANCER RISK\b0\par\par\uc2 1,157 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STRIKE, DOCKWORKERS, VELOZ, DAGGETT, RHEE, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, OWENBY, NORWOOD, CRISWELL, RAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESPERATE SEARCH & RESCUE MISSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HELENE'S DEATH TOLL RISES WITH HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRACK, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TROPICAL THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE STORM FORMING THAT COULD HIT THE GULF COAST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BIDEN, HARRIS, HURRICANE HELENE, ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES RESPOND TO HURRICANE DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS BRIEFED BY FEMA OFFICIALS; PRESIDENT BIDEN BLASTS TRUMP COMMENTS\par \par \uc2 WALZ, VANCE, DEBATE, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE VICE PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO EXPECT WHEN WALZ & VANCE FACE OFF TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 HEZBOLLAH, ISRAEL, BIDEN, NETANYAHU, HAGARI\par \par \uc2 ROSE, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BASEBALL GREAT PETE ROSE PASSES AWAY AT 83\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MLB'S ALL-TIME HITS LEADER WAS BANNED FOR GAMBLING ON THE GAME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II, JOHNSON, BONE CANCER, SHIP\par \par \uc2 KORS, MERGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUXURY MERGER LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FTC\uc2  FIGHTS PLAN TO BRING UPSCALE FASHION BRANDS TOGETHER\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SALES FORECAST, SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY SHOPPING SALES FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHEN & WHERE TO SCORE THE BIGGEST DEALS\par \par \uc2 KNUDSEN, BREAST CANCER, STATISTICS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS ON BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY RELEASES NEW STATISTICS REPORT\par \par \uc2 JONES, MARQUEZ, WEDDING, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWER IN PINK, 8:14\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DOCKWORKERS, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 CARTER, 100, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, LIONS, SEAHAWKS, DOLPHINS, TITANS\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, STONE, TEROU, STOKELY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, KNUDSEN, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER AWARENESS MONTH\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, COMEN, JAMES, KNUDSEN\par \par \uc2 BELLER, "SPICE RACK", CANCER, RISK, FIBER\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now with our Power in Pink series and recipes that may help reduce cancer risk. Dietitian and our good friend \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2  is the author of "Spice Rack." She joins us now with swap outs you can make on the plate that could have a powerful impact. You've never done this in front of a live audience. You got this, you got this.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 No. Ooh, it's been a few, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, as always. We should tell people, now, you're not saying you can prevent or cure cancer, but there are some foods that can reduce the risk.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so I'm glad you mentioned that. It's very important to note that no single food or diet can prevent or cure cancer, but what we eat over time collectively can make a difference, along with physical activity, so yes, we're starting with our fiber.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know. She loves to talk about fiber every time that she's here. Tell us how can we, in breakfast, how can we get more fiber?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so it's very important. My goal is to recommend 30 to 35 grams of fiber. This is really important because it supports our hormonal balance, helps us with our weight management, immunity, gut health, so much. And I recommend starting with about ten grams in the morning. That's important because it gives you a good start to the day. And I have some example here. So, raspberries, a cup, eight grams of fiber. Edamame for breakfast, protein and fiber, nine grams. It's great. Avocado, ten grams of fiber. And I have some breakfast examples for you. We've got an avocado chia toast here. We upgraded it. Sprouted, flourless bread, about 11 grams of fiber.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a lot.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 And then I've got a pear bliss parfait here, about 13 and a half grams of fiber. Pears - do deserve a better PR, because it's got six grams of fiber, which is incredible. So simple and easy.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I grabbed a pear on the way in this morning, because I knew that you were saying that, talking about that. Okay, with lunch. You're talking about there are other ways we can sneak some things in. And dressing. We are always told to avoid dressing, but you are saying something different.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 No, dressing is a great unexpected way to upgrade the nutritional value of your meal. So what I have here is a dressing that includes ground flax seed. Number one source of lignans, which are protective, and then I added the Beller Nutrition Power Spice Blend, the Vegitude Power.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, I have this, yes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWER SPICED DRESSING\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 It's absolutely great. Garlic, onion, turmeric, black pepper and parsley. An incredible, simple way to really elevate, sneak in antioxidants, anti-inflammatory properties, good stuff, and it's delicious. And it comes with a book, too.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Parsley. What's up with parsley?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARSLEY POWER TONIC\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 This is a parsley tonic. I love this, parsley is rich in (inaudible) which is very protective. Also in this tonic I added some fennel seeds, some Ceylon cinnamon. This is great for digestion. I personally drink this to help me de-bloat. I love it. You can have it chilled or warm. Excellent.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, so can we get dessert after all of this? I mean, come on.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 Of course. (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we've worked really hard.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 We all need dessert. So we need to treat ourselves, so the powerhouse ingredient here is sesame seeds. I have got tahini paste. It is the second leading source of lignans after flax seed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SESAME COOKIES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What, the tahini?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (DIETICIAN NUTRITIONIST/AUTHOR OF "SPICE RACK")\par \par \uc2 The tahini is basically ground up sesame seeds. And it's great. I added it to this cookie. We also have almond - sorry, almond flour, which is very nourishing. Ceylon cinnamon, or you can use the Beller Nutrition (inaudible) spice or you can add a little chocolate chips, some dark chocolate chips. Yeah. Good stuff. You know, we want to enjoy, but we want to keep it simple. That's the key. That's what I really focus on in my master class and also in my book. And then the power spice blend. It's all to make things simple and easy.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know how passionate you are about making a difference. And you do, in so many lives. Thank you, Rachel. Appreciate that. And you can get these recipes on - goodmorningamerica.com. Let's go now to Ginger. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241010eka10003j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RACHEL PLATTEN LIVE PERFORMANCE\b0\par\par\uc2 347 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STRIKE, DOCKWORKERS, VELOZ, DAGGETT, RHEE, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE HELENE, OWENBY, NORWOOD, CRISWELL, RAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESPERATE SEARCH & RESCUE MISSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HELENE'S DEATH TOLL RISES WITH HUNDREDS STILL UNACCOUNTED FOR\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRACK, HURRICANE HELENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TROPICAL THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE STORM FORMING THAT COULD HIT THE GULF COAST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BIDEN, HARRIS, HURRICANE HELENE, ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES RES