of them, with snipers. And I got involved with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was doing the killing. That's the fighting force within Afghanistan. They don't bother doing that because, you know, they deal with the wrong people all the time. But I got involved. And Abdul, who's the head of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , he is still the head of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And I told Abdul don't do it anymore, you do it anymore you're gonna have problems. And he said why do you send me a picture of my house? I said you're gonna have to figure that out, Abdul. And for 18 months we had nobody killed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We did have an agreement negotiated by \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . It was a very good agreement. The reason it was good, it was, we were getting out. We would've been out faster than them but we wouldn't have lost the soldiers. We wouldn't have left many Americans behind and we wouldn't have left, we wouldn't have left $85 billion worth of brand-new, beautiful military equipment behind. And just to finish, they blew it. The agreement said you have to do this, this, this, this, this, and they didn't do it. They didn't do it. The agreement was terminated by us because they didn't do what they were supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I want to move on.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And these people did the worst withdrawal and in my opinion the most embarrassing moment in the history of our country. And by the way, that's why Russia attacked Ukraine. Because they saw how incompetent she and her boss are.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, thank you. I want to move on now to race and politics in this country. Mr. President, you recently said of Vice President Harris, quote, "I didn't know she was Black until a number of years ago when she happened to turn Black, and now she wants to be known as Black." I want to ask a bigger picture question here tonight. Why do you believe it's appropriate to weigh in on the racial identity of your opponent?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I don't and I don't care. I don't care what she is. I don't care. You make a big deal out of something. I couldn't care less. Whatever she wants to be is okay with me.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But those were your words. So I'm asking...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I don't know, I don't know. I mean, all I can say is I read where she was not Black, that she put out, and I'll say that, and then I read that she was Black and that's okay. Either one was okay with me. That's up to her. That's up to her.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice, Vice President Harris, your thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I think it's, I mean, honestly, I think it's a, a tragedy that we have someone who wants to be president who has consistently over the course of his career attempted to use race to divide the American people. You know, I do believe that the vast majority of us know that we have so much more in common than what separates us. And we don't want this kind of approach that is just constantly trying to divide us. And especially by race. And let's remember how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  started. He was a - land, he'd owned land, he owned buildings, and he, he was investigated because he refused to rent property to Black families.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Let's remember, this is the same individual who took out full-page ad in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " calling for the execution of five young Black and Latino boys who were innocent, the Central Park Five, took out a full-page ad calling for their execution. This is the same individual who spread birther lies about the first Black President of the United States. And I think the American people want better than that, want better than this, want someone who understands as do, I travel our country, we see in each other a friend.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We see in each other a neighbor. We don't want a leader who is constantly trying to have Americans point their fingers at each other. I meet with people all the time who tell me can we please just have discourse about how we're going to invest in the aspirations and the ambitions and the dreams of the American people? Knowing that regardless of people's color or the language their grandmother speaks we all have the same dreams and aspirations and want a president who invests in those, not in hate and division.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, thank you. Linsey?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, this is now your third time...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 This is the most divisive presidency in the history of our country. There's never been anything like it. They're destroying our country. And they come up with things like what she just said, going back many, many years when a lot of people including Mayor Bloomberg agreed with me on the Central Park Five. They admitted they said, they pled guilty. And I said, well, if they pled guilty they badly hurt a person, killed a person ultimately. And if they pled guilty, then they pled we're not guilty. But this is a person that has to stretch back years, 40, 50 years ago because there's nothing now.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I built one of the greatest economies in the history of the world and I'm gonna build it again. It's gonna be bigger, better and stronger. But they're destroying our economy. They have no idea what a good economy is. Their oil policies, every single policy, and remember this. She is Biden. She's trying to get away from Biden. I don't know the gentleman, she says. She is Biden. The worst inflation we've ever had, a horrible economy because inflation has made it so bad and she can't get away with that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mr. President, thank you. Your time is up. Linsey?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I want to respond to that, though. I want to just respond briefly. Clearly, I am not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I am certainly not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And what I do offer is a new generation of leadership for our country, one who believes in what is possible, one who brings a sense of optimism about what we can do instead of always disparaging the American people. I believe in what we can do to strengthen our small businesses, which is why I have a plan. Let's talk about our plans and, and let's compare the plans. I have a plan to give startup businesses $50,000 tax deduction, to pursue their ambitions, their innovation, their ideas, their hard work.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I have a plan, $6,000 for young families for the first year of your child's life, to help you in that most critical stage of your child's development. I have a plan that is about allowing people to be able to pursue what has been fleeting in terms of the American dream by offering a help with down payment of $25,000, down payment assistance for first-time homebuyers. That's the kind of conversation I believe, David, that people really want tonight as opposed to a conversation that is constantly about belittling and name calling. Let's turn the page and move forward.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, thank you. Let's turn to policy. Let's turn back to policy.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, we have to move on. President Trump...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) she has a plan to destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's turn to policy, please?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 She has a plan to defund the police. She has a - plan to confiscate everybody's gun.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, we do have to move on to other issues.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 She has a plan to not allow fracking in Pennsylvania or anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 That's what her plan is until just recently.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I, I just need to respond because...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Linsey, go ahead (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, President Trump...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 No, the former president has said something twice that I need to respond to.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, I'm sorry. No, I'm, I'm sorry, we're gonna need to move on, Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I just need to respond one time to what he has said multiple times.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, this is now your third time running for president.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You have long vowed to repeal and replace the Affordable Care Act, as known as Obamacare. You have failed to accomplish that. You now say you're gonna keep Obamacare, quote, "unless we can do something much better." Last month you said, quote, "We're working on it." So tonight, nine years after you first started running, do you have a plan and can you tell us what it is?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Obamacare was lousy healthcare. Always was. It's not very good today. And what I said, that if we come up with something, and we are working on things, we're gonna do it and we're gonna replace it. But remember this, I inherited Obamacare because Democrats wouldn't change it. They wouldn't vote for it. They were unanimous. They wouldn't vote to change it. If they would've done that, we would've had a much better plan than Obamacare. But the Democrats came up, they wouldn't vote for it. I had a choice to make when I was president, do I save it and make it as good as it can be, never gonna be great, do I let it rot? And I felt I had an obligation, even though politically it would've been good to just let it rot and let it go away.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I decided and I told my people, the top people, and they're very good people, I have a lot of good people in this, that administration. We read about the bad ones. We had some real bad ones too. And so do they. They have really bad ones. The difference is they don't get rid of them. But let me just explain. I had a choice to make do I save it and make it as good as it can be or do I let it rot? And I saved it. I did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But it's still never going to be great and it's too expensive for people. And what we will do is we're looking at different plans. If we can come up with a plan that's gonna cost our people, our population less money and be better health care than Obamacare, then I would absolutely do it. But until then I'd run it as good as it can be run.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, just a yes or no, you still do not have a plan?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I have concepts of a plan. I'm not president right now. But if we come up with something, I would only change it if we come up with something better and less expensive. And there are concepts and options we, we have to do that, and you'll be hearing about it in the not too distant future.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, in 2017 you supported Bernie Sanders' proposal to do away with private insurance and create a government-run healthcare system. Two years later you proposed a plan that included a private insurance option. What is your plan today?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, I absolutely support and over the last four years as vice president private health care options. But what we need to do is maintain and grow the Affordable Care Act. But I, I'll, I'll get to that, Linsey. I just need to respond to a previous point that the former president has made. I've made very clear my position on fracking. And then this business about taking everyone's guns away. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and I are both gun owners. We're not taking anybody's guns away, so stop with the continuous lying about this stuff.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 As it relates to the Affordable Care Act, understand, let's just look at the history to know where people stand. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, 60 times he tried to get rid of the Affordable Care Act, 60 times. I was a senator at the time. When, I will never forget the early morning hours when it was up for a vote in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and the late great \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , who you have disparaged as being - you don't like him, you said at the time, because he got caught. He was an American hero. The late great John McCain, I will never forget that night, walked onto the Senate floor and said no, you don't. No, you don't. No, you don't get rid of the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 You have no plan. And what the Affordable Care Act has done is eliminate the ability of insurance companies to deny people with preexisting conditions. I don't have to he will it the people watching tonight, you remember what that was like? Remember when an insurance company could deny if a child had asthma, if someone was a breast cancer survivor, if a, if a grandparent had diabetes? And thankfully, as I've been vice president and we over the last four years have strengthened the Affordable Care Act, we have allowed for the first time Medicare to negotiate drug prices on behalf of you the American people.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump said he was gonna allow Medicare to negotiate - drug prices. He never did. We did. And now we have capped the cost of insulin at $35 a month. Since I've been vice president we have capped the cost of prescription medication for seniors at $2,000 a year. And when I am president we will do that for all people understanding that the value I bring to this is that access to health care should be a right and not just a privilege of those who can afford it. And the plan has to be to strengthen the Affordable Care Act, not get rid of it. Past is prologue...\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...in terms of where Donald Trump stands on that.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to move to an issue that's important for a lot of...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 She made a mistake. Number one, John McCain fought Obamacare for ten years. But it wasn't only him. It was all of the Democrats that kept it going. And you know what? We could do much better than Obamacare, much less money. But she won't improve private insurance for people, private medical insurance. That's another thing she doesn't want to give.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 People are paying privately for insurance that have worked hard and made money and they want to have private. She wants everybody to be on government insurance where you wait six months for an operation that you need immediately.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, thank you. We have another issue that we'd like to get to that's important for a number of Americans, in particular younger voters, and that's climate change. President Trump, with regard to the environment, you say that we have to have clean air and clean water. Vice President Harris, you call climate change an existential threat. The question to you both tonight is what would you do to fight climate change? And Vice President Harris, we'll start with you. One minute for you each.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, the former president had said that climate change is a hoax. And what we know is that it is very real. You ask anyone who lives in a state who has experienced these extreme weather occurrences, who now is either being denied home insurance or is being jacked up. You ask anybody who has been the victim of what that means in terms of losing their home, having nowhere to go. We know that we can actually deal with this issue. The young people of America care deeply about this issue. And I am proud that as vice president over the last four years, we have invested trillion dollars in a clean energy economy while we have also increased domestic gas production to historic levels.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We have created over 800,000 new manufacturing jobs while I have been vice president. We have invested in clean energy to the point that we are opening up factories around the world. Donald Trump said he was gonna create manufacturing jobs. He lost manufacturing jobs. And I'm also proud to have the endorsement of the United Auto Workers and Shawn Fain, who also know that part of building a clean energy economy includes investing in American made products, American automobiles. It includes growing what we can do around American manufacturing and opening up auto plants, not closing them like happened under Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 That didn't happen under Donald Trump. Let me just tell you, they lost 10,000 manufacturing jobs this last month. It's going, they're all leaving. They're building big auto plants in Mexico, in many cases, owned by China. They're building these massive plants, and they think they're gonna sell their cars into the United States because of these people. What they have given to China is unbelievable. But we're not gonna let that. We'll put tariffs on those cars so they can't come into our country. Because they will kill the United Auto Workers and any autoworker, whether it's in Detroit or South Carolina or any other place.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 What they've done to business and manufacturing in this country is horrible. We have nothing because they, they refuse you know, Biden doesn't go after people because, supposedly, China paid him millions of dollars. He's afraid to do it. Between him and his son, they get all this money from Ukraine. They get all this money from all of these different countries, and then you wonder why is he so loyal to this one, that one, Ukraine, China? Why is he? Why did he get three and a half million dollars from the Mayor of Moscow's wife? Why did he get, why did she pay him three and a half million dollars? This is a crooked administration, and they're selling our country down the tubes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back with closing statements from both of our candidates. It's an historic night. This ABC News Presidential Debate from Philadelphia, back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 The ABC News Presidential Debate will continue after this.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from the National Constitution Center in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, here again David Muir and Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back tonight. The time has come for closing statements. And Vice President Harris, we begin with you.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 So, I think you've heard tonight two very different visions for our country, one that is focused on the future and the other that is focused on the past and an attempt to take us backward. But we're not going back. And I do believe that the American people know we all have so much more in common than what separates us and we can chart a new way forward. And a vision of that includes having a plan, understanding the aspirations, the dreams, the hopes, the ambition of the American people, which is why I intend to create an opportunity economy, investing in small businesses, in new families, in what we can do around protecting seniors, what we can do that is about giving hardworking folks a break in bringing down the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I believe in what we can do together that is about sustaining America's standing in the world and ensuring we have the respect that we so rightly deserve including respecting our military and ensuring we have the most lethal fighting force in the world. I will be a president that will protect our fundamental rights and freedoms including the right of a woman to make decisions about her own body and not have her government tell her what to do. I'll tell you, I started my career as a prosecutor. I was a DA. I was an attorney general, a United States Senator, and now vice president. I've only had one client, the People.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And I'll tell you, as a prosecutor, I never asked a victim or a witness are you a Republican or a Democrat. The only thing I ever asked them, are you okay? And that's the kind of president we need right now, someone who cares about you and is not putting themselves first. I intend to be a president for all Americans and focus on what we can do over the next 10 and 20 years to build back up our country by investing right now in you the American people.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, thank you. President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 So, she just started by saying she's gonna do this, she's gonna do that, she's gonna do all these wonderful things. Why hasn't she done it? She's been there for three and a half years. They've had three and a half years to fix the border. They've had three and a half years to create jobs and all the things we talked about. Why hasn't she done it? She should leave right now. Go down to that beautiful White House, go to the Capitol, get everyone together and do the things you want to do. But you haven't done it. And you won't do it. Because you believe in things that the American people don't believe in.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 You believe in things like we're not gonna frack. We're not gonna take fossil fuel. We're not gonna do things that are going to make this country strong, whether you like it or not. Germany tried that and within one year they were back to building normal energy plants. We're not ready for it. We can't sacrifice our country for the sake of bad vision. But I just ask one simple question. Why didn't she do it? We're a failing nation. We're a nation that's in serious decline. We're being laughed at all over the world. All over the world they're laughing. I know the leaders very well. They're coming to see me, they call me. We're laughed at all over the world. They don't understand what happened to us as a nation. We're not a leader.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We don't have any idea what's going on. We have wars going on in the Middle East. We have wars going on with Russia and Ukraine. We're gonna end up in a third World War. And it'll be a war like no other because of nuclear weapons, the power of weaponry. I rebuilt our entire military. She gave a lot of it away to the Taliban. She gave it to Afghanistan. What these people have done to our country, and maybe toughest of all is allowing millions of people to come into our country. Many of them are criminals, and they're destroying our country. The worst president, the worst vice president in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Trump, thank you. And that is our ABC News Presidential Debate from here in Philadelphia at the National Constitution Center. I'm Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And I'm David Muir. Thank you for watching here in the US and all over the world. And from all of us here at ABC News, good night.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020240925ek9a00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 2024 PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE HARRIS VS. TRUMP: THE PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE PRE-TALK\b0\par\par\uc2 8,634 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2024 PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 20024, DEBATE, HARRIS, TRUMP, RULES, VOTERS, ISSUES, POLLS, COTTON, SHAPIRO\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And good evening. I'm Martha Raddatz joining you live from Philadelphia and across the globe with our Powerhouse team coverage, as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are set to face off for the first time in a presidential debate in less than 59 minutes.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I'm \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 . When \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  walk on that debate stage in less than an hour, it will be the very first time they have been face to face, the first time the two have ever met. And the circumstances of tonight's meeting, beyond extraordinary. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is now the Democratic nominee without competing in a single primary. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican nominee, stands indicted for his efforts to overturn the last election and has suggested he won't accept the results of this one if he doesn't win.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Joining us at the anchor desk, chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce, senior congressional correspondent Rachel Scott. Both have been out on the campaign trail crisscrossing the country with the candidates, gearing up for what could be the most consequential moment of this incredibly consequential campaign. Just moments ago, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's motorcade arrived here at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 . There you see it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's plane touching down in Philadelphia less than two hours ago.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: MOMENTS AGO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL CONSTITUTION CENTER, PHILADELPHIA, PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  arriving yesterday after spending days hunkered down in debate prep in Pittsburgh. Both campaigns have been here over the past few hours to get a firsthand look at that debate stage. And Mary, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been preparing for this for weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Intensely preparing. She even went so far as to setup a mock debate stage. That stage there that she is about to walk out on, full with TV lights, doing these mock rehearsals. And she's also been, been turning to, to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . I'm told that they have spoken in the last week. She turned to a lot of Clinton's advisers, those who prepped her for her face-offs with Trump, even one who embodied the role of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in those rehearsals, even dressing like the former president.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Her goal here tonight on that stage is to really reintroduce herself to voters, remind them of her proposals and policies and then to pivot and go on the attack. She wants to remind voters of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stands for, what his administration might look like a second go around. And yes, she is also going to try and get under his skin to needle him a bit. The challenge for her will be to bait him without getting baited herself.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Both candidates have been here looking at that debate stage. But I, I have to say, Rachel, Trump seemed to spend a lot of time looking at all the angles, the camera angles. He is, been preparing as well.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this is a former president who is keenly aware of image. And despite his advisers insisting he doesn't need any debate prep, the former president, I am told, is taking this very seriously. He knows just how consequential this debate, this moment could be. Also with him during that walk around the debate stage, former Congresswoman \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 . You may remember she challenged Harris on the debate stage back in 2019 when...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When she was a Democrat running for president.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...when she was a Democrat running for president, now an Independent who's supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . She has been involved in these debate preparations. The big question of course tonight, which version of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  shows up. Is it the former president that stays on message or is this the former president that's on the attack?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you were in that, on that debate stage. If he shows up on the attack, it's a very small, intimate stage.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Small and intimate. They will be just six feet apart. It is the room where it's all going to be happening. The stage is set for tonight's debate. So, let's take you inside of that room. I went inside the debate hall just right before the candidates arrived. Take a look. When these two candidates enter this debate hall, it will be their very first time coming face to face, and they'll be standing just six feet apart. No live studio audience to cheer them on. No campaign staff allowed in this room either. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the coin toss and decided to give the final word in this debate. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  choosing the podium on the right side of the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The candidates' microphones will only be turned on when it is their turn to speak. They will have two minutes to answer each question, two minutes for a rebuttal, and just 60 seconds for any follow-up questions, which means they'll be paying close attention to this clock right here. The colors will change as the time winds down, turning yellow when there's just 15 seconds left, and red when their time is up. And, of course, sitting right here will be our very own David Muir and Linsey Davis, who will be moderating the first and likely only debate before the November election.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) control.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. So, look, it is one thing to obviously meet your rival, but it's another thing to meet them on stage at the debate for the very first time less than 60 days out from a consequential election. No pressure, right, Mary?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE RULES\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) None at all. But, of course, the, those strict rules that you just mentioned were carefully negotiated by the campaigns. Let's go over them again. No opening statements. Only closing remarks. Each candidate's microphone will only be turned on when it is their turn to speak. Otherwise, they will be muted. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won that coin toss and chose to deliver the last closing statement. Of course, not unusual for them to want to coordinate all of these details.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Martha...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's the details indeed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The details. And, and you've been at events, and you, you've moderated general election debates. You in fact moderated that last amazing debate between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The microphones were not muted, I can tell you that right now.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I always tell people the first debate I did in 2012, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Paul Ryan, I said it was like studying for college exams and then taking them in front of 70 million people. The debate with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was like studying for college exams and then taking it in front of a firing squad. He would attack the moderator. You know, he would say I was unfair. He would say you're giving her more time. You really had to concentrate on keeping them both on message as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has changed the debate stage forever. He's changed the way these debates are done. He had that one debate in 2020 where he was really off the rails. I think he learned his lesson from that one. But you absolutely never know what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is gonna say. And our moderators are so prepared for tonight but there are always, always surprises.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and let's remember, he is no ordinary candidate. This is absolutely no ordinary election. And we mentioned he is under indictment for trying to overturn the last election. He has suggested he would not accept the results if he won here. We're here at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 . I'm sure we will be talking about the issues. But, but for the first time you have a president who is talking about retribution against his enemies if he gets back in, in office. He has actually suggested military tribunals for his enemies should he get back in power. This is a very different kind of election.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It, it is a very different kind of election. And, and just what you said, Jon, that military tribunals, I mean, there are military officers out there who served under Trump before, who are worried they're gonna get pulled back and court martialed. Looking at his political opponents...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Including his own former chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) His own chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff who he basically accused of treason, people like that. It is a different election. And people want to hear about what kind of president he would be again if he wins and of course what kind of commander in chief \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would be.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Mary, what I'm wondering is, will \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  make theta central part of her case? Will he, will she prosecute that case against Trump? Or, will she stick to the issues?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, she certainly has made clear out on the trail that she is not shying away from prosecuting the case against Trump. It's a central part of her biography as she reintroduces herself to voters here. But really her focus tonight is on seizing this moment. Likely the largest audience she has so far to make her case and make clear the split-screen with Trump.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Rachel, January 6th, which we'll probably hear something about here, January 6th is actually something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't want to run away from. He once told me it was one of the proudest moments of, of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's really embraced it. And I think a lot of Republicans, after January 6th, were hoping that he would create some distance between himself and that date. But since then, the former president has called those who stormed the Capitol rioters. He has vowed to pardon them. And I...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Patriots.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM JULY 2024, \uc2 NABJ\uc2  CONVENTION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I, but called them Patriots, excuse me. I had an opportunity to ask the former president about that recently. Take a look. My question is on those who assaulted officers.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Were the people that tried to, excuse me, you have to answer. What's gonna happen...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Would you pardon those people?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, absolutely I would. If they're innocent...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You would pardon those...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 If they're innocent I would pardon them.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They've been convicted.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And by the way, the Supreme Court just under, well, they were convicted by a very, a very tough system. Yeah. So, to be clear there, the former president calls them patriots. He stands by his pledge to pardon those rioters, even those who have been convicted of assaulting police officers that day.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and look, for all that and all that we've discussed and all the chaos of the last several weeks of this campaign, this is essentially a tied race. Te