me, foreign policy and more, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. We're live at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  in Philadelphia ahead of the first presidential debate. Both nominees will define themselves tonight and lay out to voters where they stand on major issues that affect us all. Number one among them, the economy. So, let's bring in our chief economics correspondent Rebecca Jarvis from New York with where things stand after inflation reached a 40-year high in 2022. Rebecca?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Martha, Trump's key advantage coming into tonight's debate is the economy. According to the latest numbers from Pew, 55 percent of voters say they are very or somewhat confident in Trump to make good decisions about economic policy compared with 45 percent who say that about Harris. Harris has to walk a fine line tonight between owning the Biden economy while also differentiating her path forward. Inflation, as you just mentioned, Martha, it's come way down from that peak nine percent to under three percent now. Wages are going up faster than prices today. Unemployment is four point two percent. That is historically very low.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US SPENDING AND INCOME\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is starting to tick higher but it's still been incredibly low throughout this presidency for the most part, but people still feel sticker shock. And that is because prices are up substantially from three years ago. The typical family is now paying about $1,100 more a month today on the same goods and services as they were in January of 2021. Inflation is not just a problem here in the US. It is a worldwide issue since the pandemic. But Americans are looking to our next president to make the American dream achievable and the cost of living affordable. And that is what they want to hear tonight from the