\uc2 (OC) All right. We are less than 30 minutes away from the first debate between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . When we come back, the global stakes in this most extraordinary moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now in Philadelphia. About 28 minutes until the debate begins, a debate the whole world is watching because the decisions the next president makes will have a profound effect on America's role in the world, from Ukraine to Russia and of course Israel. So, let's start there with our Matt Gutman, who has spent months covering the conflict in the Middle East. And Matt, I know this debate is being watched very closely there.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very closely, Martha. And a former longtime adviser for Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu told me that he's gonna be watching very closely for what former President Trump has to say about Iran. He said for Netanyahu, both Harris and Trump are known quantities. But the adviser telling me that for Netanyahu, a Trump presidency would be a dream come true, especially because of the mounting scrutiny that Trump has, that Netanyahu has been under increasing allegations that he's been dragging his feet on this hostage deal and ceasefire, mounting even further after the release of that video showing the tunnel in which those six hostages were executed including American Hersh Goldberg-Polin, that just a couple of hours ago.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if you ask Israelis and Palestinians what they're looking for from Vice President Harris, they're likely to say they want to know what she has to offer that is different from what the Biden administration has been doing the past several months despite massive efforts. They've promised that a deal is around the corner, but it remains elusive. And it's gonna be a lot easier of a sell for the former president, who's never faced a crisis like this but he's likely gonna say and repeat what he said in the past, that if he were president October 7th would never have happened, if he were president he would have been able to have strong-armed \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Netanyahu into a ceasefire and hostage deal that would've saved the lives of many Palestinians and of course those hostages, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of challenges about that tonight for sure, Matt. Thanks so much. Ukraine also at the center of this. Let's bring in chief foreign affairs correspondent Ian Pannell, who is in Ukraine. Closely watched there as well, Ian.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that's right. I mean, I spoke to an adviser to the President's office today, and he used one simple word, critical. It's critical what happens tonight. It's critical what happens in November, not just to Ukraine, but I think this sends strong signals to other critical areas in terms of foreign policy for the United States, for example, China. And this war is just grinding on. I mean, overnight Ukraine launching one of its biggest drone attacks on Russia since the start of this war. Twenty drones were shot down in the Moscow region, over 145 into Russia, an apartment block hit, a woman killed and one of Moscow's airports was also struck.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What we've seen is Ukraine repeatedly trying to target Russia in an attempt to try to bring the war home to the Kremlin and to the Russian people. And this is partly because they've been given restrictions on the weapons they're being supplied by the United States and others and what Ukraine is hoping is that those restrictions are gonna be lifted. US Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is gonna come here to Kyiv this week. Speaking in London he confirmed that Iran has now transferred ballistic missiles to Russia.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's expecting those are gonna be used in, in the coming weeks. He describes this also as a critical moment for Ukraine. So all eyes are gonna be on tonight's presidential debate, even, apparently, President Zelenskyy expected to tune in. And the outcome, I think, of this presidential debate is gonna be critical to the outcome of Ukraine's defense against Russia's invasion. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks so, so much, Ian. And Ian and I have both spent a great deal of time in Afghanistan, Jon, as you know, and that will be a key issue tonight. President, former President Trump has been slamming the Biden-Harris administration for that disastrous withdrawal, the way it was done in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, look, let, let, let's say it bluntly. It was a dark moment for the Biden campaign, for the Biden presidency. It might be the darkest moment of his presidency. In fact, the Republicans have now given the Congressional Medal of Honor, wait, not, not the Medal of Honor there. They, they, they, they announced...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM AUGUST 16, 2021\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Medal of Freedom, Medal of, Congressional Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Medal of, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, to the, to the 13 service members who died at, at Abbey Gate in, in Afghanistan. And one thing, if you look at that moment, so that withdrawal from Afghanistan happened in August of 2021. The other thing that happened in August of 2021 was that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became an unpopular president. His popularity, his favorability ratings went below 50 percent. They never came back.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was also at the time saying this would not be like Saigon. We all saw the, saw those images of that. It, it's hard to play that down. If you're the President, it's hard to call that a success. And Mary, let's go back to Israel and Gaza, a key issue also for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and a very, very fine line she has to walk.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A very fine line. And this is one of those rare issues where she is taking a notably different tone from the President. She has been much more forceful in speaking out about the humanitarian crisis in Gaza, stressing, yes, of course Israel has a right to defend itself, but saying Palestinians also have the right to dignity, security and freedom. Now, policy-wise she is largely vowing to continue the policies of the Biden administration, but there is no question on this issue she is facing the most pressure from Democratic voters. I've been at nearly every Harris rally so far, and at almost every one she is interrupted by protesters eager to make their voices heard on that. They will be listening closely.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're strong. Very strong voting bloc there too. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they're gonna be listening to that message. All right. So, coming up, Senator Tom Cotton and Governor Josh Shapiro are standing by with just about 20 minutes until the debate. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And there you see the debate stage here in Philadelphia. The debate now less than 20 minutes away. And we are joined on behalf of the Trump campaign by Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton. Senator Cotton, thanks a lot for being, we only have a couple minutes. This debate's about to get underway. But I wanted to ask you, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do you think's gonna show up tonight? You know him well. Is it gonna be the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that is gonna be making personal attacks, insulting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? Or, is it gonna be the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that's gonna focus on his issues?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Jon, I think you're gonna see the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who's president for four years, who has a clear record. This campaign is unusual. We don't have a candidate running for office for the first time. The American people remember what it was like under President Trump. We had stable prices, rising wages, a secure border, and peaceful stable relations around the world. Under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we've had record high inflation. We've had a wide open border. We've had war breaking out everywhere. The American people are gonna remember after tonight that times were good under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they want another four years of President Trump back in the White House.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we noticed he was spending a lot of time looking at the angles, studying the, the debate stage. He does know the television of this as well.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well, he does. But I think the style and the debate stage and the lighting and all that is gonna be secondary in the minds of most voters, especially those trying to make up their minds to what they hear from the two candidates about their record. People remember that with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  they had good times. They had rising wages. They had stable prices. We had peace and stability. And with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , a dangerous San Francisco Liberal, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House we've had none of that. So, I think it's gonna be a clear contrast between President Trump and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tonight, and the American people starting tonight and over the next eight weeks are gonna make the decision they want President Trump back in the White House.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. There's Senator Cotton with one side of this debate, very sharply worded. Mary, the other side.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For the other side now, Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro joins us, of course a key surrogate for the Harris campaign. You heard Senator Cotton there. He says that voters know who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is. You've seen the polling, a large slice of voters say basically they don't know enough about the Vice President. Is this her last best chance to reach out to those critical voters, show them who she really is, what she stands for?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JOSH SHAPIRO (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 Look, I'm not sure what world Senator cotton was living in, but when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in charge the only thing we had more of in this country was more chaos. I think under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  you're gonna see someone who's compassionate, who cares deeply about the welfare of all Americans no matter what they look like, where they come from, who they love, who they pray to, an economic message that lifts people up instead of shutting out the middle class. I expect you'll see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  speak not only about her strong track record but her strong vision for the nation and prosecute the case effectively against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You mentioned the case against Trump. What about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? You know, how does she go about building off of their record but making clear to voters who are concerned that she won't just be Biden 2.0?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JOSH SHAPIRO (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 Well, serving as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's vice president made her fit and ready to serve on day one. I think it's important that we examine \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's positions, her vision, the things she wants to do for this nation. Elections are about the future. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's vision for the future is a stark contrast to the dangerous vision of the future that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has, one of less freedom and more chaos under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will see how it unfolds on the debate stage. Governor Shapiro, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. So we've talked to surrogates from both campaigns. Coming up, we are going to talk to somebody who has both debated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and helped him prepare for big debates, with tonight's showdown less than 14 minutes away.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. If you're just joining us, I'm Martha Raddatz. We're live from Philadelphia at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 , the site of tonight's debate, now about ten minutes away.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I'm \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  here with Mary Bruce and Rachel Scott. The candidates are just off the stage ready to walk out. They are in their hold rooms just minutes from making that walk. Martha, you have been backstage at this moment. What's it like?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, it's, it's stressful but it's also that time you just say this is go time. You get in your zone. I'm sure the nominees, the candidates right now are thinking the same thing. They've studied. They're gonna go out. Open the curtain.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Well then, let's bring somebody in who has been there both debating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and helping him prepare for general election debates, the former Governor of New jersey, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . So, so Chris, you've, you've helped prepare Trump for this moment several times. He says he doesn't prepare, but, but he actually does. Bring, bring us into that room right now. What's going on with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, at, at this point he's, he's in the worst hour of, of his prep because you just have to sit there and wait, and wait for the time to come. And they make you get there early. And so he is there. He's not really being spoken to. Yeah, a little small talk here and there, but nobody's trying to give him any kind of clues.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, so let me ask you the question I...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's go time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Let, let me ask you the question I, I tried to ask Tom Cotton. He didn't exactly give me an answer, but you will. Which Trump is gonna show up tonight? You know what I mean by that. Which, which Trump?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think at the beginning of the debate the Trump who will show up will be the calmer \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He knows inherently, after the 2020 first debate against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that the other \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not the one that's a winning formula. The question's gonna be will he be able to continue to maintain that approach through questioning by David and Linsey and through any comments that Vice President Harris makes in his direction? And that will be the key to how he performs tonight.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you and I were talking. You helped prepare him for those 2016 debates against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . He, he told you that he had respect for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was smart, as you were preparing. What about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? We hear not the same.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, certainly he's not saying that publicly in any way. And I can tell you that in 2016 he would regularly say during the many debate prep sessions that we had with him that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was smart, that she was a good debater, that she was not to be taken lightly. And I don't think there was ever a moment in 2016 where he took her lightly. I do think he took \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lightly in 2020, and clearly during the prep sessions that I had with him it was very, very difficult to get him to focus on what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  might or might not do in that first debate and I think his performance in part reflected that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the question's going to be is what he's saying publicly what he really believes? We know that often with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that's performance. But I will tell you that in his 2016 prep, he was serious about getting ready for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . In his 2020 debate prep, he was completely unserious about getting ready for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think performances in both those debates reflected that approach.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , he'll be watching with us. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we have about six minutes left before the debate. So, let's bring in Donna Brazile, formerly of the DNC, and Reince Priebus, the RNC. Welcome to both of you. And Donna, let's start with you. You have said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has to own the future. But she also has to own the past. So how does she do this?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's quite easy because I do believe that in talking about the future you draw from your past. You draw from your past experiences. She draws from how she was raised, the values that was instilled in her as a young lady. Look, she is steady on her feet, steely in her resolve, and tonight she should talk to the American people. Sit in their living rooms. Go to their kitchen table and say look, I have some ideas that I want to share with you. There's so much that we have done together over the last four years, but here's what I think we can do together over the next four years.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Be positive in her approach and really remind the American people that we don't wanna back to the chaos and the confusion, the disaster that made of course the American people go out in droves in 2020 to fire the former president. Say I want to be a president that leads all Americans. I want to keep it strong and secure and here's the plan that want to share with you tonight.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Donna, I, of course, meant Biden. She, she has to own up to the Biden administration, any mistakes they made too. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is gonna go after her on that.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, Martha, they made mistakes. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Mr. Pence made mistakes. But you know what? If we focus on our mistakes we won't focus on all of the good things that we could do together as Americans. I do believe there is something to say about people who are now able to work, the people who can buy their insulin without paying over $1,000. Thirty-five dollars. There's so much good that she can talk about, from the visits that she's made to small towns across this country, large cities across the country. Bring us together. The American people want to be led into the future, not dragged back to another era.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Reince, I, I want your thoughts on, you've also prepped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for debates back in 2016 I believe. What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do you think we'll see tonight? Can he be disciplined about talking about policies and what kind of president he will be?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well, he certainly can be. I mean, the reality is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't have a lot to lose tonight. I mean, his support has always been almost unmovable. So, the second piece is the honeymoon I think is over for kamala Harris. And you all kind of touched on it with Donna. She, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has to answer for the state of the economy that she took a role in in the Biden administration, the border, you know, her, her past positions on decriminalizing the border, decriminalizing certain drugs, what her role was in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So really the, the spotlight is on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tonight because she played a role in the Biden administration, she wants to act like she's not an incumbent. So, this is where the focus is going to be. And she's got a lot to answer for tonight. And I think that's where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's focus will be and where it actually should be tonight.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, one of the things he wants to do is have female voters look at him. How does he attract female voters after saying things about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and we'll see how he pronounces that tonight, things he's said about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , that she's dumb as a rock. That's not really going to attract female voters, is it?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. No, no, it won't. But I also think that just like he did in the first debate against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I think the formula here is to constantly tie \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to the Biden administration, which is where Trump is winning against her on the economy, inflation and all these other problems that we're having, and then sit back and let her talk. Let her explain herself, her past positions, current positions. How does she feel about how Biden has done on the economy? How does she feel about the border? Then turn it over and let her talk.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks so much for joining us tonight, Donna and Reince. We're all staying tuned. Can't wait.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I have to say as we are just a couple, less than two minutes away, this such an extraordinary moment and especially, Rachel, when you consider that these two people that have been the center of our lives for the last two months have never met each other.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Have never even met each other. And Harris said if you've got something to say, say it to my face. The question tonight, does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually go there? He's questioned her racial identity. He's questioned her intelligence as well. Or, does he stick to the issues?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, this is the largest audience they're likely gonna have in this race. It will be interesting to see if Harris can make clear the stark choice before voters and where does she decide to land her punches. She can punch hard. Can she do that while rising above the needling coming her way? But first and foremost, are we gonna see a handshake when the two of them meet for the first time?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We didn't back in 2016 when I did that debate. We'll stand by to see that tonight. But the first time side to side together.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I have my doubts that we're gonna see that handshake, but what we are gonna see is a spirited debate and perhaps the most consequential general election debate we have ever seen because almost certainly gonna be the last one. So, with the, the stakes so high, the Presidential Debate just moments away, we, we have the entire news division keeping an eye on everything. We are gonna be fact checking on our live blog. It's all gonna be happening right here. The debate now just ten seconds away. And we will be back with our postgame show as soon as it's over. We are gonna toss it right now to David Muir and Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020240925ek9a00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE POST-TALK\b0\par\par\uc2 3,689 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2024 PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 20024, DEBATE, HARRIS, TRUMP, RULES, VOTERS, ISSUES, POLLS, COTTON, SHAPIRO\par \par \uc2 2024, DEBATE, HARRIS, TRUMP, ECONOMY, ABORTION, BORDER, RIOT, WAR, ACA, CLIMATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS: 2024 PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, HARRIS, TRUMP, HANDSHAKE, WALZ, ABORTION, VANCE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are here live on ABC with our post-debate comments. I'm Martha Raddatz. It was a lively debate, from the economy to abortion to Afghanistan, they had it all. And it was historic, \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , fiery.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, wow. Wow, wow. That was, that was quite a debate. And I'm \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  here with, of course, Mary Bruce and Rachel Scott. Our reporters across the news division have been updating our live blog looking at some of the allegations made back and forth, doing fact checks. You can find all of that on abcnews.com. But I would like to replay the moment at the very start of this debate because it was quite a moment. The handshake. And if you look...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Nice to see you finally.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (2024 DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, poignant moment, first of all, because we hadn't seen a handshake. We haven't seen a handshake in a long time.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she initiated that one.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, she did immediately.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And not, I mean, not only did she initiate it, she walked over to him and sought him out, put the hand out and also made the first words.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and probably had a plan if he hadn't...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...put his hand out either.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Interesting. So, so Martha, bottom line, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I think clearly what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wanted to do was be the candidate of hope, the candidate of the future. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seemed angry. Within four minutes, I think, we, we saw him going from, from very serious at the beginning, never really seemed to look at her, to just angry. I mean, really yelling at some points, trying to make some, to make his points. I don't think we heard a lot of policy and plans on either side of that, despite those questions over and over again. But what you had was that optic.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You had \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  looking over, baiting him again and again and again. The microphones are muted when they weren't speaking but many times she didn't need a microphone. You watched \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  get mad, scowl through things. And you know, Jon, we were all talking leading up to this about how this would go, what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  you would see. I think he had a plan for talking about what he saw as, as, as a next, as a next term, but then he was talking about rumors about people eating their neighbors' pets.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, he really devolved into that as well.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, I - I mean, look, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn't really explain some of the big questions that were out there about why she changed her policy views on so many things. Part of the reason is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really was not effective in bringing the debate to her. I agree with you. I think that he looked not just angry but also rattled. And in contrast, Harris, who again, much of the world doesn't know, Harris looked like she belonged on the stage, looked like she is somebody that can serve as president.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is certainly how the campaign is feeling tonight. I can tell you, the Harris campaign feels very good. They feel they had a very strong night, that she was in command, that she did look presidential and that she was able, they feel, to hold \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accountable for his actions. And of course, that was their goal going into this, that she would be able to put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's record under a microscope. And they feel that the strongest example of that was that exchange on abortion where she was able really to blame \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  directly to his face.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, she said what you are putting women through is unconscionable. Yes, she goaded him. She flat out trolled him many times, got him off message. But she also talked directly to voters, even inviting them to a Trump rally to underscore that central argument in her campaign, that she says he's only out for himself, that she argues she's out for the American people.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Well, look, the Trump campaign is gonna be seizing on the fact that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not do a great job today really explaining her policy shifts, right? And that is what they have been seizing on. They're really trying to define her for voters, when voters have a lot of questions about her policy positions. At the same time, though, almost every single thing that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said tonight got under Trump's skin.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is exactly what his allies and his advisers did not want to see. I can tell you I do not think that part of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's debate prep was people eating dogs in Springfield or comparing rally sizes. They wanted him to stay focused on the issues, especially the ones that he has an edge on Harris, economy and immigration.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, you get, you wanna get under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's skin, you question the people at his rallies. And that was something else. Martha, you have?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have with us right now Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , of course, the Democratic vice presidential candidate. Welcome, Governor Walz. How are you feeling? What's your reaction to the debate tonight?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEMOCRATIC VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Martha, I'm feeling great because what I knew and I think so many knew and now many Americans know is you saw a president for all America in \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . You saw the confidence. You saw the vision. You saw the poise. And you saw the compassion for people, understanding she spoke about an economy that moves us forward, moves all of us forward, lets everybody be a part of it. And she continued to lay that out. Whether it's reproductive freedom or how we start small businesses or how we make homeownership easily.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEMOCRATIC VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 On the other side, you saw a nearly 80-year-old \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  angry, unhinged and as you've been discussing, talking about things that are deeply concerning because they make no impact on the American public. He shouldn't be anywhere near the White House. And I have to tell you, if it, if it weren't so dangerous, it reminds you of, of an old man yelling at the clouds. That was his thing, get off my yard. That's what (inaudible). Contrast that with the poise of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  laying out the (inaudible) of vision where everybody matters.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEMOCRATIC VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And so, I think (inaudible) whole country can see what happened tonight. We'll keep pushing forward. That's the way that the country wants to see our politics be. She laid it out clearly. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought nothing tonight. Anger.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Gov...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEMOCRATIC VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Resentment.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor, Governor Walz, I, I do want to ask you, she did not ask, answer questions on abortion restrictions. She never really answered that question. Can you clear that up? Where the Harris-Walz ticket stands on that.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEMOCRATIC VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Certainly. You couldn't get any different than this. The reinstitution of Roe versus Wade, what was the law of the land for, for 30, for 52 years you saw this in there. And him pushing back to the states, she's exactly right. (Inaudible) people like Amanda in Texas, which everybody saw on the stage in Chicago, nearly bleeding out, having sepsis twice because she can't get the care. She's been absolutely clear on this. Everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, that is untrue. The clarity of this is Roe. When you have Roe, you have the protections in place where women make their own decisions with their health care providers, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks so very much for joining us, Governor Walz. Jon?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we have the other side of the ticket. We have Senator JD Vance, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's running mate, joining us now. Senator Vance, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Jon. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I, I want to get to one part in the debate where you came up. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was asked if he - he would veto a national abortion ban. He didn't answer the question, suggested, I guess, that he wouldn't. And then it was pointed out that you in fact said not that long ago that he would veto a national abortion ban. Can you explain the, the, the mix up here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think the President's been very clear that he doesn't want a national abortion ban. I think in some ways he finds the question a little bit ridiculous because why are we asking him about legislation that's never gonna actually happen and why would he veto it or not veto it when he says very explicitly that, that he doesn't support a national abortion ban and he wants these policies to be made by the states. But I think there's a deeper and more important issue here in this debate which is that you had \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  effectively echoing platitude after platitude after platitude.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 The American people can't pay their grocery bills on platitudes. They can't put their kids in a house on platitudes. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had a lot to say but very little actual substance behind it for how she's gonna lower grocery prices, secure the border and make housing more affordable in this country. She's been the Vice President for three and a half years. She can't run on her record. And she apparently can't run on much of her plans either because it's just a lot of slogans and not a whole lot of substance.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but Senator Vance, why wouldn't he just say that he would veto the national abortion ban? And by the way, had you spoken to him about it before you were put in a position to answer that question?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 No, I, I hadn't spoken to him about it because again, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thinks the question is absurd because he doesn't support a national abortion ban and he's been explicit about it. And Jon, I think that it's so preposterous for the media to focus so much on an issue where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been crystal clear and not on the fact that people can't afford groceries and housing because of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's policies.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 This election is actually quite simple and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and apparently the media would like to distract Americans on issues by the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's been crystal clear about but they're not talking nearly enough, this debate didn't talk nearly enough about the fact that Americans are struggling because they can't afford groceries. They can't afford housing and a lot of their kids are dying of fentanyl overdoses because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has let the Mexican drug cartels take over the southern border.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well, Senator...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 This is a disgrace what's going on in our country, Jon, and we could do so much better if we just had better leadership. We've got the best workers, the most beautiful country, the best natural resources but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's leadership is squandering the great benefits of this country. It's why we've got to get back...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's leadership.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator, by, by the way, I asked because he hadn't been crystal clear and he said something different than what you said. That's why I was seeking clarification. But let me ask about this for just, just very quickly. I heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before this debate describe \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as, I'll just read a few of them, a low-IQ individual, somebody who doesn't have the mental capacity to be president, really dumb, unable to put two sentences together. Is that what you saw on the stage tonight?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Jon, what I saw was a lot of slogans. I think the American people are smart and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  talks to them like they're children. She repeats these ridiculous platitudes where if you actually think about what she said you realize that it doesn't make any sense. She talks a lot about her plans but then her plans when you analyze the substance don't make any sense. And more importantly, Jon, the American people don't get fed on plans. She has been the Vice President for three and a half years. What she can't do, refuses to do and has no ability to do is to say I accomplished lower prices, because she didn't\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Or, I accomplished lower housing prices, because she didn't. Or I secured the southern border, 'cause she didn't do that. She has failed as vice president.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And the fundamental question is, do we want to give her a promotion? I think the answer has to be no, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Senator Vance, thank you for joining us. Mary.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One thing both sides can agree on, this was I a consequential night in this race. More reaction when we come back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now here at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  in Philadelphia and with \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . So Governor Christie, you heard the debate. Remarkable debate. Bottom line, what'd you think?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She was exquisitely well prepared. She laid traps. And he chased every rabbit down every hole instead of talking about the things that he should've been talking about. This is the difference between someone who's well prepared and someone who's unprepared. Whoever did debate prep for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be fired. He was, he was not good tonight at all. And what Harris did was what she had to do, which was to prove that she belonged on that stage. She has a voice that wants to try to unite the country.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent more time talking about people eating pets, people at his rallies and whether he had more or less, than he did about the economy. And that is a huge fail tonight. I think what we're gonna see is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is now being seen by a lot of voters as a potential president. That's what she needed to accomplish tonight. Trump better get to work or he's gonna lose this election.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Characteristically blunt assessment from Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 . Thank you, Governor. Mary?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Let's go to former DNC chair Donna Brazile. So Donna, you have certainly studied many a debate in your career. How does this one compare?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think it was one of the best debate performances I've ever seen in my life. And I've seen a lot. And I've helped a lot of people prepare for these big moments. She met the moment. She seized the opportunity to tell the American people what she would do in, during her four years. She commanded the issues and she did not allow the former president to bait her. Instead she led the way forward. She turned the page. And I think this race is about to get even more interesting now.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will see if it moves the needle, Donna. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Over to the other side now, former RNC chair Reince Priebus. All right. Reince, the Harris campaign says they want another debate. If you were advising the Trump campaign, would you tell them to do it?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure, I would tell them to do it. I - and the reason is, is like I said before, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's numbers just don't move. I mean, the reality tonight, I know Donna's excited, but the truth is she didn't, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn't address the most important thing that the American people care about, which is the economy and the cost of living. She had no answers on immigration. She had no answers when she was asked about defunding the police.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She had nothing to say about the families that were lost in Afghanistan, which was a true layup. She should've been fact checked a lot more on some of the things she said in regard to this comment about a bloodbath which was about the auto industry. So, look, I think a lot of things were, were not said tonight that she needed to say in order to move the numbers. And I don't think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's numbers will move at all. They haven't moved at all for months.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Reince Priebus, Donna Brazile, both, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Well, I've got to say, again, what an incredible debate. Consequential debate, no doubt. I don't know how many votes will actually be moved. I don't know how many persuadable voters are actually out there. But \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had a chance here to introduce herself to the country, to people who know who she is but don't know where she stands. Now, you could debate how much she did in terms of the specifics of where she stands. But she stood up there. And I, I think it was very significant to hear \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  say that she acted like, she seemed like she belonged.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. And, and Chris was clearly impressed with that. But I, to your point, Jon, what matters is what the voters think.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was hurling things against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And he's been convicted, and January 6th, and everything else she could throw at him. But in the end, right now, he is tied with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Does this change anything? We've all seen debates where people are very proud of what they did. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  thought she won that debate, many debates in 2016 and she didn't win. So you have to be very careful.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's very important to see what the voters think.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And look, the Harris campaign certainly thinks that they were, you know, they put him on the defensive. They are going to keep getting out and making this message. She's gonna be out there in those critical battlegrounds again later this week. They're still optimistic, they want that second debate. As Rachel said, the message from the campaign tonight Vice President Harris is ready for a second debate. They're looking for one in October. They say, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , question mark. One last goading of him to end the night.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, that is something else. I did not predict that at the beginning. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Bottom line, voters out there want a change. That's what the polls say. And both of these candidates really trying to present themselves as the agent of change. We will have to see in the polls who did the most effective job of doing that tonight.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We will see. All right, that is it for us here in Philadelphia, a night to remember for sure. Our race for the White House post-debate coverage continues on ABC News Live, Disney Plus, \uc2 Hulu\uc2 , and anywhere else you stream live news. And your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020240925ek9a00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE HIGHLIGHTS OF FIRST PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,990 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, DEBATE, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us from the National Constitution center in Philadelphia, where candidates \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just wrapped up what may be the only debate of this presidential race. The energy on stage tonight contentious, reflecting the all-out battle for votes. An election with only 55 days left in which still either candidate's to win or lose.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Presidential candidates \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  meeting on the debate stage for the first time.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . So good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Good to see you. Have fun.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Vice President walking to the former president's podium to shake his hand in Philadelphia. The two standing just six feet apart. From the start, the gloves were off.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you're going to hear from the same old, tired playbook. A bunch of lies, grievances and name calling.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump punching back.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 She's a Marxist. Everybody knows she's a Marxist. Her father is a Marxist professor in economics, and he taught her well.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The stakes could not be higher for tonight's debate. The two candidates locked in a dead heat in polls. The economy and inflation on the top of American voters' minds. Trump laying the blame on the Harris and Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 We have inflation like very few people have ever seen before, probably the worst in our nation's history. We were at 21%.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris responding.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us the worst unemployment since the Great Depression. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us the worst public health epidemic in a century.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Like the Trump Biden debate, tonight, there was no studio audience and the microphones were turned off.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And remember this, she is Biden. You know, she's trying to get away from Biden. "I don't know the gentleman," she says. She is Biden. The worst inflation we've ever had. A horrible economy because inflation has made it so bad, and she can't get away with that.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris responding.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Clearly, I am not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I am certainly not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And what I do offer is a new generation of leadership for our country.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The debate turning personal.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was fired by 81 million people. So, let's be clear about that. And clearly, he is having a very difficult time processing that.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump claiming Biden was pushed off the Democratic ticket.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 You talk about a threat to democracy. He got 14 million votes and they threw him out of office. And you know what, I'll give you a little secret. He hates her. He can't stand her.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both candidates getting heated when discussing the issue of abortion, an area of vulnerability for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Her vice-presidential pick says abortion in the ninth month is absolutely fine. He also says, execution after birth, it's an execution, no longer abortion because the baby is born, is okay. And that's not okay with me. Hence, the vote.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ABC News fact checking the former president.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is no state in this country where it is legal to kill a baby after it's born.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris, after reminding Americans of Trump's role in selecting members of the Supreme Court who overturn Roe v. Wade, went on the attack.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I absolutely support reinstating the protections of Roe v. Wade. And as you rightly mentioned, nowhere in America is a woman carrying a pregnancy to term and asking for an abortion, that is not happening. It's insulting to the women of America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 63% AMERICANS: ABORTION SHOULD BE LEGAL IN ALL OR MOST CASES\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to a \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  study, a majority of Americans believe abortion should be legal in all or most cases. President Trump, hammering the Biden administration on the border, an area of vulnerability for Harris.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 There's never been anything done like this at all. They've destroyed the fabric of our country. Millions of people let in. And all over the world, crime is down. All over the world except here.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris defending her record, pointing to the bipartisan border bill that Trump killed.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That bill would have put more resources to allow us to prosecute transnational criminal organizations for trafficking in guns, drugs, and human beings. But do you know what happened to that bill? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got on the phone, called up some folks in Congress and said, kill the bill. And you know why? Because he'd prefer to run on a problem instead of fixing a problem.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At one point, Trump repeating debunked claims about immigrants in Springfield, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 In Springfield, they're eating the dogs, the people that came in. They're eating the cats. They're eating, they're eating the pets of the people that live there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I just want to clarify here. You bring up Springfield, Ohio. And ABC News did reach out to the city manager there. He told us there have been no credible reports of specific claims of pets being harmed, injured or abused by individuals within the immigrant community.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both candidates going after one another. Harris needling Trump about his rallies.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 You will see during the course of his rallies, he talks about fictional characters like Hannibal Lecter. He will talk about windmills cause cancer. And what you will also notice is that people start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 People don't go to her rallies. There's no reason to go. And the people that do go, she's bussing them in and paying them to be there.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ABC News anchor Linsey Davis challenging the Vice President on her changing positions.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Vice President Harris, in your last run for president, you said you wanted to ban fracking. Now, you don't. You wanted mandatory government buyback programs for assault weapons. Now, your campaign says you don't. You supported decriminalizing border crossings. Now, you're taking a harder line. I know you say that your values have not changed. So, then why have so many of your policy positions changed so my values have not changed?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 So, my values did not change, and I'm going to discuss every one of the, at least every point that you've made.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight was crucial for both candidates. But for Harris, it was her biggest stage yet, as she re-introduces herself to voters. Recent polls showing nearly a third of voters say they still don't know enough about her. While tonight was her first presidential debate, she did not hold back.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 When you then talk in this way in a presidential debate and deny what, over and over again, are court cases you have lost because you did, in fact, lose that election, it leads one to believe that perhaps we do not have in the candidate to my right the temperament or, or the ability to not be confused about fact.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump launching attacks on Harris's record.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 She's worse than Biden, in my opinion. I think he's the worst president in the history of our country. She goes down as the worst vice president in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight was Trump's seventh presidential debate. Less than three months ago, he was on the debate stage facing off against a different candidate.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 With the COVID, excuse me, with dealing with everything we have to do with, look, if we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Shortly after Trump survived an assassination attempt, then Biden dramatically ending his bid for a second term, passing the torch to his vice president. Polling shows Harris and Trump locked in a tight race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A "New York Times"-Siena poll found Trump leading Harris by one percentage point. The former president's support holding steady despite a New York jury finding him guilty on all 34 counts of falsifying business records in the Stormy Daniels hush money case.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I think this is so rich coming from someone who has been prosecuted for national security crimes, economic crimes, election interference, has been found liable for sexual assault. And his next big court appearance is in November at his own criminal sentencing.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump responding.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm winning most of them, but those are cases. It's called weaponization. Never happened in this country. They weaponized the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight's debate coming amidst prolonged conflict in the Middle East. Israel, a controversial topic.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 She hates Israel. If she's president, I believe that Israel will not exist within two years from now. And I've been pretty good at predictions, and I hope I'm wrong about that one. She hates Israel.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vice President Harris, he says you hate Israel.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's absolutely not true. I have my entire career and life supported Israel and the Israeli people. He knows that. He's trying to again divide and distract from the reality, which is it is very well known that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is weak and wrong on national security and foreign policy. It is well known that he admires dictators, wants to be a dictator on day one, according to himself.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After the debate, Taylor Swift endorsing Harris, writing in part on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , "I'm voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  because she fights for the rights and causes I believe need a warrior to champion them." With the election less than two months away, the impact of tonight's debate can't be underestimated.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What matters is what the voters think. But in the end, right now, he is tied with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Does this change anything? You have to be very careful, and it's very important to see what the voters think.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back, our powerhouse roundtable drills down on the action of the evening and tells us what it all means as we move through this contentious and consequential presidential election.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020240912ek9a0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW MAMMOGRAM RULE GOING INTO EFFECT MANDATES WOMEN BE TOLD IF THEY HAVE DENSE BREASTS\b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEAD HEAT\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, HARRIS, TRUMP, PREP, CAMPAIGN, CHENEY\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, HARRIS, PREP, CAMPAIGN, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, HARRIS, TRUMP, POLLS, BATTLEGROUNDS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, EVACUATIONS, ACRES, TEMPS, DRY, PASCUA\par \par \uc2 STORM, GULF COAST, RAIN, WINDS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, HIGHWAY, INJURIES, ACCIARDO, MANHUNT, SUSPECT, DINOTO\par \par \uc2 HILL, POLICE, VIDEO, DOLPHINS, HANDCUFFED\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , RODGERS, 49ERS, INJURY, COMEBACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORNING TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 RODGERS, JETS, ACHILLES, REHAB, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 EMMYS, LEVY, HOST, "SCHITT'S CREEK", COMEDY\par \par \uc2 , COLLEGE, NOTRE DAME, NORTHERN ILLINOIS, HAMMOCK\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, DEBATE, ELECTION, PREPARATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO HIGH-STAKES DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS & TRUMP PREPARING FOR FIRST FACE-OFF\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, GEORGIA, FAMILY, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUESTIONS IN DEADLY SCHOOL SHOOTING INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT'S FAMILY CLAIMS THEY CALLED SCHOOL TO WARN\par \par \uc2 MAMMOGRAM, DENSITY, BREASTS, NOTIFICATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW MAMMOGRAM RULE GOING INTO EFFECT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANDATES WOMEN BE TOLD IF THEY HAVE DENSE BREASTS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Robin. We turn now to the mammogram rule going into effect starting tomorrow. The FDA had mandated that women be told if they have dense breasts. Janai Norman is here with why this is critical for some women screening for breast cancer, incredibly important change. Good morning, Janai.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Rebecca, incredibly important, as mammograms are one of the most important screening tools for breast cancer and now an important new update. Beginning tomorrow, all women having a mammogram will be notified if they have dense breasts, which can make the spotting of cancer on a mammogram more difficult. The Food and Drug Administration is requiring facilities doctors to notify their patients about breast density in an effort to improve cancer detection. So, some states had required mammogram reports to include breast density, but tomorrow it becomes a nationwide requirement. So, women with dense breasts can talk to their doctors and can look into taking additional steps like maybe more frequent screenings or think about things like imaging, like ultrasound or MRI.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) About half of women over the age of 40 have dense breast tissue, and while it doesn't pose an immediate threat, it does increase the risk of breast cancer. Current guidelines suggest that women start getting regular mammograms at age 40, or even earlier depending on additional risk factors like family history, Robin. And so, obviously, we know that early detection is important, and this is big for so many women.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It really is, it really is Janai. Our thanks you to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240927ek990003a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COUNTDOWN TO HIGH STAKES DEBATE TRUMP SET FOR TV TOWN HALL AMID STRUGGLES WITH ABORTION RIGHTS STANCE\b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER EXTREMES ACROSS AMERICA\par \par \uc2 RAIN, FLOODS, HEAT, TEMPERATURES, FIRES\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, DEBATE, PREP, CAMPAIGN, POLICY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , DEBATE, CAMPAIGN, POLICY, RALLY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about that. Thank you, Mary. Let's go to Rachel Scott of course who's tracking the Trump campaign. Not one for traditional debate prep.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO HIGH STAKES DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP SET FOR TV TOWN HALL AMID STRUGGLES WITH ABORTION RIGHTS STANCE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , George, has never been a fan of any formal traditional debate prep, so I am told there will be no mock debates for the former President, instead he is reviewing policy with a small group of advisers. And his campaign has brought on former Congresswoman \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 . You may remember she was once a rival to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  during the 2020/2019 Democratic primary election, she challenged her on that debate stage.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This shaping up to be the most consequential moment of this campaign. But this is not the face-off that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted. He wanted to run against President Biden, especially after his first performance in that debate. And since Biden has exited the race, Trump has really had a hard time trying to define Vice President Harris. His advisers, close allies to the former President, have been urging him to steer away from those personal attacks.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Stay focused on the message and those critical issues, especially the one where he has an edge on Harris, immigration, crime, the economy. The former President will be doing a town hall later today. He's delivering remarks on the economy in New York tomorrow, then holding a rally in Wisconsin this weekend. But even those who are close to Trump admit they don't know which version of him is going to show up on that debate stage next week, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It will be a big night. Okay, Rachel, thanks very much. Of course you can see the debate on Tuesday night right here on ABC. Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240920ek940005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,399 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2024 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, GAZA, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , GOLDBERG, POLIN, DNC, HOSTAGES, HAGARI, BIDEN, NETANYAHU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , TRUMP, HARRIS, IVF, ARLINGTON, CEMETERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAELI AMERICAN HOSTAGE HERSH GOLDBERG-POLIN'S BODY RECOVERED\par \par \uc2 POLIS, HARRIS, CNN, MEDICARE, BORDER, ECONOMY, HOME, BUYERS\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 IPSOS, POLL, 2024, VOTE, RESULTS, HARRIS, TRUMP, GENDER, ISSUES, ATTRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, CNN, BASH, ELECTION, IPSOS, TRUMP, IVF, ABORTION, BIDEN, DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Roundtable's back, former DNC chair Donna Brazile, former RNC chair, Reince Priebus, "Wall Street Journal" national politics reporter Vivian Salama, and ABC News Washington bureau chief, Rick Klein. So, Donna, you see our poll. It certainly looks a lot better for, for Democrats than it did when Joe Biden was at the top of the ticket, but no real convention bounce. This race is basically tied, maybe...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NOW JUST 65 DAYS UNTIL ELECTION DAY 2024\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...a slight lead for Harris.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO POST-CONVENTION BUMP FOR HARRIS IN NEW ABC/IPSOS POLL\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...the Republicans didn't get a bounce. We didn't get a bounce. But you know what? We've gotten a boost, a boost of energy, a boost of excitement, which allows us to close some of the gaps that Rick pointed out in the analysis of the poll. I'm, I'm very satisfied with where things are, but there's room to grow. This candidate, Kamala Ha