nia.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rick, Rick, what do you think, are you hearing? Will we see more interviews or do they think that was that like, it?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think there's gonna be more, maybe even more with like, influencers, podcasters, maybe not as many mainstream media interviews as, as we go. But look, I think you saw in that, kind of a, a strategy of not engaging with Trump. That to me was the most interesting, is that, when Dana Bash from CNN put forward some of these, some of the critiques, she didn't want to go there.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think you have to guard against complacency at the same time, because right now she's telegraphing a strategy that says she wants Trump to be Trump on his own. That means they're gonna be a little more hesitant on some policy.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, some real locker room talk from a reporter who broke barriers for women covering professional sports. Her remarkable story when we come back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020240911ek9100006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP CAMPAIGN FOCUSES IN ON PENNSYLVANIA HOLDS MULTIPLE EVENTS, ATTENDEE ARRESTED FOR ATTEMPTING TO JUMP PRESS RISER\b0\par\par\uc2 838 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORMY HOLIDAY WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRAVEL, LABOR DAY, STORMS\par \par \uc2 LABOR DAY, AIR TRAVEL, TSA\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, LABOR DAY, GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HITTING THE ROAD DURING THE HOLIDAY WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS ON THE BEST TIMES TO TRAVEL AS GAS PRICES CONTINUE TO FALL\par \par \uc2 GAUDREAU, HIGGINS, HOMICIDE, YORK, KAPLAN, BUMM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARREST IN TRAFFIC DEATHS OF NHL PLAYER & BROTHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALLEGED DRUNK DRIVER CHARGED WITH HITTING PAIR OF BICYCLES\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, WALZ, ELECTION, BLALOCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CAMPAIGN FOCUSES IN ON PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLDS MULTIPLE EVENTS, ATTENDEE ARRESTED FOR ATTEMPTING TO JUMP PRESS RISER\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To the presidential race now. Heading off to a new phase now of intensity this holiday weekend, the first mail-in ballots going out to voters next week. And the ABC News debate is two weeks away. ABC's Maryalice Parks is here. Good morning, Maryalice. It's quite literally go time.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Gio, good morning. It's the end of summer, that means the start of this final leg in the sprint to the White House. The campaigns are counting down to that debate and, yes, working to get out the vote now with some ballots in crucial states like North Carolina mailed out the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  overnight speaking at the national summit of the controversial parents' rights organization Moms for \uc2 Liberty\uc2 , known for pushing book bans and upping attacks on public schoolteachers, focusing on immigration.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Our country is being poisoned, poisoned. And your schools and your children are suffering greatly.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Education front and center. Gwen Walz, wife of Democratic vice-presidential candidate \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , a public school teacher herself, holding her first solo event with fellow educators.\par \par \uc2 GWEN WALZ (WIFE OF GOVERNOR TIM WALZ)\par \par \uc2 Say it with me, we are not going back. One more time in your best teacher voice, we are not going back.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Earlier Friday, Trump campaigning in western Pennsylvania, ticking through other hot-button issues like energy and climate, hitting Harris for saying in 2019 she would ban fracking. In that new CNN interview, Harris explaining her position.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VP HARRIS WRAPS UP GEORGIA BUS TOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENDS VALUES & POLICIES IN FIRST SIT-DOWN INTERVIEW SINCE HEADING TOP OF TICKET\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 No, and I made that clear on the debate stage in 2020 that I would not ban fracking. As Vice President, I did not ban fracking. As President, I will not ban fracking.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She added that her values around needing to fight climate change have not changed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP ON THE CAMPAIGN TRAIL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS HE WILL VOTE NO ON MEASURE LOOKING TO OVERTURN FLORIDA'S NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And abortion, Trump Friday now saying that he will, as a Florida resident, vote no on a ballot measure aiming to overturn the state's near total abortion ban. Even though just the day before, he indicated the state's new six-week ban was too restrictive.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 When I heard about it, I disagreed with it. At the same time, the Democrats are radical.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Outside Trump's event, protesters focused on this very issue.\par \par \uc2 LARRY BLALOCH (PENNSYLVANIA RESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 His work at appointing three of the Supreme Court justices who committed themselves to overthrowing Roe v. Wade is a human rights slap in the face.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump supporters looking ahead to the debate.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm looking forward to him contrasting himself with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , our current vice president, because it's dramatically different and she keeps copying his policies.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, Harris put out a statement overnight, again saying that she promised she would push for a national law restoring abortion rights. Look, all of this back and forth on the issues, clearly a sign that the candidates are gearing up for that debate. They know just how consequential that head-to-head will be.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. So many things turning and twisting in the midst of all this. Thank you, Maryalice. And, of course, the ABC News debate, it will host the first presidential debate between Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It takes place on September 10th in Philadelphia's \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  beginning at 9:00 PM Eastern. Our own David Muir and Linsey Davis are going to be the moderators, and it is live right here on ABC and streaming on \uc2 Disney\uc2 + and \uc2 Hulu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will be watching that, Gio.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240913ek8v00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE TRUMP PROMISES TO MAKE IVF FREE WITHOUT OFFERING DETAILS\b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, BIDEN, CAMPAIGN, VALUES, WALZ, POLICIES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IVF, CAMPAIGN, ABORTION, FLIP-FLOP, POLL\par \par \uc2 MOSQUITOS, VIRUSES, TRIPLE E, WEST NILE, CASES, PROCTOR\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, AIRPORTS, TSA, ROADS, TRAFFIC\par \par \uc2 STORMS, LIGHTNING, WINDS, HAIL\par \par \uc2 DOJ, REPORT, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SEX ABUSE, HOROWITZ, NASSAR, MANLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, FOOTBALL, COLORADO, SANDERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAT INDEX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "JURASSIC PARK"\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, TRIAL, VENUE, JUDGE, TAYLOR, BATEY, GONCALVES\par \par \uc2 FANTASY FOOTBALL, SCHEFTER, WOMEN, POPULARITY\par \par \uc2 HOWARD, COLLEGE, FOOTBALL, MATCH-UPS, PREVIEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ELECTION, REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS, IVF\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meantime, former President Trump's blitzing state in the Rust Belt and, overnight, making a promise about IVF. Selina Wang is in La Crosse, Wisconsin, where he held a town hall last night. Good morning again, Selina.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP PROMISES TO MAKE IVF FREE WITHOUT OFFERING DETAILS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Rebecca. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to rebrand his image on women's reproductive rights. It's an issue he knows could hurt him at the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump is now claiming he would make IVF treatment free by having insurance companies or the government pay for it. But Trump provided no details on how this costly plan would actually work. IVF treatment can cost tens of thousands of dollars. And nearly 90,000 babies are born in the US through fertility treatments every year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a statement to ABC News, Vice President Harris' campaign saying, quote, "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's own platform could effectively ban IVF and abortion nationwide. Trump lies as much if not more than he breathes, but voters aren't stupid." Now, Trump made the striking proposal on IVF despite repeatedly bragging about appointing the Supreme Court justices to overturn Roe versus Wade, opening the door for new restrictions on fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP UNDER PRESSURE OVER ABORTION RIGHTS STANCE\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in his own home state of Florida, Trump is now saying that he opposes the state's six-week ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Trump's campaign later tried to appease his base of support of anti-abortion supporters, saying that Trump has not yet said how he would vote on Florida's ballot initiative. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Without a doubt one of the biggest issues this election season. All right, thank you so much, Selina.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240913ek8u00038\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE TRUMP PROMISES TO MAKE IVF FREE WITHOUT OFFERING DETAILS\b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, BIDEN, CAMPAIGN, VALUES, WALZ, POLICIES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IVF, CAMPAIGN, ABORTION, FLIP-FLOP, POLL\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That debate moderated by our very own David Muir and Linsey Davis on September 10th, right here on ABC News. Thank you so much, Mary. We turn now to former President Trump, who is blitzing states in the rust belt, and overnight making a promise about IVF. Selina Wang is in La Crosse, Wisconsin, where Trump held a town hall last night. Good morning, Selina.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP PROMISES TO MAKE IVF FREE WITHOUT OFFERING DETAILS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Rebecca. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to rebrand his image on women's reproductive rights in order to shake up this race, claiming that he would make a IVF treatment free without giving any details on how that would work.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  floats a striking new proposal as he suddenly tries to rebrand himself on women's reproductive rights, an issue that he knows could hurt him at the polls in November. Trump claiming that he would make in vitro fertilization free if elected without providing any specifics on how he would pay for this costly plan or enact it.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Under the Trump administration, your government will pay for, or your insurance company will be mandated to pay for, all costs associated with IVF treatment.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) IVF can cost tens of thousands of dollars, with nearly 90,000 babies born in the US through fertility treatments each year.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We want to produce babies in this country, right, we want to produce babies?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP UNDER PRESSURE OVER ABORTION RIGHTS STANCE\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' campaign telling ABC News, "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's own platform could effectively ban IVF and abortion nationwide. Trump lies as much if not more than he breathes, but voters aren't stupid." Trump making this proposal despite repeatedly bragging about appointing three of the Supreme Court Justices who overturned Roe V. Wade, opening the door to new restrictions for fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 He broke Roe V. Wade, and we did something that nobody thought was possible, we gave it back to the states.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Earlier this year, \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  declared frozen embryos human beings, entitled to full rights, causing fertility clinics across the state to shut down for weeks. A new poll finding Harris has a 16-point advantage over Trump on who voters trust more to handle the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In his own home state of Florida, Trump now saying he opposes the state's ban on abortion at six weeks, before most women even though they're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I think the six weeks is too short. It has to be more time, and so, and I've told them that I want more weeks.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump contradicting his own statements that the issue of abortion should be left to the states. But Trump's campaign attempting to appease his antiabortion base, later saying he has not yet decided how he will vote. He simply reintegrated that he believes six weeks is too short.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Over the years \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has flip-flopped on the issue of abortion, but his focus on IVF is clearly an attempt to blunt Harris' attacks that he would rip away women's reproductive freedoms. Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Selina, there's also a new ABC News/IPSOS poll out overnight. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW POLL: TWO THINGS SAY TRUMP NOT PREPARED TO ACCEPT ELECTION RESULTS\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Rebecca, that's right. In a new ABC News/IPSOS poll, it finds that only 29% say that Trump is prepared to accept the outcome of this election. But Trump's election denialism also resonates with some voters. Despite there being no evidence of widespread voter fraud, one in three say they are not confident that votes will be accurately counted in this election. Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Selina, thank you. Gio.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240913ek8u0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : SIX WEEKS "TOO SHORT" ATTEMPTS TO CLEAN UP COMMENTS ON FL ABORTION BAN\b0\par\par\uc2 888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LABOR DAY, TRAVEL, STORM, REINFORD, FARBSTEIN\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GAUDREAU, HIGGINS, YORK, DWI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NHL STAR & BROTHER KILLED IN CRASH\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, ELECTION, MULLIN, ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP: SIX WEEKS "TOO SHORT"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the race for the White House and the abortion battle heating up. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , after saying a six-week abortion ban is too short, today saying he'll cast a vote that would keep Florida's six-week ban in place. Trump also promising to pay for IVF. The \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign responding to these shifting positions saying, "Voters aren't stupid." ABC's Terry Moran with the former president in Johnstown, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ATTEMPTS TO CLEAN UP COMMENTS ON FL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, former President Trump in Johnstown, Pennsylvania, taking aim at Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and that first interview, telling the crowd he will be a "pro-family" president.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And if I'm elected this November, for the first time ever, your government will pay for or require insurance companies to pay for all costs associated with IVF fertility treatment. We want more babies to be born in America. We want more babies. We need them. We need them.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hours earlier, the Trump faithful gathering outside.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is that an important issue for anybody?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We wanted to hear their opinion about Trump's latest proposal.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 So, he did the right thing, as far as you're concerned?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Sure. I mean, I wouldn't have my children if it weren't for in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You came out and support it.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I did seven years of infertility treatments, I spent $70,000, I had six miscarriages. Yeah, it's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some anti-abortion right leaders are dismayed by Trump's proposal. They see IVF, where unused embryos are often discarded, as equivalent to abortion. That's a minority opinion, and we didn't hear it here. But some Trump loyalists are questioning how mandating insurance companies to pay or having the government cover treatments would work.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2  (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 I think there's going to be an issue on how you pay for it. There's always that issue.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tonight, Trump is trying to play cleanup after he indicated he might support a referendum on the ballot in his home state of Florida that aims to overturn the state's near-total abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I think the six week is too short. It has to be more time. And so, that's and I told them I want more weeks.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So, you'll vote in favor of the amendment?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm voting that, I am going to be voting that we need more than six weeks.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The campaign quickly issued a statement, "President Trump has not yet said how he will vote on the ballot initiative in Florida, he simply reiterated that he believes six weeks is too short." When Trump was asked again today, he said "I'll be voting no." Trump has consistently said abortion should be an issue handled at the state level since the three Supreme Court justices he appointed helped overturn Roe versus Wade. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  meanwhile saying she would sign a bill to restore women's right to choose abortion nationally if it came to her desk. Her campaign saying "Voters aren't stupid." In that CNN interview, Harris laid out her priorities for American families on day one if she's elected.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I've already laid out a number of proposals in that regard, which include what we're going to do to bring down the cost of everyday goods. What we're going to do to invest in America's small businesses. What we're going to do to invest in families. For example, extending the child tax credit to $6,000 for families for the first year of their child's life to help them buy a car seat, to help them buy baby clothes, a crib.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry Moran following the Trump campaign in Pennsylvania tonight, where that rally just ended there. And, Terry, with this race extremely tight, it seems both candidates are really trying to appeal to more moderate voters?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're seeing it in real-time, Whit, with dramatic changes from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on his message on abortion. Just weeks ago, he was still bragging about killing Roe versus Wade and praising some of those state bans. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she's abandoning her previous support for fracking, for Medicare, for banning fracking, for Medicare for all, and others. Both candidates in this tight race, they know where the last crucial votes are, in the middle. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020240904ek8u00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HARRIS: "MY VALUES HAVE NOT CHANGED" DEFENDS CHANGING POLICY POSITIONS IN FIRST INTERVIEW\b0\par\par\uc2 821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, ELECTION, REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. It's great to have you with us on this Thursday night. I'm Whit Johnson, in for David.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS: "MY VALUES HAVE NOT CHANGED"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're following major developments tonight in the race for the White House. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  defending his recent visit to Arlington National Cemetery after a reported altercation between the Trump campaign and an Arlington staffer.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But we begin with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her running mate Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  holding that first highly anticipated joint interview. Harris asked to explain how her views have shifted on key issues since she first ran for president in 2019, including on the border, fracking, and single payer health care for all. Her answer? "My values have not changed." Meantime, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is blitzing states in the Rust Belt, making news on his positions on IVF and abortion even as the controversy grows over an event at Arlington National Cemetery. The army responding tonight.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And new national polling shows the race is a virtual dead heat with just 68 days until the election. ABC's chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce leads us off in Savannah, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENDS CHANGING POLICY POSITIONS IN FIRST INTERVIEW\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in her first in-depth interview since entering this race, insisting her values have not changed since her last run for the White House. Speaking with CNN in an interview that airs tonight.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I think the most important and most significant aspect of my policy perspective and decisions is my values have not changed. You mentioned the Green New Deal. I have always believed, and I have worked on it, that the climate crisis is real. My value around what we need to do to secure our border, that value has not changed.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris and running mate \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  sitting down for that interview together as they wrap up a two-day tour of battleground Georgia. Harris capping it off rallying thousands in Savannah, as new national polls tonight show Harris now with a slight lead. The Harris campaign optimistic they're putting key Sun Belt states back in play. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had been leading here in Georgia, but polling now shows Harris gaining ground.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 One of the things that we know, this is going to be a tight race until the very end, okay? So, let's not pay too much attention to the polls, because we are running as the underdog.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris hammering Trump on one of the issues that will define this race. Abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Why don't they trust women? Well, we trust women. And when Congress passes a bill to restore reproductive freedom, as president of the United States, I will proudly sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well aware that reproductive rights have become a political liability for Republicans, Trump, who has bragged about appointing three of the justices who overturned Roe vs. Wade and said states are handling the issue brilliantly, now tonight saying he thinks six-week abortion bans are too short and saying, his administration would ensure IVF treatments are more easily accessible, vowing to have the government or insurance companies cover the cost.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Under the Trump administration, your government will pay for or your insurance company will be mandated to pay for all costs associated with IVF treatment, fertilization for women, IVF treatment. Because we want more babies, to put it very nicely.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Though he did not provide any details on how he would ensure IVF treatments are fully paid for. And his own running mate, JD Vance, has voted against protecting the right to fertility treatments like IVF.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce joins us now where that Harris rally just wrapped up. And, Mary, the Vice President responding to Trump's IVF comments and making news about her potential Cabinet?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Whit, in a statement just now, the Harris campaign notes that Trump's own party's platform could effectively ban IVF and abortion nationwide. Adding, quote, "Voters aren't stupid." The Harris campaign is hoping that the issue of abortion will help her expand her reach beyond the Democratic Party's base. In that interview set to air tonight, Harris will say that, if elected, she would likely appoint a Republican to her Cabinet, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary, our thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020240903ek8t00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN NEW STUDY ON BREAST CANCER SCREENING\b0\par\par\uc2 1,161 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXTREME HEAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORMS\par \par \uc2 HEAT, TEMPERATURES, RECORDS, SCHOOLS, DONNER\par \par \uc2 REINDICTED, SMITH, TRUMP, SUPREME COURT, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, WALZ, BATTLEGROUND, TRUMP, RFK, GABBARD\par \par \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , OUTAGE, CUSTOMERS, SOFTWARE\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, RAID, WEST BANK, PALESTINIANS, HOSTAGE\par \par \uc2 DELTA, KILLED, INJURED, WORKERS, TIRE, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 TRIPLE E, MOSQUITO, DEATH, WEST NILE, CDC\par \par \uc2 US OPEN, SINNER, STEROID, BANNED, CONTROVERSY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GIRARDI, CONVICTED, FRAUD, EMBEZZLEMENT, ARCHIE, JAYNE\par \par \uc2 MACKRELL, VANKER, SPEEDING, CRASH, DRIVER, JUSTICE\par \par \uc2 MCCREERY, CONCERT, ASSAULT, PERFORMANCE, AUDIENCE\par \par \uc2 US OPEN, COMEBACKS, OSAKA, EVAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WATCHING THE TROPICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXTREME HEAT\par \par \uc2 HEAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 RICHINS, MURDER, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 STRESS, PARENTS, KIDS, MENTAL HEALTH, MURTHY, NILLES, MARTINEZ\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, SCREENING, AMA, DENSITY, MRI, BIOPSY, COMEN\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we've got to be there for each other, too, Whit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA HEALTH ALERT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON BREAST CANCER SCREENING\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. We turn to a "GMA" health alert. A new report in the "Journal of the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 " finding additional breast cancer screening could benefit some women with dense breasts and save lives. Medical oncologist Dr. Elizabeth Comen from NYU Langone Health joins us with more. It is so nice to see you, Doctor. This is such an important study. Tell us more about it.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for having me. So, this study was really important because it showed that the addition of breast MRI to traditional mammography may improve the detection of breast cancer, particularly in women cancer, particularly in women with dense breasts, which is over 50 percent of the population of women over the age of 40.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Now, the caveat to this study is that while we know that detecting cancers earlier can help save lives, the, there's a cost to that. So, with breast MRIs, there's an increase in what we call "false positives." False positives are when you're called back and may need a biopsy, but that biopsy ends up being negative, meaning no cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 So, I've had a biopsy myself, and I can say it can be very anxiety-provoking. So, this study means that it's important for women who are going to get a breast MRI or have been recommended to get a breast MRI, that they talk to their doctor about the expectations that they may be called back for something like a biopsy.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I've, I've have been called back for biopsies myself...\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...not after an MRI yet, I haven't had that done, but it is something that I talked to my doctor about, and I felt better going into it because of that conversation. You also mention dense breasts.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And these are a, lots and lots of women have them.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes, exactly. So, over 50 percent of women over the age of 40 have dense breasts, but there's a common misconception that you can feel this. That if your breasts look perkier or feel a certain way or sag more, that that relates to breast density.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 "Breast density" is a term that a radiologist finds on a mammogram. Okay? So, very different than anything you can see or feel on physical exam. And the reason why this is so important to know is if you have dense breasts, it can be harder to detect a breast cancer on a mammogram.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Dense breasts means that you have more what's called fibroglandular tissue as related to fatty tissue, and on a mammogram, the fibroglandular tissue looks white, fatty tissue looks black. So, if your breasts are more dense, it's harder to see cancer, because cancer can look white. So, it's like trying to find a snowflake in a snowstorm. Mammograms are harder to detect breast cancers in dense breasts.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is why then an MRI becomes a possibility, and ultrasounds as well, as part of all of this.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 A possibility, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW \uc2 FDA\uc2  MANDATE STARTING SEPTEMBER 10\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Starting on September 10th, it will now be mandatory to inform women of this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOCTORS MUST INFORM WOMEN IF THEY HAVE DENSE BREASTS\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Exactly. So, now the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is requiring standard language so that women are informed when they get a mammogram if they have dense breasts. So, if you've been told that you have dense breasts, it's incredibly important that you talk to your doctor about your personal risk factors for breast cancer, your family history, and what that breast density means for you, because it may mean that you - need additional imaging, such as with an ultrasound, or in this case, a breast MRI.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And speaking of ultrasounds, MRIs, mammograms...\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...the science, or the, the acknowledgements around this have changed a lot over the years...\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...what women are supposed to do, when they're supposed to start.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is the date? What is the age now?\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Exactly, there's been so much ping-ponging back and forth. But recently, the United States Preventative Services task force lowered the age. So, for women of average risk, you should start screening with breast mammograms at age 40.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Now, how do you determine your risk? There are tools online, breast - cancer risk assessment tools that you can do with your doctor to determine your lifetime risk of breast cancer. If it's an average risk, then you would start screening at age 40.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 But if you have a family history of breast cancer or other types of cancers or other personal risk factors, you may need to start screening before the age of age 40.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Dr. Comen, thank you very much. We appreciate it. Important conversation.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240916ek8s00065\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STATE OF RACE TWO WEEKS BEFORE FIRST MAIL IN VOTING TRUMP'S WEEK AHEAD; MICHIGAN, WISCONSIN, PENNSYLVANIA\b0\par\par\uc2 745 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY LANDSLIDE\par \par \uc2 LANDSLIDE, EVACUATIONS, DEAD, INJURED, DAMAGE\par \par \uc2 CYBERATTACK, SEA-TAC, TRAVELERS, OUTAGES, OPERATIONS\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, ATTACKS, POWER, WATER, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , GAZA, CEASEFIRE, ATTACKS, NAEF\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , IRAN, LEBANON, US MILITARY\par \par \uc2 CAMPAIGNING, VOTING, TRUMP, HARRIS, ABORTION, VANCE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tensions are very high in the region. All right, Martha, thank you for your reporting. We're going to turn now the race for the White House, as the Trump team packs its schedule this week with events in battleground states. And Vice President Harris enjoying a post-convention boost along with a healthy war chest, raising more than $500 million in just over a month. Selina Wang is at the White House now with the latest. Good morning, Selina.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF THE RACE TWO WEEKS BEFORE FIRST MAIL IN VOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S WEEK AHEAD; MICHIGAN, WISCONSIN, PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Gio. In just over a month, Vice President Harris has completely up ended this election. She's gaining ground over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the polls and in her fundraising. To counter her rise, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is stepping up his campaigning, blitzing through battleground states this week.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  preparing to storm the battleground states that will decide this election. The former President struggling to define his opponent, rallying supporters later today in Michigan, before hitting Wisconsin and Pennsylvania later this week, hoping his new endorsement from independent candidate \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , will give him a boost. With Vice President Harris erasing his lead in the polls.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 He has a lot of votes that he could have gotten. I think he's gonna have a huge influence.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The former Democrat now suspending his campaign that was in part fueled by conspiracy theories around vaccines and COVID.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY (FMR PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 We talked about, not about the things that separate us, because we don't agree on everything, but on the values and the issues that bind us together.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump ramping up his campaigning to counter Harris' rise. Her campaign reporting they've raised more than half a billion dollars in just over a month. With the most donations pouring in the hour after her acceptance speech at the Democratic National Convention on Thursday night. Harris striking a moderate tone, trying to appeal to independents.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I will never waiver in defense of America's security and ideals because in the enduring struggle between democracy and tyranny, I know where I stand and I know where the United States belongs.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As part of his new offensive, Trump trying to strike a new tone on one of his biggest vulnerabilities, abortion. Writing over the weekend that his administration would be "Great for women and their reproductive rights." This after months of boasting that his pick to the Supreme Court helped overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His running mate, Senator J.D. Vance, now committing that Trump would not sign a federal abortion ban, something Trump has repeatedly waffled on.\par \par \uc2 NEWSCASTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Would he veto it?\par \par \uc2 SEN JD VANCE (REP) (OHIO/2024 REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I think he'd be very clear, he would not support it.\par \par \uc2 NEWSCASTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 But would he veto it?\par \par \uc2 SEN JD VANCE (REP) (OHIO/2024 REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I mean, if you're not supporting it as the President of the United State, you fundamentally have to veto it.\par \par \uc2 NEWSCASTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So he would he veto a federal abortion ban?\par \par \uc2 SEN JD VANCE (REP) (OHIO/2024 REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I think he would.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In response the Harris campaign says, "The women of America cannot trust what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and JD Vance say because they've already seen what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and JD Vance have done. Trump ripped away women's freedoms in all 50 states by overturning Roe."\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Harris is also getting back on the trail this week with a bus tour through battleground Georgia, but she's also focused on debate preparation ahead of her first face-off with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on ABC News on September 10th. Sade.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240910ek8q00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON DNC FEATURES SOME REPUBLICANS AND FMR. TRUMP AIDES OPPOSING HIM\b0\par\par\uc2 2,944 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2024 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 WAR, ISRAEL, ROCKETS, HEZBOLLAH, LEBANON, CEASEFIRE, BIDEN, NETANYAHU, TEHRAN, IRAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL STRIKES\par \par \uc2 SANDERS, ISRAEL, GAZA, DNC, HARRIS, IMMIGRATION, CAMPAIGN, AD, BORDER, WALL\par \par \uc2 COTTON, KINSINGER, GRISHAM, DUNCAN, HARRIS, TRUMP, KENNEDY, ABORTION, MEDAL, 2020\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (REP) (FORMER CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has suffocated the soul of the Republican Party. His fundamental weakness has coursed through my party like an illness, sapping our strength, softening our spine, whipping us into a fever that has untethered us from our values.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GRISHAM\uc2  (FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE OFFICIAL)\par \par \uc2 He has no empathy, no morals, and no fidelity to the truth. He used to tell me, "It doesn't matter what you say, Stephanie. Say it enough, and people will believe you."\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 GEOFF DUNCAN (REP) (FORMER GEORGIA LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR)\par \par \uc2 His actions disqualified him from ever, ever, ever stepping foot into the Oval Office again.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those were some of the many Republicans who spoke out against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this week at the Democratic Convention. I'm joined now by top Trump ally, Senator Tom Cotton of Arkansas. Welcome to "This Week." So, what, what do you...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL CONDUCTS PREEMPTIVE STRIKE AGAINST HEZBOLLAH\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Morning, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you make, all those Republicans speaking at the Democratic Convention?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DNC FEATURES SOME REPUBLICANS AND FMR. TRUMP AIDES OPPOSING HIM\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, what I make of them is that Trump derangement syndrome is a real thing. But what most Americans remember is that for four year when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, we had peace and prosperity. Everything has gone to hell under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  over the last four years. And that's a clear contrast we have. This is an unusual presidential election because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been president before. The American people know what he did. They know that he understands the kind of pain they've suffered for the last four years under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I think that's why they're ready to return \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the White House.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But there seemed to be a clear strategy on the part of the Democrats to try to appeal to the Republicans. And of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has absolutely solid support in the Republican base. But there are a lot of Republicans uneasy with him as the nominee again, and they seem to be going right at them.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well, in every election, Jon, you have some members of one party endorsing a candidate of the other party. That's a very traditional aspect of American politics. I mean, look at what just happened this week. The Democratic Party under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has gone so far to the left that you actually had a Kennedy endorse a Republican. That's pretty remarkable that the Democratic Party has become so radical under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that a member of the Kennedy family has come out to endorse a Republican.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I don't know if Bobby Kennedy Jr. at this point is that representative of the Kennedy family. But I wanna get to that in, in a second. But, but first, there, there are reports, there's obviously a bit of a momentum change here up. You see it in the polls. You see it in the, in the money flowing into the Harris campaign. And there was a report in "Politico" that Trump now knows that he has to change things up or, or, or that he could lose. You, you, you talk to him. I mean, what, what, what's your sense, though? Do you need a change in direction?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump and his campaign has known all along, and they've said all along that this race is gonna be a close race.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Actually, he said it would be a landslide, just to be clear. He said he was gonna win in a landslide.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 But look, we, we know the race will probably come down to a few hundred thousand votes in a few states - President Trump has been campaigning hard and vigorously now for months. Obviously, that's gonna increase in its pace as we get closer to the election. And, and President Trump is gonna draw a sharp contrast with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who has supported things like decriminalizing illegal immigration or giving taxpayer-funded health insurance to illegal aliens, or taking away health insurance on the job for 170 million Americans, banning gas cars, confiscating firearms. These are all positions that...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible). What do you mean taking away health insurance? What are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP FIRES OFF PERSONAL ATTACKS DESPITE PLEAS TO FOCUS ON POLICY\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 She said when she ran for president that she wants to eliminate private health insurance the job...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 ...for 170 million Americans, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I mean, I mean, that is not her position now. She no longer (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 How do you know that's not her position? How do you know that's not her position?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, she, she said she no longer supports...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 She has not said that. She has not said that.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Medicare for all.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 She has not said that. Maybe anonymous aides on a Friday night have that. But the - but the last thing that she said on most of those comments...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this was not a radical convention. I mean, she, she, she, as you heard me go through with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , she is not taking the positions of the far left of her party. She's clearly making an effort to move to the middle.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS, DEMS LEAN INTO 'FREEDOM' MESSAGE DURING CONVENTION\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I, I did hear what you said to Senator Sanders. And I, I thought it was clear that he's very disappointed that she's taking these efforts not to change her positions, but to hide her positions, Jon. The American people are totally justified to conclude that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a dangerous San Francisco Liberal based on what she campaigned on the last time she ran for president and what this administration has done the last four years.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Again, you would've thought watching the Democratic Convention last week that the Democrats are not in office, that they're not in power, that they're campaigning against an incumbent Republican, when in reality, she's been part of the failures of the Biden-Harris administration for four years. And when she campaigned for president in her own right, she did, in fact, promise things like decriminalizing illegal immigration...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, but...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 ...and taking away health insurance (inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, and yeah, but that's a position she's clearly changed on and she has said she has changed.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 No she hasn't.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, yes, yes she has.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 No. No, she has not. Jon...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) she, she...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 ...she has not said that. She has not, you pointed out to Senator Sanders repeatedly things that her campaign has said. Anonymous aides speaking on background reporters have said, well, she no longer believes these things. Okay. Maybe she has changed her position on things like taking away your health insurance and confiscating your gun. If she has changed her position, she owes it to the American people to come out and say...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS AIM TO WIN OVER INDEPENDENTS, ANTI-TRUMP VOTERS\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 ...in her own words when she changed and why she changed. That's one reason why she needs to address the American people and speak to these questions because the only basis they have to conclude what she will be like as president is what she's done for four years in this administration and what she said in her own voice in the last campaign. Remember, these are not, like, college essays, Jon. This is what she said when she was campaigning for president as a 54-year-old woman. If she's had such a radical change of heart in the last five years, she owes the American people answers.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, so speaking of radical changes of heart, I want to ask you about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had to say a couple of days ago. He said, and this is an exact quote, statement he put out, "My administration will be great for women and their reproductive rights." Is he now going to be campaigning on abortion rights? What's going on here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 He will be great for women. He was great for women. Women had great opportunities under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He had a healthy, growing economy. You had safe communities and you had peace. You had peace around the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP: MY ADMINISTRATION WILL BE 'GREAT' FOR 'REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS'\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But I'm asking you about the reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well, look, Democrats can use phrases reproductive rights all they want to describe their pro-abortion position which would force Americans to pay with tax dollars for abortion up to the moment of birth. That's the Democratic position.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, that's...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 They can use that phrase if they want to, they don't, they don't get monopoly...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Democrats say that is not (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 They don't get a monopoly on the language to cover up their radical positions.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but, but actually that's, it's now Trump using those words...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 But he, but he's been clear...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S STANCE ON ABORTION FORCES OTHERS IN GOP TO PIVOT\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...reproductive rights. But he, and he's also said, we also heard from JD Vance that he would veto a national abortion ban, and he came out against using the Comstock Act to, to prevent the, the shipping of the, the abortion pill. So what, what is happening? I mean, in fact, he, he said just the other day, he suggested that he may support the amendment on the ballot in Florida to provide a constitutional right to an abortion. I mean, what, what is happening with Trump on abortion?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 But, but, Jon, I think you just made a very important point. The only candidate here proposing a national abortion law is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and we know what that law looks like because congressional Democrats have voted on it. It would require taxpayer funding for abortion up to the moment of birth. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's, that's not the law. They want to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 No, that's not at all (inaudible) say about their law, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, I'm, I'm asking you about, I'm asking you about...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Jon?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  though. What...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 So, first off...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What, what, and you, you, you have been a, you have a solid pro-life record for as long as you've been in public office. What do you make of what he is saying now, saying he is going to be protecting reproductive freedom and that he could even support a constitutional amendment in Florida...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 First off, Jon...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...legalizing abortion?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 I voted on the law. You didn't. It does not codify Roe. It goes far beyond what Roe says. It would provide taxpayer funding for abortion up to the moment of birth. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been consistent. He said that Roe v. Wade was wrongly decided. Before Roe v. Wade, this question was largely regulated by the states. Now that Roe v. Wade has been reversed, it will be again, largely regulated by the states. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the one that proposes the radical national abortion law.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I also want to ask you something else that, that Trump had to say recently, is saying that the Presidential Medal of Freedom, which is something presidents give out, I mean, Trump gave one to, you know, to Devin Nunez, the former Congressman, is, is the equivalent and much better than the Medal of Honor, which of course, as, as the fact, as you know as a combat veteran, sacred. I mean, it is the nation's highest military honor. I mean, what, what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMPS DESCRIPTION OF MEDAL OF HONOR RECIPIENTS SPARKS BACKLASH\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Look, Jon, it's totally taken out of context.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's not taken out of context.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 He - he was, the point he was making is that he gives the Presidential Medal of Freedom, just like other presidents do, to civilians who have done important things for their nation, but who haven't sacrificed life and limb. And when you go to a Medal, Medal of Honor ceremony at the White House and you're presenting it to a soldier who's sacrificed his body or to his parents...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do, do, do you...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 ...or to his children, or his spouse, that it is of a different order of magnitude. It's totally out of context. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do, do, do, do you agree that the Presidential Medal of Freedom is much better? Those are his words.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 No. Jon, Jon, it was totally taken, you're totally taking that out of context. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was great for our military. He is great for our veterans. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the one who's proposed for instance to cut the military every single year. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the one who voted for military cuts when she was in the Senate. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has utmost respect for our veterans and our troops and you see that by his record in office.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and before you go, I, I want to ask you about something else Trump said, this is about the 2020 Election. It's something I have never heard him say before.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (2024 PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 This was last week in office for me because of a horrible, horrible election where I got many millions more votes than I got the first time, but didn't quite make it. Just a little bit short.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP SAYS HE CAME UP 'JUST A LITTLE BIT SHORT' IN 2020 ELECTION\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But short, is he now finally acknowledging the truth about the 2020 Election that he lost?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well, Jon, what he is saying there is that there were a lot of - irregularities. He challenged them in court. Those court challenges didn't succeed. It's as simple as that. It's what he's saying about this election...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No. He has said before it was stolen and he had many millions of more votes than...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...than, than, than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . All stuff that isn't true. You know. We've been over this. I mean, he's...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Well, look, I mean, obviously, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the President and Vice President, they're in the White House. That's why people are suffering so badly. His court challenges did not succeed. That's the point only he's making.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. I, I, okay. Well, thank you very much, Senator Cotton. I appreciate your time. Thanks for coming on "This Week."\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Coming up, former party chairs Donna Brazile and Reince Priebus join our Powerhouse Roundtable to break down the state of the race. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our ABC debate with former President Trump and Vice President Harris is now just 16 days away. How could it change the race? The Powerhouse Roundtable is here. We're back in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020240906ek8p00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEBRASKA'S DUELING ABORTION AMENDMENTS OPPOSING MEASURES TO APPEAR ON NOVEMBER BALLOT\b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAMPAGE AT GERMAN FESTIVAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY STABBING ATTACK\par \par \uc2 KNIFE, ATTACK, GERMANY\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, KENNEDY, ENDORSEMENT, POLL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP TOUTS RFK, JR. ENDORSEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SON OF ROBERT KENNEDY SUSPENDED INDEPENDENT CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEARTLAND ROASTS IN LATE SUMMER HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS TEMPERATURES FROM MINNESOTA TO TEXAS\par \par \uc2 FORTSON, DURAN, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER DEPUTY CHARGED IN KILLING OF AIRMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FACED ONE COUNT OF MANSLAUGHTER WITH A FIREARM\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS, MARTIN, GUILTY PLEA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER POLICE OFFICER PLEADS GUILTY IN TYRE NICHOLS' DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EX-COP CHANGES PLEA AHEAD OF NEXT MONTH'S TRIAL\par \par \uc2 SECRET SERVICE, TRUMP, ROWE\par \par \uc2 HOME EXPLOSION, BOYD, HALBERSTADT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE EXPLOSION LEVELS MISSOURI HOME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST TWO RESIDENTS, DOG KILLED IN BLAST FELT MILES AWAY\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, TEST KITS, VACCINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FREE AT-HOME COVID TEST PROGRAM TO RESUME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILIES WILL BE ABLE TO ORDER UP TO FOUR FREE TESTS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INTEREST RATES, POWELL, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTORS RESPOND TO INTEREST RATES NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOW GAINS MORE THAN 460 POINTS AS FED CHAIR SIGNALS RATE CUT COMING\par \par \uc2 RAILROAD, STRIKE, CANADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANADA RAIL STRIKE LOOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORK STOPPAGE COULD DISRUPT SUPPLY CHAINS\par \par \uc2 LITTLE LEAGUE WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, TRUMP, ELECTION, ENDORSEMENT, KENNEDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP TOUTS RFK JR. ENDORSEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SON OF ROBERT KENNEDY SUSPENDED IN INDEPENDENT CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 HOWARD, COLLEGE FOOTBALL, IRELAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLLEGE GAMEDAY IN DUBLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAKING THE SHOW OVERSEAS\par \par \uc2 FALL, TRAVEL, DEALS, KELLY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALL TRAVEL PREVIEW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHERE TO GET THE BEST DEALS RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 BASEBALL, FOUL BALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 ATTACK, GERMANY, MANHUNT, DEATHS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERAL KILLED IN ATTACK AT GERMAN FESTIVAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES HUNT FOR SUSPECT IN BRUTAL ATTACK\par \par \uc2 MANSLAUGHTER, INVESTIGATION, YACHT, SINKING, ITALY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS IN SINKING OF LUXURY YACHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES LAUNCHING MANSLAUGHTER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , RFK, ENDORSEMENT, CAMPAIGN, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP TOUTS RFK, JR. ENDORSEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SON OF ROBERT KENNEDY SUSPENDED CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 NEBRASKA, ABORTION, AMENDMENT, CHOICE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEBRASKA'S DUELING ABORTION AMENDMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OPPOSING MEASURES TO APPEAR ON NOVEMBER BALLOT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Voters in Nebraska, meantime, will choose between opposing abortion amendments on the ballot in November. One measure calls for an expansion of abortion rights. The other would enshrine a 12-week ban in the Constitution, with exceptions for rape, incest and to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240909ek8o00068\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "BOY MEETS WORLD" STAR'S CANCER DIAGNOSIS DANIELLE FISHEL HAS BREAST CANCER AT AGE 43\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DNC, BIDEN, HARRIS, ELECTION, CLINTON, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN PASSES THE TORCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT MAKES THE CASE FOR VP HARRIS IN EMOTIONAL DNC SPEECH\par \par \uc2 DNC, PROTESTS, ARRESTS, SNELLING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS OF PROTESTERS DESCEND ON DNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMONSTRATORS BREACH OUTER PERIMETER, FACE OFF WITH POLICE\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , WAR, HEZBOLLAH, BLINKEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL ACCEPTS "BRIDGING PROPOSAL" TO CEASE-FIRE DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BODIES OF SIX HOSTAGES RECOVERED FROM GAZA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN FORCES GAIN GROUND INSIDE RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS FORCED TO EVACUATE AS FIERCE FIGHTING RAGES\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, STORM, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY NORTHEAST FLOOD DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST TWO KILLED IN WAKE OF CATASTROPHIC CONNECTICUT DOWNPOURS\par \par \uc2 BOEING, \uc2 FAA\uc2 , INSPECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, KNIFE ATTACK, FOSTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAYLOR SWIFT MEETS WITH STABBING VICTIMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPERSTAR INVITES GIRLS & FAMILY BACKSTAGE AFTER DEADLY ATTACK\par \par \uc2 TYPE 2 DIABETES, MOUNJARO, ZEPBOUND, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIABETES GAME-CHANGER?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POPULAR WEIGHT LOSS DRUG COULD HELP HIGH-RISK PATIENTS\par \par \uc2 DONAHUE, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING PHIL DONAHUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OPRAH, MARLO THOMAS PAY TRIBUTE TO DAYTIME TV ICON\par \par \uc2 BODGE, LABOR DAY, SHOPPING, DEALS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EARLY LABOR DAY SALES ON RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUY NOW, OR WAIT UNTIL BLACK FRIDAY?\par \par \uc2 FOSTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, PRESIDENT, ELECTION, DNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN PASSES THE TORCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT MAKES CASE FOR VP HARRIS IN DNC SPEECH\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS, POLICE, DNC, GAZA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS OF PROTESTORS DESCEND ON DNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMONSTRATORS BREACH OUTER PERIMETER\par \par \uc2 "BOY MEETS WORLD", FISHEL, ACTOR, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BOY MEETS WORLD" STAR'S CANCER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANIELLE FISHEL HAS BREAST CANCER AT AGE 43\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to continue now with our "GMA" cover story and "Boy Meets World" actress Danielle Fishel, revealing she has been diagnosed with breast cancer. Reena Roy joins us now with more on this. Good morning to you, Reena.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, good morning to you. That's right, Fishel says that she was not planning on sharing this publicly\u160\'00\'A0but ultimately decided to speak out, knowing it could help others.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, Danielle Fishel revealing to fans she has breast cancer on her podcast, "Pod Meets World."\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE FISHEL ("BOY MEETS WORLD")\par \par \uc2 I was recently diagnosed with DCIS, which stands for ductal carcinoma in situ, which is a form of breast cancer. It is very, very, very, early.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The TV star, known as Topanga in "Boy Meets World"...\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "BOY MEETS WORLD"\par \par \uc2 ACTOR ("BOY MEETS WORLD")\par \par \uc2 Topanga.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...saying doctors discovered the cancerous cells at her yearly mammogram.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE FISHEL ("BOY MEETS WORLD")\par \par \uc2 The only reason I caught this cancer when it is still stage zero is because the day I got my text message that my yearly mammogram had come up, I made the appointment.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) About one in five new breast cancer cases will be ductal carcinoma in situ. The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  says nearly all women who are diagnosed can be cured of it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (SANCTUM MED + WELLNESS)\par \par \uc2 When we think of where the cells are and how they line the milk ducts, and stay within the duct, and therefore they're not spreading to any other surrounding breast tissue, it has a very high success case, and the prognosis is excellent and 98 percent survival rate when we look at 10 years and out.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now Fishel is hoping her story will remind others just how important it is to get those regular checkups and screenings like mammograms.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE FISHEL ("BOY MEETS WORLD")\par \par \uc2 I want to share this because I hope it will encourage anyone to get in there. If it's time for your appointment, if you've never had an appointment before, get in there.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Fishel says she will have surgery and will also have some follow-up treatment, certainly a long road ahead but is reassuring her fans that she's going to be fine and, thankfully, has the support that she needs. Will.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Reena, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240906ek8k0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL CONVENTION NIGHT ONE\b0\par\par\uc2 17,349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HARRIS, WALZ, BIDEN, CLINTON\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening from the Democratic National Convention right here in Chicago on this first night of the convention. History unfolding with Vice President Harris here in the United Center. You'll hear from her in a moment. She made her first appearance here tonight. But let's go live right down to the floor. Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton speaking to the convention.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 As vice president, as vice president, Kamala sat in the Situation Room.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 But we also know as vice president, Kamala sat in the Situation Room, and stood for America's values. I know what it takes, and I can tell you, as commander in chief, Kamala won't disrespect our military and our veterans. She, she reveres our Medal of Honor recipients. She won't be sending love letters to dictators. She will defend democracy and our constitution, and will protect American from enemies foreign and domestic.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 Think about it. The Constitution says, the president's job is to take care that the laws be faithfully executed. Those are the words of our founders. Take care. Just look at the candidates. Kamala cares, cares about kids and families, cares about America. Donald only cares about himself. On her first day in court, Kamala said five words that still guide her. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the people. That is something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will never understand. So it is no surprise, is it, that he is lying about Kamala's record, he's mocking her name and her laugh. Sounds familiar.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 But we have him on the run now. So, so, no matter what the polls say, we can't let up. We can't get driven down crazy conspiracy rabbit holes. We have to fight for the truth, we have to fight for Kamala as she will fight for us. Because you know what? It still takes a village to raise a family, heal a country, and win a campaign. And American needs every one of us, our energy, our talents, our dreams. We're not just electing a president, we're uplifting our nation. We're opening the promise of America wide enough for everyone.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 Together, we put a lot of cracks in the highest, hardest, glass ceiling. And tonight, tonight, so close to breaking through once and for all. I want to tell you what I see through all those cracks, and why it matters for each and every one of us. What do I see? I see freedom. I see the freedom to make our own decisions about our health, our lives, our loves, our families. The freedom to work with dignity and prosper, to worship as we choose, or not. To speak our minds freely, and honestly. I see freedom from fear and intimidation from violence and injustice, from chaos and corruption.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 I see the freedom to look out children in the eye and say, in America, you can go as far as your hard work and talent will take you, and mean it. And you know what? On the other side of that glass ceiling is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  raising her hand and taking the oath of office, as our 47th president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 Because my friends, when a barrier falls for one of us, it falls, it falls and clears the way for all of us. So for the next 78 days, we need to work harder than we ever have. We need to beat back the dangers that Trump and his allies pose to the rule of law and our way of life. Don't get distracted or complacent. Talk to your friends and neighbors. Volunteer, be proud champions for the truth and for the country that we all love.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 I want, I want my grandchildren and their grandchildren to know I was here at this moment, that we were here, and that we were with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  every step of the way. This is our time, America. This is when we stand up, this is when we break through. The future is here, it's in our grasp. Let's go win it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Quite a moment, eight years later, eight years after the election of 2016, Martha Raddatz, Secretary of State Hillary Clinton returning to the convention stage, saying on the other side of the glass ceiling, with all those cracks, I see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  raising her hand, taking the oath, as the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she seemed to have her whole heart and soul in those comments, in what she was saying about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . You would have to think that was a hard moment for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . In so many ways, she was passing the torch. She knew what happened to her eight years ago, she doesn't want that to happen to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . You know, I spoke to so many voters this week across battleground states. They often talked about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and the pride they had in a woman like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  running, but they also saw her as a cautionary tale.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The enthusiasm they said they had for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , they said, but we have to be careful, because we watched what happened to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You almost heard that from Secretary Clinton herself, Mary Bruce. She said no matter what the polls say, because she remembers, along the way, there were moments when she looked very strong up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and of course, ended up winning by nearly three million in the popular vote, but not winning the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and - what really strikes me there in listening to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is that she's talking about something that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  herself doesn't mention on the campaign trail, the fact that she could now be the first women president, perhaps, you know, learning a little bit of that lesson from Hillary Clinton's campaign. You don't hear \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  out there talking about breaking that glass ceiling, even though she very much could, could do that. So I think \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  here sees perhaps a second chance, a second shot at finally getting a woman into the White House.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's take a look at the stage right now. We're watching Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , who in recent weeks, signaled that he would support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  if that's the way this went. She's now at the top of the ticket. He changed the fortunes of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and tonight, hopes to do the same for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP JAMES CLYBURN (DEM) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 REP JAMES CLYBURN (DEM) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you and good evening. Thank you, South Carolina, thank you. Four years ago, Americans were experiencing high anxieties. And great uncertainties. A deadly virus raged, schools closed, businesses shuttered, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  mismanaged a crisis from day one. Looking out for himself, instead of the country. The American people responded to the crisis in leadership by electing new leaders. Thanks to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , we reopened our schools, brought back our businesses, and restored our faith in the American can-do spirit. Thanks to Joe and Kamala, we reduced the price of prescription drugs, repaired roads and bridges, and replaced lead pipes.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMES CLYBURN (DEM) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Thanks to Joe and Kamala, we honored our heroes in uniform, and expanded benefits to over a million veterans. Thanks to Joe and Kamala, (inaudible) in American is no longer just a slogan, but a movement that is bringing millions of manufacturing jobs back to America. For President Biden's lifetime of achievement, his service of his country, we owe him a great debt of gratitude. And we are all grateful for one of the best decisions he made, selecting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as his vice president and endorsing her to succeed him.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMES CLYBURN (DEM) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 I often say that we are but the sum of our experiences. In the introduction to my memoir, I wrote, "all my experiences have not been pleasant, but I consider all of them to be blessings." So has been the case with Kamala. Her experiences have prepared her for this moment. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a true, battle tested leader, a district attorney, attorney general, senator, and vice president, who gets things done. While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been bragging about how he overturned Roe, Kamala has been fiercely advocating for the (inaudible) of reproduction freedoms.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMES CLYBURN (DEM) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 White Trump has been looking out for himself, and his billionaire buddies, Kamala has been fighting to lower costs for all Americans. And while Trump falsely pleads ignorance of Project 2025, which in my opinion, is Jim Crow 2.0, Kamala has been offering the American people enlightened proposals and visionary leadership. Having grown up in a parsonage, I often look to the good book for understanding and guidance. As II Corinthians informs, "we are troubled on every side, yet not distressed. We are perplexed, but not in despair, persecuted, but not forsaken. Cast down, but not destroyed." Our great democracy has been tested.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMES CLYBURN (DEM) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 And so has the basic goodness of the American people. But our resolve to remain a great country with freedom and justice for all, will not falter. We will continue our march toward a more perfect union. United in our common purpose, and emboldened by our resolve to elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  as the next president and vice president of these United States of America. Thank you and God speed.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , South Carolina. John Karl, he was so instrumental in getting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  elected to the presidency during that primary. You remember when it seemed things were not going well for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He turned it around for him.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't become president without \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 . I mean, \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  is the one that made Biden's victory in that Democratic primary possible. Biden came in, what, fourth in Iowa, fifth in New Hampshire. He was done as a candidate, and Clyburn endorsed him in the next primary state of South Carolina, Clyburn's home state, and it was the endorsement that mattered.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And when all the talk began, would \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stay in this race, \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  was continuously pressed, will you support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  if she's the top of the ticket. He was so careful with how he answered that question, but he would hint that absolutely, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  agrees, if this is his idea, I'll be with her. Here he is tonight.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Clyburn was doing two things. He was absolutely standing by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . 100%. But even as he was doing that, making clear that if, for whatever reason Biden would be forced out, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was not just his choice, but the only alternative, if Biden were to be out of the race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, 17 minutes past 10:00 in the East in primetime, ABC News live coverage of the Democratic National Convention, night number one here. We want to bring everybody up to speed at home, who might just be joining us for the 10:00 hour. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did appear here for the first time, at the top of the 9:00 hour. I just wanted to show you the thunderous applause that she received when she walked into the hall for the first time. There has been much said about how, there's \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , her choice for running mate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) More excited than anybody in the room, perhaps, hoping that she is his future boss, hope that they are elected. That's certainly his hope here in the hall. We'll be hearing from him later in the week. But coming into this convention hall, and the very first thing she did, in addition to smiling, and the joy that she brought to the room, a lot has been written about how she hopes to bring joy to this campaign, as she thanks the President of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And I want to kick us off by celebrating our incredible President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Who will be speaking later tonight. Joe, thank you for your historic leadership, for your lifetime of service to our nation, and for all you will continue to do. We are forever grateful to you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The chants here in Chicago, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe. And then this is what the Vice President said, in looking up at all the faces here in Chicago. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I see the beauty of our great nation, people from every corner of our country, and every walk of life are here. United by our shared vision for the future of our country, and this November, we will come together and declare with one voice as one people, we are moving forward, with optimism, hope, and faith, so guided by our love of country, knowing we all have so much more in common than what separates us. Let us fight for the ideals we hold dear, and let us always remember, when we fight, we win. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we fight, we will win, that is her refrain on the campaign trail. We heard it night one of the convention, Linsey Davis, and we'll see her when she gives her speech here Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, what I'm struck by is there's such a delicate dance by every single person who's gotten up there, who has basically given a nod to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the past and the present, and also then turning the page toward the future. We've heard it basically with every speaker, with, in particular, with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . She said, first, let's salute President Biden and now, we are writing a new chapter. And with regard to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , in particular, while she was never able to break through that highest, hardest ceiling, in a way, she did normalize the idea for seeing a woman at the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when you're thinking about the history of this moment, and you and I were discussing earlier, the idea of, of 1968, when the protestors were chanting, "the whole world is watching," fast forward 56 years later, the whole world is still watching here in Chicago, but for a much different reason. As you have the Democratic party rallying behind the first black and Asian woman for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In many ways, a very unified Democratic party, they've stolen some rules from the Republican playbook in how unified they have been from the moment President Biden decided to bow out of this race. You talk about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in many ways normalizing to have a woman at the top of the ticket. The applause in this room, she had to wait a long time before she could actually start talking here tonight...\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't think she minded.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I don't think she did either. It was eight years in the making, waiting to come back. And she did here tonight. Jamie Raskin, Congressman Jamie Raskin's on the floor, he said, welcome to the future of democracy convention. Let's just dip in for a moment, and hear what he's saying.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 ...raised by this sore loser, who does not know how to take no for an answer, from American voters, American courts, or American women. Remember what the mob chanted as they stormed the Capitol, and injured our officers? Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Someone should have told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that the President's job under Article II of the Constitution, someone should have told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that the President's job under Article II of the Constitution is to take care that the laws are faithfully executed, not that the vice president is executed.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 Pence has now joined more than two dozen officials from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's own administration in denouncing him, an historical record, and Pence is the first vice president in more than two centuries not to support the president he served with in a general election. And by the way, J.D. Vance, do you understand why there was a sudden job opening for running mate on the GOP ticket? They tried to kill your predecessor. They tried to kill him, because he would not follow Trump's plan to destroy and nullify the votes of millions of Americans. Your votes, Pennsylvania. Your votes, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 Your votes, Georgia, Nevada, Arizona. Are we gonna go back to the days of election suppression and violent insurrection?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 No. (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 We're not going back. (Inaudible) one week after that "beautiful day," as Trump calls it, the Republicans, ten Republicans joined all of the Democrats to impeach Trump for the worst high crime and misdemeanor ever committed by a president, inciting insurrection against our own Constitution. Make no mistake, a man who uses fraud, theft, and violence to take power, will commit any crime to keep it.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 And now, Trump's promising to pardon hundreds of his fellow criminal convicts, and insurrectionists. He calls for, quote, the "termination of all rules, regulations and articles," even those in the Constitution. My friends, are we the people going to let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  terminate our constitution and obliterate the blessings of liberty for our posterity?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 REP JAMIE RASKIN (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 That's right, we're going to elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We're gonna elect the career prosecutor, and inspiring public servant, and Coach Walz, and we're going to defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the career criminal, and incorrigible recidivist conman and his pet chameleon J.D. Vance. And America, let's make it a landslide so big that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his kangaroo court, Supreme Court justices cannot even try to steal it. Let's make every vote count, let's make sure every vote is counted, let's let freedom ring, and let's let democracy work in America.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Congressman Jamie Raskin, Maryland, of course, four term congressman, member of the House Committee investigating January 6th, making the case for the Constitution, for democracy moving forward, and making the case for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let's go down to the floor and Terry Moran, and the energy on this night one of the convention, a night of thanking President Biden. And of really passing the torch. We're gonna be hearing from President Biden here shortly. We heard from Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , a former first lady, former senator, of course. This is quite a symbolic night here in the hall.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about it, David. This is a night of passing the torch, and it's the emotions on the floor, after the tumultuous several weeks, you know, the two words that you hear again and again, offered to you voluntarily by delegates, joy, and fight. And they are overjoyed. What this party has gone through, it seemed almost impossible. Could have gone sideways really fast. They had a crisis, they pushed their candidate out, who also happens to be the President of the United States, turned on a dime, coalesced around the new candidate, and feel carried on that energy.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The degree of difficulty of that is extraordinary. And divided parties lose, they are coming together here, and you can feel that, feel that joy, feel that determination to fight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's dip back into the stage. Congresswoman Jasmine Crockett of Texas. Terry, thank you. Comparing what she believes are two very different resumes of the candidates.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 ...while he became a career criminal. With 34 felonies, two impeachments, and one porn star to prove it. Her entire career as an elected district attorney, attorney general, and senator, she's always worked for one client, the people. Meanwhile, he's a 78 year old lifelong predator, fraudster, and cheat, known for inciting violent mobs. Listen, y'all, he's only looked out for one person, himself. As women are dying, he is bragging about overturning Roe. And y'all know I come from Texas, and right now in Texas, come on, Texas. But right now, in Texas, they want to institute the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 That is a problem. While \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is fighting for our reproductive rights to be restored. She is also the leader we need on the global stage. She helped secure the release of Americans wrongfully detained in Russia. at the same time, he cozies up to his role model, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and MAGA holds legislation hostage, here at home. Critical resources to secure the border, military aid to Ukraine, and even the Farm Bill. She has lived the American dream, while he's been America's nightmare.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 America, looking at the two choices before you, who would you hire? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah!\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a resume. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a rap sheet. She presides over the Senate, while he keeps our national secrets next to his thinking chair. Y'all know what I said that other time. In Mar-a-Lago. While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to put our 1787 constitution through his Project 2025 paper shredder, and make every day January 6th, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is fighting to fulfill the promise of America. In the real world, this wouldn't even be close. But this election is.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Don't make a mistake. We are the underdogs in this fight, even though there is only one person qualified. Only one person who's done the work, and who has delivered the results. And she needs you. She needs your one vote, this November. Can we count on you?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Some of you know a little bit of my history, some of you don't, so let me tell you. I was a public defender. I did criminal defense as well as practice civil rights law. For almost two decades. I know a good prosecutor when I see one. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the kind of prosecutor we long for in the cases like those of Breonna Taylor. Yeah. She was the first attorney general in the nation to order that her officers wear body cams, and she started the Back on Track program to reduce recidivism.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Listen, y'all, she did all these things because she genuinely cares about people. She sees each person as just that, a person, not a statistic. She's proven that since the first day she stepped into a courtroom, and said what y'all already heard Hillary say. I did not copy off of her speech. I just want y'all to know. She walked into that courtroom and said, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , for the people. And she meant that. Many of you know her credentials, but what I love about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  goes beyond her resume.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Is that she sees the humanity in everyone. She's the only candidate in this race who is capable of empathy. When I first got to Congress, I wasn't sure I made the right decision. The chaos caucus couldn't elect a speaker, and the Oversight Committee was unhinged. I was going through all of this, when I visited the Vice President's residence for the first time. As I approached Vice President Harris for our official photo, she turned to me and asked, what's wrong?\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Mind you, we've never met. But she saw right through me. She saw the distress. I immediately began crying. And the most powerful woman in the world wiped my tears and listened. It's so hard for me to tell this story. She then said, among other things, you are exactly where God wants you. Your district chose you, because they believe in you, and so do I. (inaudible) The next month, I went viral for the first of many times to come. For hitting Republicans with a dose of their own medicine.\par \par \uc2 REP JASMINE CROCKETT (DEM) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 That brief but impactful interaction gave me my legislative legs, and I've been running ever since. The question before us is, will a vindictive, vile, villain violate voters' vision, for a better America or not? I hear alliterations are back in style. We deserve better. We deserve a president who can be a bright light in a sea of darkness, one who will put us, who will pull us forward, because we won't go back. Amanda Gorman said it best. "There's always light if only we are brave enough to see it. If only we are brave enough to be it." \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  showed me that light. And America, when she is our president, together we will shine, as that beacon of hope and freedom around the world once more. God bless y'all.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Resounding applause here in the hall for Congresswoman Jasmine Crockett of Texas, who told a personal story that I hadn't heart before, during a visit to the Vice President's residence, outside Washington. She says that in approaching the Vice President for a photograph, that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said, what's wrong? And she said, we had never met before, I hadn't shared with her anything, and she could see right through me, in her words.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think that so much of the world knows, or this country in particular, knows \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  the district attorney, the prosecutor, the attorney general, but they don't have a sense of that, as her stepkids call her, that Mamala, right? That caretaker. And so I think for her to tell that, that personal story, we got beyond maybe the politician, or that public persona, of what she's like when no one's looking.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And for Congressman Crockett to actually tear up herself, I think that is the intent of tonight. I think so far they're starting to deliver on that, is personifying \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who is she as nurturer. And many people have described that and now they're doing it in a very public way here in front of the nation tonight. I want to go down to the floor, Rachel Scott down on the floor, because Rachel, you have covered reproductive rights as an issue this presidential campaign. And this is where they're making a turn here tonight. We're about to hear from women across this country who have been affected in a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. And Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been the leading voice on the issue of reproductive rights. And so tonight, Democrats, using their platform, their biggest platform yet of the year, to bring this issue front and center. You're going to hear from women in just a few moments here, who will paint a picture of what is playing out across the country in the 22 states that have banned or severely restricted access to abortion. David, I've had a chance to talk to a few of these women. Amanda Zurawski, who was denied an emergency abortion. She ended up getting sepsis. Kaitlyn Joshua, as well. (Inaudible) mentioned, this all comes down to freedom of choosing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's listen to Kaitlyn Joshua and her husband Landon (sic).\par \par \uc2 JOSH ZURAWSKI (AFFECTED BY TEXAS ABORTION BAN)\par \par \uc2 ...100% certainly we would lose our baby girl, Willow, and we were sent home. For three days, we waited until Amanda was sick enough to receive standard abortion care. Eventually, Amanda's temperature spiked, she was shaking, disoriented, and crashing. I don't remember what I threw into our bag that day, only that instead of welcoming Willow, I was hoping Amanda's life could be saved. I'm here tonight because the fight for reproductive rights isn't just a woman's fight. This is about fighting, this is about fighting for our families, and as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says, our future.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI (AFFECTED BY TEXAS ABORTION BAN)\par \par \uc2 Every time I share our story, my heart breaks. For the baby girl we wanted desperately, for the doctors and nurses who couldn't help me deliver her safely. For Josh, who feared he would lose me too. But I was lucky, I lived. So I'll continue sharing our story, standing with women and families across the country. Today, because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , more than one in three women of reproductive age in America lives under an abortion ban. A second Trump term would rip away even more of our rights.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI (AFFECTED BY TEXAS ABORTION BAN)\par \par \uc2 Passing a national abortion ban, letting states monitor pregnancies, and prosecute doctors, restricting birth control and fertility treatments. We cannot let that happen. We need to vote as if lives depend on it, because they do.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA (AFFECTED BY LOUISIANA ABORTION BAN)\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, my husband and I were expecting our second child. Our daughter, Lauryn, couldn't wait to be a big sister. I was getting ready for her fourth birthday party, when something didn't feel right. Two emergency rooms sent me away, because of Louisiana's abortion ban, no one would confirm that I was miscarrying. I was in pain, bleeding so much, my husband feared for my life. No woman should experience what I endured, but too many have.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA (AFFECTED BY LOUISIANA ABORTION BAN)\par \par \uc2 They write to me saying, what happened to you, happened to me. Sometimes they're miscarrying, sacred to tell anyone, even their doctors. Our daughters deserve better. America deserves better. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will fight for reproductive rights and our freedom, and our shared future.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY DUVALL (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Growing up, I was an all-American girl. Varsity soccer captain, cheerleading captain, homecoming queen and survivor. I was raped by my stepfather, after years of sexual abuse. At age 12, I took my first pregnancy test, and it was positive. That was the first time I was ever told, you have options. I can't imagine not having a choice. But today, that's the reality for many women and girls across the country, because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY DUVALL (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 He calls is a beautiful thing. What is so beautiful about a child having to carry her parent's child? There are other survivors out there who have no options. And I want you to know that we see you. We hear you. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will sign a national law to restore the right to an abortion. She will fight for every woman and every girl, even those who are not fighting for her.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY DUVALL (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 And now, I am honored to introduce another champion for women. A leader who's fought for me, and for so many others. Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky. He was considered on the short list for a potential running mate for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . A rare Democrat governor in the South. Winning reelection in his state, and campaigning on abortion rights for women in his state, a deeply red state, and winning. Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Andy, Andy, Andy, Andy, Andy.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (DEM) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 Hadley Duvall is one of the bravest people I've ever met. I'm amazed at the courage it takes to share her pain, to share her truth. Yet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brags about tearing a constitutional right away from Hadley and every other woman and girl in our country. That's why we must tear away any chance he can ever be president ever again. In 21 states, Trump's actions have resulted in extreme bans on abortion. In my state, even in cases of rape, incest, and nonviable pregnancies. That leaves 12 year old girls like Hadley with no options.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (DEM) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 That fails any test of humanity, any test of basic decency, any test of whether you have any underlying empathy. Thankfully, this extremism is being soundly rejected all over our country. In Kentucky, we put reproductive freedom on the ballot last November, and I beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's handpicked candidate by more than five percentage points. This November, we're going to beat them again. Elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , and protect reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (DEM) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 Folks, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed the Supreme Court justices who got us into this mess. His Project 2025 goes even further. Here's the thing, Trump and Vance simply don't believe in your freedom. Trumps says people are absolutely thrilled that women have their basic rights eliminated. J.D. Vance says women should stay in violent marriages, and that pregnancies resulting from rape are simply inconvenient. Their policies give rapists more rights than their victims. That's not inconvenient, it's just plain wrong.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (DEM) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 And a women grieving a nonviable pregnancy shouldn't be required to carry it to term just to listen to her child die, or to hear no sound at all. All women should have the freedom to make their own decisions. Freedom over their own bodies, freedom about whether to pursue IVF, freedom about whether to have children at all. How we treat people transcends party lines. It goes right to the heart of who we are. My faith teaches me the golden rule, that I am to love my neighbor as myself.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (DEM) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 And the parable of the Good Samaritan says we are all each other's neighbors. So I want anyone watching tonight, Republican, Independent, Democrat, to know that you are welcome here. We believe in an America where we live out our values, end anger politics once and for all, and move beyond this us versus them by remembering we are all Americans. That's how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  lead. They both called to ask how they could help Kentucky in recovering from natural disasters. They helped us improve our roads, our bridges, and invested in our people.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (DEM) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 They didn't ask me who Kentuckians voted for. They asked me what Kentuckians needed, and folks, they delivered. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets it. She knows we must move beyond anger, extremism and division. That everyone has dignity and deserves respect. America, we're going to win. And we're gonna win by staying true to our values of compassion, empathy, and doing right by our neighbors. I'm so proud to be all in for the next president of the United States, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky. John Karl, he's had quite a few weeks in the national spotlight. Known in the South and known after natural disasters, we've covered him and how he's handled it. But he's had a front row seat here, and the country has to him as well, as a potential running mate for her for a time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And just think what he's accomplished. Kentucky's a state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won in 2016 by about 30 points. He won it again in 2020 by more than 25 points. Beshear elected twice, and as you heard, reelected, with a five point commanding victory.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Two elections for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  as well in Georgia. The senator from Georgia, of course, a key battleground.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Well.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Warnock, Warnock, Warnock, Warnock.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Hello, America.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Warnock, Warnock, Warnock, Warnock, Warnock, Warnock.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so very much. On January 5th, 2021, the people of Georgia did an amazing thing. They sent a black kid who grew up in public housing, and the Jewish son of an immigrant, to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in one fell swoop. And among those Georgians, was my then 82 year old mother. She grew up in Waycross, Georgia. Do you know where that is? It's way across Georgia. She grew up in Waycross, Georgia where she picked other people's cotton. And other people's tobacco. But because this is America, the 82 year old hands that used to pick somebody else's cotton and somebody else's tobacco, picked her youngest son to be a United States senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 This is my America. Thank you, Mom, thank you, Georgia, thank you, America for raising your voice and using your vote. A vote is a kind of prayer for the world we desire for ourselves and for our children, and our prayers are stronger when we pray together. And so together, together we flipped the Senate, held the House, and we sent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to the White House. Together. Together we vaccinated our citizens, we fortified our cities and our towns, and we stood by our small businesses. Together we set out to heal the land.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 A nation besieged by a deadly pandemic, and beset by the awful and divisive rhetoric of a man too small for the office entrusted him, or the task set before him. The day after my January 5th election, he instigated an insurrection. A violent assault on our nation's Capital and the peaceful transfer of power. All driven by the big lie. But behind the big lie was an even bigger lie. It is the lie that this increasingly diverse American electorate does not get to determine the future of the country. The lie and the logic of January 6th is a sickness. It is a kind of cancer that then metastasized into dozens of voter suppression laws all across our country.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 And we must be vigilant tonight, because these antidemocratic forces are at work right now in Georgia and all across our country, and the question is, who will heal the land? And so here we are, America, are you ready?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Are you ready to stand up in this moral moment?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Stand up for the best in the American covenant.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Elections are about the character of a country. And we must decide again we are the latest generation of Americans who get to decide what kind of country we want to be. And we must choose between the promise of January 5th, and the peril of January 6th. A nation that embraces, a nation that embraces all of us, or just some of us. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's America is the America of January 6th. People who have no vision traffic in division. He does not know how to lead us, and so he wants to divide us. America, make no mistake, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a plague on the American conscience.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 He is - he is a clear and present threat to the precious covenant we share with one another. And yes, I - saw him, I saw him holding the Bible, and endorsing a Bible as if it needed his endorsement. He should try reading it. It says do justice, love kindness, and walk humbly with your God. He should try reading it, it says love your neighbor as yourself. It says in as much as you have done unto the least of these, you have done it also unto me. I choose the American covenant.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 E pluribus unum, out of many, one. I choose January 5th, I choose a nation that provides a path for ordinary people and gives every child a chance. And that's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's America. And he's been fighting for it for more than a half century. President Biden, America is so much better because of you, a true patriot who has always put the people first. Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you something else. Not only is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's America, that's \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's America. She was leading with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , when we expanded the child tax credit, cutting child poverty in America nearly in half. We ought to renew it. She cast a tie breaking vote for my bill capping the cost of insulin to no more than $35 per month for seniors. We ought to extend it to everybody. Together we passed an infrastructure bill, bolstered American manufacturing and clean - energy, an investment in the house that we all share together. And I've got news for you, we are just getting started. Are you ready to win this election?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  represent the new way forward. We're not going back. We're not going back because we are the United States of America. We always dream about the future. And so forward, on women's reproductive rights, because we believe that a patient's room is too small and cramped a space for a woman, her doctor, and the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . That's too many people in the room. Forward, forward on worker rights, because most people do not mind working, they just want to share in the prosperity that they are creating for others.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Forward on voting rights, forward on affordable housing and access to health care. We are moving forward. And so I'm inspired tonight. I'm inspired by all of you. I'm inspired by the resilience of an American spirit, that has rebounded from the pandemic, and is holding at bay the forces that are trying to divide us, and I'm inspired tonight by the memory of my late father, a preacher and a junk man. Monday through Friday, he lifted old, broken cars, and put them on the back of an old rig.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 But on Sunday morning, the man who lifted broken cars, lifted broken people whom other people had discarded, and told them that they were God's somebody. My dad discovered strength in the broken places, a power made perfect in weakness. And so I'm convinced tonight that we can lift the broken, even as we climb. I'm convinced tonight that we can heal sick bodies, we can heal the wounds that divide us, we can heal a planet in peril. We can heal the land. And in a strange way, in a strange way, the pandemic taught us how.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 A contagious air borne disease means that I have a personal stake in the health of my neighbor. If she's sick, I may get sick also. Her health care is good for my health. I'm just trying to tell you that we are as close in our humanity as a cough. I need my neighbor's children to be okay, so that my children will be okay. I need all of my neighbors' children to be okay, poor, inner city children in Atlanta, and poor children of Appalachia, I need the poor children of Israel and the poor children of Gaza, I need Israelis and Palestinians, I need those in the Congo, those in Haiti, those in Ukraine, I need American children on both sides of the track to be okay.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Because we are all God's children. And so let's stand together. Let's work together. Let's organize together. Let's pray together, let's stand together, let's heal the land. God bless you. Keep the faith.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  of Georgia. Georgia senator, won that key swing state twice in just a short period of time. Senator Chris Coons of Delaware, good friend of President Biden's, occupies the seat that then Senator Biden had for many years, Mary.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He is one of his closest friends. We saw him emotional in the days after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decided to bow out. This is a difficult moment for him.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 ...Good evening, Chicago. Are there any Democrats in the room tonight? Folks, we are on the verge of making history. We are on the verge of an historic election. And I want to talk with you for just a few minutes, about my dear friend, our president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's mom had a saying. You are defined by your courage, and you are redeemed by your loyalty. Four years ago, I had the honor of helping introduce \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to our national convention. I spoke about Joe's faith, his love of family, his determination to restore the soul of our nation.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 Back in 2020, American was nearly flat on our back, from a deadly pandemic, an economic collapse, and then in January, an assault on our democracy. All of this, all of this because the drama and chaos of our former president had knocked us down. But folks, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's courage, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's faith in us, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's determination to heal the soul of our nation gave us hope and gave us confidence. Someone who has himself been knocked down by life time and again. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knows the grit and determination of the American people.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 He believed in us, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  helped us get back up again. Using the incredible skills he'd developed in many years in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . Joe got passed and signed into law the most consequential legislation of any president in 60 years. Helping our veterans, advancing gun safety, cutting prescription drug prices, fighting climate change, rebuilding bridges and broadband. Bringing manufacturing back to America. Together, Joe and Kamala helped rebuild our economy, from the middle up, from the bottom up, and the middle out, not from the top down.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 And they made our families safer, and our country stronger, at home and abroad. Today, we are so much better off, because they believed in us, in our democracy. And our incredible First Lady Jill Biden, our incredible First Lady Jill Biden, our incredible First Lady Jill Biden, was at Joe's side every step of the way. Fighting for education, advancing the cancer moonshot, determined to improve women's health care, honoring military families. Joe and Jill together, you have done so much for us, and there's so much more you will do, and we can do together.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, thank you, thank you, for elevating a great leader in \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Vice President Harris, thank you, for choosing a great running mate, with a heart for the middle class like yours, in Sergeant, Congressman, Coach, Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . I have hope, do you?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 I have hope, do you?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 I have hope, do you?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 And it is in no small part because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I have never known a more compassionate man than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I have never known a man who has taken from his own loss and his own faith and delivered so much for the future of so many others. Mr. President, Mr. President, you were my senator as a Delawarean. You are my president as an American. And you will always be my friend. On behalf of our nation, Joe, for your courage in fighting for our democracy, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 On behalf of our Democratic party, for your loyalty in fighting for our Democratic values, we thank you. And on behalf of Delaware, thank you, Joe, and God bless you. We love you. We love Joe. We love Joe. We love Joe.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (DEM) (DELAWARE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you all. We love Joe. God bless you and thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator Chris Coons of Delaware, very emotional in the days following President Biden's decision to bow out of the race. The President behind that stage, ready to come out and speak to what he believes are his accomplishments of his term as president. Now there are, there's a video playing, telling the story of Dr. Jill Biden. Of course, married to the President for 47 years. Raised two sons with him, from his first marriage, of course, that ended tragically when his wife was in a car accident, losing his wife and their baby.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Married 47 years. They have a daughter, Ashley, together. She raised Hunter and Beau as their own sons, and she was right by the President inside, John Karl, she was at Camp David after that very difficult debate, when they still didn't know how they would proceed, whether or not the President would stay in the race or not. She has said publicly that I would - have supported either decision that the President made, but that it was his decision to make.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that was the family position. Look, in this moment, where all this pressure came to bear on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from his own party, people saying he needed to get out of the race. The inner circle became the family, the Biden family. His daughter, his son, his wife, and a very small group of aides. And there she is. She was perhaps more important than any other person, but she was not, as some reported, saying he had to stay in. She was saying, I support you no matter what you decide.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I stand by you, no matter what the decision is. Let's listen to the First Lady, Jill Biden, who of course was in the Oval Office when her President, her husband addressed the nation. Now she stands here tonight, for her husband and for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Jill, Jill, Jill, Jill, Jill. We love you.\par \par \uc2 FIRST LADY DR. JILL BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Thank you so much, love you too. Joe and I have been together for almost 50 years. And still, there are moments when I fall in love with him all over again. Like when I handed him our baby, Ashley, for the first time, and saw the smile that lit up his face. Or on nights after an exhausting day working in the Senate, when he would read one more bedtime story, just because the kids asked. When he stops on a rope line, because he sees someone grieving, who needs to know that everything is going to be all right one day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE BIDEN FAMILY\par \par \uc2 FIRST LADY DR. JILL BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Or to encourage that child with a stutter to find the confidence she needs. Those moments when I'm reminded of all he's accomplished in the name of something bigger than himself, receiving the Medal of Freedom with humility. Placing his hand on our family Bible to take his oath of office. And weeks ago, when I saw him dig deep into his soul, and decide to no longer seek reelection, and endorse \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . With faith and conviction, Joe knows that our nation's strength doesn't come from intimidation or cruelty, it comes from the small acts of kindness that heal deep wounds.\par \par \uc2 FIRST LADY DR. JILL BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 From service to the communities that make us who we are, from love of a country that shines with promise and renewal. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  knows that too. Our son Beau first worked with Kamala when he was attorney general of Delaware. He told me at the dinner table one night, Mom, she's special. Someone to keep your eye on. And he was right. Joe and I know Kamala. We have seen her courage, her determination, and her leadership up close. Kamala and Tim, you will win. And, and you are inspiring a new generation.\par \par \uc2 FIRST LADY DR. JILL BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We are all a part of something bigger than ourselves, and we are stronger than we know. The future of our country is in the hands of those in this room and all of you watching at home. It's going to take all of us. And we can't afford to lose. With faith in each other, hope for a brighter future, and love for our country, we will fight, and we will win, together. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The First Lady Jill Biden, speaking about her husband, saying they've been married almost 50 years, and that there are still times, she says, when she falls in love with him all over again. Talking about those poignant moments along the way, handing him their baby Ashley. To his painstaking decision just a couple weeks back, to decide to bow out of the race. Here is that baby, by the way, all these years later, Ashley Biden now coming to the podium to introduce her father, for what will be one of his probably final speeches to an audience of this size. Let's listen to Ashley.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I have this memory, it's the eve of my eight birthday. Dad is still in DC, tending to urgent matters in the Senate. That night, as a surprise, Mom - told me, Beau, and Hunter to get in the car. I remember pulling up to the Wilmington Amtrak station, riding up the escalator to the platform. The train stops, doors open, and Dad steps out. As soon as I saw him, I run down that platform and jump into his arms. Like magic, Mom brought out a cake, they sang "Happy Birthday," and I blew out the candles. Dad hugged me, and he said that he had to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 He crossed to the southbound train, and off he went to DC. That was a snapshot of one moment of one day on this extraordinary journey of being \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's daughter. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the OG girl dad. He told me I could be anything, and I could do anything. As a child, I would sit on the leather chair in his office doing my homework, and he would sit next to me, doing his work, drafting the Violence Against Women Act. And he wasn't just a girl dad.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 ) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 I could see, and he wasn't just a girl dad, I could see that he valued and trusted women, how he listened to his mother, how he believed in his sister. And most of all, how he respected my mother's career. Dad was always there doing everything he could to be a true partner to her. Dad, you always tell us, but we don't tell you enough, that you are the love of our lives, and the life of our love. I had my wedding reception in my parents' backyard. At the time, my dad was Vice President.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 But he was also that dad, who literally set up the entire reception. He was riding around in his John Deere four wheeler, fixing the place settings, arranging the plants, and by the way, he was very emotional. I thought that I would be a mess, but he was the one crying. And I was the one who had to comfort him. Before he walked me down the aisle, he turned to me and said that he would always be my best friend. All these years later, Dad, you are still my best friend.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 His example in service inspired my career. I'm a social worker in Philadelphia. I support formerly incarcerated women as they heal from past trauma, and they reclaim their lives. Dad always told me that I was no better than anybody else, and nobody was better than me. He taught me that everyone deserves a fair shot, and that we shouldn't leave anyone behind. That's what you learn from a fighter, who has been underestimated his entire life. When I look at Dad, I see grace, strength, and humility.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 I see one of the most consequential leaders ever in history. And I also know that he never stops thinking about you. About your dreams, about your dignity, about your opportunities, about your family. Dad knows that family is everything. When Hunter and I lost our brother Beau to cancer in 2015, the grief and the pain felt like it might never end. Dad had the capacity to step out of his own pain and absorb ours. And I know that Beau is here with us tonight as he is always with us.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 After Beau passed, I got this tattoo on my wrist. It says courage, dear heart. A reminder to myself to keep going, to get back up, like my dad has always done. He has taught me that a courageous heart is a miraculous thing, a courageous heart can heal a family. A courageous heart can heal a nation, and maybe even the world. And now this election requires the courageous hearts of all of us. In 2020, my dad selected \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he knows in 2024, she will beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So tonight, I am asking you, if you stood with us in 2020, call upon your courageous heart, stand with us today, work harder than you have ever worked before in your life. This is the fight of our lifetime. Our freedom, our democracy, our reproductive rights. All of this, all of it is on the ballot. And I know together, we can do this. Because my dad helped show us the way.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BIDEN (DAUGHTER OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And now, I would like to introduce my father, your 46th President of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ashley Biden, with a very heartfelt introduction, saying her father is still her best friend after all these years. Embracing her father. And tears from the President. Mary, you cover this family, you cover the White House. It's impossible to overstate what they must have been through these last four weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is an incredibly emotional and bittersweet moment for this family. I just keep thinking, David, a month ago, the President thought he was gonna walk out onto this stage at his own convention. Now he is here to turn over that baton, to pass the torch, and begin winding down his own 50 year plus career in politics. He is going to, of course, make clear that he now thinks - \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the future of this party.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's gonna try and cement his legacy. A big part of that, of course, is now making sure that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is elected the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) An extraordinary reception here in the United Center in Chicago for President Biden. Again, even a month and a half ago, they were preparing for a Biden convention, John Karl. He's now here tonight for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He is, and you heard Mary say it, his legacy depends on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  winning this election.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, thank you, thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. I love you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, thank you, thank you. That was my daughter.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe. Thank you, Kamala, thank you, Kamala, thank you, Kamala.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, thank you, thank you. Thank you. Thank you - I tell you what. To my dearest daughter, Ashley. God love you, you're incredible. Thank you for that introduction, and for being my courageous heart, along with Hunter, and our entire family. And especially our rock, Jill, who as those of you who know us, she still leaves me both breathless and speechless. Everybody knows her, I love her more than she loves me. She walks down the stairs and I still get that going, boom, boom, boom, boom. You all who know me know I'm not kidding. Let's give a special round of applause to our First Lady Jill Biden.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 My dad, my dad used to have an expression, for real, he's say, Joey, family is the beginning, the middle, and the end. And I love you all. Folks, and America, I love you. Folks, let me ask you.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you. Are you ready to vote for freedom?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah!\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Are we ready to vote for democracy and for America?\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah!\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you, are you ready to elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah!\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 ...President and Vice President of the United States? My fellow Democrats, my fellow Americans, nearly four years ago, in winter, on the steps of the Capitol, on a cold, January day, I raised my right hand, and I swore an oath to you and to God, to preserve, protect and defend the Constitution, and to faithfully execute the office of the President of the United States. In front of me, in front of me was a city surrounded by the National Guard. Behind me, a Capitol, just two weeks before, had been overrun by a violent mob.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 But I knew then, from the bottom of my heart, and I do now, there is no place in America for political violence, none. You cannot say you love your country only when you win. In that moment, I wasn't looking to the past, I was looking to the future. I spoke to the work at hand, the moment we had to meet. It was as I told you then, a winter of peril and possibility. Of peril and possibility. We were in the grip of a once in a century pandemic, historic joblessness, a call for racial justice long overdue.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Clear and present threats to our very democracy. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe, we love Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And yet, I believed then and I believe now that progress was and is possible. Justice is achievable, and our best days are not behind us, they're before us. Now it's summer, winter has passed, and with grateful heart, I stand before you now on this August night to report that democracy has prevailed. Democracy, democracy has delivered, and now, democracy must be preserved. You've heard me say it before, we're facing an inflection point. One of those rare moments in history when the decisions we make now will determine the fate of our nation and the world for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 That's not hyperbole, I mean it literally. We're in a battle for the very soul of America. I ran for president in 2020 because of what I saw in Charlottesville in August of 2017. Extremists, coming out of the woods, carrying torches. Their veins bulging from their necks, carrying Nazi swastikas and chanting the same exact anti-Semitic bile that was heard in Germany in the early '30s. Neo-Nazis, white supremacists, the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2 , so emboldened by a president then in the White House, that they saw as an ally.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They didn't even bother to wear their hoods. Hate was on the march in America. Old ghosts in new garments, stirring up the oldest divisions. Stoking the oldest fears. Giving oxygen to the oldest forces, that they long sought to tear apart America. in the process, a young woman was killed. When I contacted her mother, I asked about what happened. She told me. When the President was asked what he thought had happened, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, and I quote, "there are very fine people on both sides." My God. That's what he said.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 That is what he said and what he meant. That's when I realized, (inaudible) to the admonition of my dead son, I could not stay on the sidelines. So I ran. 'Cause I had no intention of running again. I just lost part of my soul, but I ran with a deep conviction, in America, I know and believe in an America where honesty, dignity, decency still matter. An America where everyone has a fair shot, and hate has no safe harbor. An America where the fundamental creed of this nation, that all of us are created equal, is still very much alive.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And a broad coalition of Americans joined with me. 81 million voters voted for us. More than any time in all of history. Because of all of you in this room, and others, we came together in 2020, to save democracy. As your president, I've been determined to keep America moving forward, not going back. To stand against hate and violence in all its forms, to be a nation where we not only live with - but thrive on diversity. Demonizing no one, leaving no one behind. And becoming the nation that we profess to be. I also ran, to rebuild the backbone of America, the middle class.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I made a commitment to you, that I'd be a president for all Americans, whether you voted for me or not. We have done that. Studies show the major bills we have passed actually delivered more to red states than blue, because the job of the president is to deliver to all of America. Because of you, and I'm not exaggerating, because of you, we've had one of the most extraordinary four years of progress ever, period. When I say we, I mean Kamala and me. Just think about it, COVID no longer controls our lives. We've gone from economic crisis to the strongest economy in the entire world.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Record 16 million new jobs, record small business growth, record high stock market, record high 401 (k) s, wages up, and inflation down, way down, and continuing to go down. The smallest racial wealth gap in 20 years. And yes, we both know we have more to do, but we're moving in the right direction. More Americans have peace of mind, that comes from having health insurance. More Americans have health insurance today than ever before in American history. And after, as a young senator beginning to fight, beginning to fight for 50 years to give Medicare the power to negotiate lower prescription drug prices, we finally beat big pharma.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And guess who cast the tie breaking vote? Vice President soon to be President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And now it's the law of the land. Instead of paying $400 a month for insulin, seniors with diabetes will pay 35 a month. The law we passed already included, starting in January, every senior's total prescription cost can be capped at $2,000, no matter how expensive the drugs they have. And what we don't focus on, and our Republican friends don't seem to understand, our reforms don't just save seniors money, they save the American taxpayers money.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You know what we just passed saved? It saved $160 billion over the next decade. That's not hyperbole, it's because Medicare no longer has to pay those exorbitant prices to the big pharma. But look...\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Kamala too. Look, folks, how can we have the strongest economy in the world without the best infrastructure in the world? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised infrastructure week, every week, for four years. And he never built a damn thing. And now because of Kamala I've done, remember we were told we couldn't get it done, remember when we came into office? We couldn't get anything passed. Well right now, we're giving America an infrastructure decade, not week.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're modernizing, our roads, our bridges, our ports, our airports, our trains, our busses. Removing every lead pipe from schools and homes, so every child can drink clean water. Providing affordable high speed internet for every American, no matter where they live, unlike, not unlike what Roosevelt did with electricity. And so much more. We are uniting the country, we're growing our economy. We're improving our quality of life, and we're building a better America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 'Cause that's who we are. How can we be the strongest nation in the world without leading the world in science and technology? After years of importing 90% of our semiconductor chips from abroad, which America invented that, those chips. Our Chips and Science Act meant that private companies from around the world are now investing literally tens of billions of dollars to build new chip factories right here in America. And over that period, they'll create tens of thousands of jobs, and many of those jobs in the so-called fabs, the buildings that makes the chips, are being constructed now.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And guess what? The average salary in those fabs, the size of a football field, will be over $100,000 a year, and you don't need a college degree. Because of you, and so many electeds out there, American manufacturing is back. Where the hell does it say we wouldn't lead the world in manufacturing? 800,000 new manufacturing jobs. Our Republican friends and others made sure they'd go abroad to get the cheapest labor. We used to import products and export jobs. Now we export American products and create American jobs, right here in America, where jobs belong.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 With every new job, with every new factory, pride and hope is being brought back to communities throughout the country that were left behind. You know, and you're from them, many of you. You know what it's like when that factory closed, where your mother, your father, your grandmother, grandfather worked, and now you're back, providing once again, proving that Wall Street didn't build America, the middle class built America, and unions, unions built the middle class.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's been my view since I came to the Senate. That's why I'm proud to have been the first president to walk a picket line. And be labeled the most pro-union president in history, and I accept it. It's a fact. Because when unions do well, we all do well. You've got it, man. You've got it.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I agree, I'm proud. Look, remember we were told we couldn't get anything done because the, couldn't get anything done in the Congress. With your support, we passed the most significant climate law in the history of mankind. Over $370 billion. Cutting carbon emissions in half by 2030. Launching a Climate Corps, similar to AmeriCorps and Peace Corps, training tens of thousands of jobs for young people of the future, who are gonna make sure this continues. Training hundreds of thousands of jobs in clean energy for American workers, including the IBEW installing 500,000, 500,000 charging stations all across America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And in the process, reducing carbon emissions, and we're seeing it, we're seeing to it, that the first beneficiaries of environmental initiatives are those fence lined communities that have been smothered by the legacy of pollution. In Louisiana and Delaware, Road 9, all the factories, all those chemical factories are right next to the poorest neighborhoods. They're the ones we're gonna bring back. And how, how can we be the greatest nation in the world without the best education system in the world?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican friends, they not only can't think, they can't read very well. Seriously, think about it, look at their Project 2025. They want to do away with the Department of Education. Well during the pandemic, Kamala helped states and cities get back, their schools back open. And we gave public school teachers a raise. We created apprenticeships with businesses and communities, putting students on a path to good paying jobs, whether or not they go to college. And by the way, we're making college a hell of a lot more affordable.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Increasing Pell grants by $900. Over $15 billion to HBCUs. Minority (inaudible), including Hispanic institutions and tribal colleges. We kept our commitment to provide more student relief than ever, by lifting the burden of helping millions of families so they can get married, start a family, buy a home, and begin to build family wealth and contribute to the community, and grow our economy. It's not costing us, it's creating more wealth. We fundamentally - transformed how our economy grows, from the middle out, and the bottom up, instead of the top down.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You know, my dad used to say, there wasn't a whole hell of a lot to drop down on my kitchen table at the end of the month. I come from a basic middle class family. Three bedroom house, four kids, a grandpop living with us. Decent neighborhood, but never a penny to spare. And look, that top down notion never worked. A lot of Democrats didn't think it would work - thought it worked, but it doesn't. And when we did all that, what we've done, everybody can do well, everybody.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  calls America a failing nation. No, I'm serious, but think about this, think about this. He publicly says to the whole world, I'm gonna say something outrageous, I know more foreign leaders by their first names and know them well, than anybody alive. That's because I'm so damn old. But I'm not joking, think of the message he sends around the world, when he talks about America being a failing nation. He says we're losing. He's the loser, he's dead wrong.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Many of you are very successful people who've traveled the world. Name me a country in the world that doesn't think we're the leading nation in the world. Without America, not a joke, think about it, I'm being literal, who could lead the world other than the United States of America? Well guess what, America is winning, and the world is better off for it. America is more prosperous, and America is safer today than under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Trump continues to lie about crime in America, like everything else.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Guess what? On his watch, the murder rate went up 30%, the biggest increase in history. Meanwhile, we made the largest investment, Kamala and I, in public safety, ever. Now, the murder rate is falling faster than any time in history. Violent crime has dropped to the lowest level of more than 50 years. And crime will keep coming down, when we put a prosecutor in the Oval Office, instead of a convicted felon. And folks, (inaudible) senator from California and I passed the first ban on assault weapons. And guess what? It worked. If we care about public safety, we need to prevent gun violence.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And what makes me ashamed when I travel the world, which I do, more children in America are killed by a gunshot, than any other cause in the United States, more die from a bullet than cancer, accidents, or anything else. In the United States of America. My God. That's why Kamala and I are proud. We beat the \uc2 NRA\uc2  when we passed the first major bipartisan gun safety bill in 30 years. I'm serious. That comes from here. And now it's time to ban assault weapons again. And demand universal background checks.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's hard. I never thought I'd stand before a crowd of Democrats and refer to a president as a liar so many times. Now, I'm not trying to be funny. It's sad. Trump continues to lie about the border. Here's what he won't tell you. Trump killed the strongest bipartisan border deal in the history of the United States, that we negotiated with the Senate Republicans. It took four- weeks. Once it passed, and everybody acknowledged the most expansive border change in American history, he called senators to say, don't support the bipartisan bill.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 He said it would help me politically, and hurt him politically. My God. I'm serious, think about it, not a joke. Ask even the press who doesn't like me, they'll tell you that's true. Typically Trump, once again, putting himself first and America last. Then I had to take executive action. The result of the executive action I took, border encounters have dropped over 50%. In fact, there are fewer border crossings today than when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left office. And unlike Trump, we will not demonize immigrants, saying they'll poison the blood of America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Poison the blood of our country? Kamala and I are committed to strengthening legal immigration, including protecting DREAMers and more. And here's what else I believe in. Protecting your freedom, your freedom to vote, your freedom to love who you love, and your freedom to choose. And - this decision overturning Roe v. Wade that you heard earlier tonight, \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  majority wrote the following, quote, "women are not without - not without - electoral or political power." No kidding. MAGA Republicans found out the power of women in 2022.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is gonna find out the power of women in 2024. Watch. And where Trump and his MAGA Republican right wingers seek to erase history, we Democrats continue to write history and make more history. I'm proud, I'm proud to have kept my commitment to appoint the first black woman to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , a symbol for every young woman in America, that you can do anything. I'm proud that I've kept my commitment to have an administration that looks like America, and that taps in to the full talent of our nation.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The most diverse Cabinet in history, including the first black woman and South Asian descent to serve as vice president. And will soon serve as the 47th president of the United States. She's good. Look...\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Kamala.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Folks, I've long said, there are many obligations as a nation. I got in trouble years ago for saying I'd make no apologies. We have only one truly sacred obligation, to prepare and equip those we send to war and care for them and their families when they come home, and when they don't. That's why I'm proud to have written and signed the PACT Act, one of the most significant laws ever helping veterans and their families exposed to toxic materials like burn pits and agent orange. I was around during the Vietnam War. It's hard, when nobody's able to prove that there's illness as a consequence of agent orange. And no one was able to prove initially, that because they lived in burn pits, like my son lived next to in Iraq for a year, that it's the cause of their illness. But because of the PACT act, a surviving spouse with two children is now eligible for a stipend about $3,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And those children who lost a parent, are eligible for tuition benefits to go to college and to get job training. It's already helping over one million veterans and their families just so far. I love them, and I'm so proud of my son's service. We get it. But guess who doesn't get it and doesn't respect our veterans. We know from his own chief of staff, the four star General \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , that Trump, when in Europe, would not go to the grave sites in - France of brave service members who gave their life to this country. He called them suckers and losers. Who in the hell does he think he is? Who does he think he is? There's no words for a person, they are not the words of a person, not worthy of being commander in chief, period, not then, not now, and not ever. I mean that. I mean that from the bottom of my heart.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Just as no commander in chief should ever bow down to a - dictator the way Trump bows down to Putin. I never have and I promise you, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will never do it. We'll never bow down. When Trump left office, Europe and \uc2 NATO\uc2  was in tatters. Not a joke. America first doctrine changed our whole image in the world. Well I've spent, they gave the hours, about 190 hours sum total, visiting my counterparts and heads of state in Europe to strengthen \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We did. We united Europe, like it hadn't been united for years, adding Finland and Sweden to \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Ten days before he died, \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2  called and said, not since, not since Napoleon has Europe not looked over their soldier at Russia with dread until now. Until now. Well, guess what? Putin thought he would take Kyiv in three days. Three years later, Ukraine is still free. When I came to office, the conventional wisdom was that China would inevitably surpass the United States. They haven't noticed, no one is saying that now. And we'll keep working to bring hostages home and end the war in Gaza and bring peace and security to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 As you know, I wrote a peace treaty for Gaza. A few days ago, I put forward a proposal that brought us closer to doing that than we've done since October 7th. We're working around the clock, my secretary of state, to prevent a wider war, and reunite hostages with their families, and surge humanitarian health and food assistance into Gaza now. To end the civilian suffering of the Palestinian people. And finally, finally deliver a ceasefire and end this war. Those - protesters out in the street, they have a point. A lot of innocent people are being killed on both sides. Just as we worked around the clock to bring home wrongfully detained Americans and others from Russia, in one of the most complicated swaps in history, but they're home.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Kamala and I are going to keep working to bring all Americans wrongfully detained around the world home. I mean it. Folks, I've got five months left in my presidency. And I've got a lot to do. I intend to get it done. It's been the honor of my lifetime to serve as your president. I love the job, but I love my country more. I love my country more. And all this talk about how I'm angry at all those people who said I should step down, it's not true.\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We love Joe, we love Joe, we love Jo, we love Joe, we love Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I love my country more, and we need to preserve our democracy. In 2024, we need you to vote. We need you to keep the Senate. We need you to win back the House of Representatives. And above all, we need you to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and elect Kamala and Tim president and vice president of the United States of America. Look, they'll continue to lead America forward, creating more jobs, standing up for workers, growing the economy, lower the cost of American families so they just have a little more breathing room. We made incredible - progress, and we have more work to do. And Kamala and Tim will continue to take on corporate greed and bring down cost of food. They'll keep taking on big pharma, making insulin $35 a month not just for seniors but for everyone in America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And capping prescription drug cost at the total of $2,000 not just for seniors but for everyone. And folks, that's going to save America again tens of billions of dollars. Folks, they will make housing more affordable, building three million new homes, providing $25,000 down payment assistance for the first-time home buyer, more than the 10 we approved. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to do tax on imported goods, food, gas, clothing, and more. You know what that will cost the average family according to the experts? $3,900 a year in tax. No, it's a fact. Kamala and Tim will make the Child Care Tax Credit permanent. Lifting millions of children out of poverty and helping millions of families get ahead.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You know what Trump has? He put the - he created the largest debt any president had in four years with his $2 trillion tax cut for the wealthy. Well, Trump has a new plan. He wants to provide a $5 billion tax cut for corporations and the very wealthy, to put, read it, put us further in debt. And folks, you know, we have - 1,000 billionaires in America. You know what their average tax rate they pay? 8.2%. If we just increase their taxes we propose to 25%, which isn't even the highest tax rate even, it would raise 500 billion new dollars over ten years. They would still be very wealthy.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Look, Kamala and Tim are going to make them pay their fair share. They'll protect Social Security and Medicare. Trump wants to cut Social Security and Medicare. Kamala and Tim will protect your freedom. They'll protect your vote to, your right to vote. They'll protect your civil rights. And you know Trump will do everything to ban abortion nationwide. Oh, he will. You know, Kamala and Tim will do everything they possibly can. That's why you have to elect a Senate and a House to restore Roe v. Wade. The ancient Greeks taught us that character is destiny. Character is destiny. For me and Jill, we know Kamala and Doug are people of character. It's been our honor to serve alongside them.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And we know that Tim and Gwen Walz are also people of great character. Selecting Kamala was the very first decision I made before - when I became our nominee, and it was the best decision I made my whole career. We've not only gotten to know each other, we've become close friends. She's tough. She's experienced. And she has enormous integrity, enormous integrity. Her story represents the best American story. And like many of our best presidents, she was also vice president. That's a joke. But she'll be a president our children could look up to.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 She'd be a president respected by world leaders because she already is. She'll be a president we can all be proud of. And she will be a historic president who puts her stamp on America's future. This will be the first presidential election since January 6th. On that day, we almost lost everything as a country. And that threat, this is not hyperbole, but that threat is still very much alive. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he will refuse to accept the election result if he loses again. Think about that. He means it. Think about that. He's promising a blood bath if he loses, in his words, and that he'll be a dictator on day one, in his own words. By the way, this sucker means it. No, I'm not joking. Think about it. Anybody else said that in the past, you'd think he was - he's crazy. But you'd think it was an exaggeration, but he means it. We can't let that happen.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Folks, all of us carry a special obligation. Independents, Republicans, Democrats, we saved democracy in 2020, and now we must save it again in 2024. (Inaudible) cast this year will determine whether democracy and freedom prevail. It's that simple. It's that serious. And the power is literally in your hands. History is in your hands. Not hyperbole, it's in your hands. America's future is in your hands. Let me close with this. Nowhere else in the world could a kid with a stutter and modest beginnings in Scranton, Pennsylvania, and Claymont, Delaware, grow up to sit behind the Resolute Desk in the Oval Office. That's because America is and always has been a nation of possibilities. Possibilities. We must never lose that, never. Kamala and Tim understand that this nation must continue to be a place of possibilities, not just for the few of us but for all of us.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Join me in promising your whole heart to this effort. I know where my heart will be. I promise I'll be the best volunteer Harris and Walz's camp have ever seen. Each of us has a part in the American story. For me and my family, there's a song that means a lot to us. It captures the best of who we are as a nation. The song is called "American Anthem." There's one verse that stands out. And I can't sing worth a damn, so I'm not going to try. I'll just quote it. "The work and prayers of centuries have brought us to this day. What shall our - legacy be? What will our children say? Let me know in my heart when my days are through. America, America, I gave my best to you."\par \par \uc2 RALLY ATTENDEES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Joe. Thank you, joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I made, I made a lot of mistakes in my career, but I gave my best to you. For 50 years, like many of you, I've given my heart and soul to our nation. And I've been blessed a millions times in return with the support of the American people. I went from being too young to be in the Senate, because I wasn't 30 yet, and too old to stay as president. But I hope you know how grateful I am to all of you. I can honestly say, and I mean this from the bottom, I give you my word as a Biden. I can honestly say, I'm more optimistic about the future than I was when I was elected as a 29 year old United States senator. I mean it. Folks, we just have to remember who we are. We're the United States of America. And there's nothing we cannot do when we do it together. God bless you all, and may God protect our troops. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Quite a night and what a way to end night one of the Democratic National Convention here in Chicago. President Biden speaking to this convention hall and speaking to the nation. The First Lady behind him there. He said earlier in the evening, his heart still beats a little faster when she comes down the stairs. And there you see the message of the evening, thank you, Joe. The First Lady waving to the crowd. He pledged to be the best volunteer the Harris/Walz campaign has ever seen. In talking about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , he said, you know, like many of our best presidents, they start as vice president, a little nod to himself there, and a twinkle in his eye.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 He talked about democracy at length in this speech. And there you have it, the Vice President greeting the President, Doug Emhoff, second gentleman with the First Lady. I believe she said, I love you, Joe, the Vice President to the President just there. Reading her lips. And she couldn't have asked for a more sincere endorsement, a fulsome endorsement tonight, Mary.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A full throated endorsement. And this was a classic \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  speech. In many ways it did feel like a lot of the speeches he's been delivering out on the trail in recent weeks, ticking through his accomplishments, but now arguing that it is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , not him, who is best suited to finish this job and take on Trump. Biden saying, I love my job, but I love my country more. And this certainly could not have been easy. You see him there soaking in this moment, well aware that in many ways, that was his swan song, ending with that, quote, "America, I gave you my best."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For 50 years, I gave you my best. And Mary, you're so right. He said, I love my job, but Martha, he said, I love my country more, to applause in this convention hall.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He did indeed. And I have to say, I struck by the fact, this was such an emotional speech for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but he really didn't show any emotion. He was powerful in his campaigning, basically, for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And really the most important thing he did tonight is what we have been talking about all night, for the Democratic Party, what he did that was so important is pass that torch, is give that wholesome, fulsome endorsement to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and speak about optimism. And you see that optimism on that stage tonight. That is the message they wanted to deliver for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tonight and the kind of president they believe she would be.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Over and over again, Linsey, in the hall, that chant, thank you, Joe. And he would interrupt them and he would say, thank you, Kamala.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a moment that he never actually had for himself as a presidential nominee. When you think about the convention in 2020, really at the height of COVID. Most of it was virtual and sparsely attended. So, this was truly meaningful. While I think that he needed also that thank you from the crowd, he realized that he is that transitional candidate, that he promised, that he pledged when he ran in 2020, that he was going to be that bridge. And what a way to pass that torch, when it comes to race, when it comes to age, and when it comes to gender.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very proud, as you mentioned, on issues of race, issues of immigration, crime, education, infrastructure, climate change. He listed them all in what he believes are significant accomplishments of the Biden/Harris administration, John, and proud of his record. And he got that chance tonight in front of the nation to make the case.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He felt a need, I think, this is a valedictory, this is, this will probably be the last major political speech of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's life. And he said that in that kind of windup at the end, where he was quoting the poem, saying, America, I gave my best to you, he felt a need to tick through a lot of it in a lot of detail. I think one of the most important lines in the speech as we go forward is when he said, the best decision I have made in my career was picking \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as my vice president. If she wins, that may well be true.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as you said before, John, that's going to be a big part of his legacy is the outcome of this November election. Let's go down to the floor. Rachel Scott, the energy on the floor tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, I can tell you, right now I am watching people wipe tears from their eyes. It was emotional for President Biden. It was emotional for people who are here as well. The President ending his campaign essentially in the way that he started it, saying that democracy is at stake. And David, you pointed out that moment. You had thousands of people shouting here, thank you, Joe, thank you, Joe. They were holding up those signs, and as he interrupted to say, thank you, Kamala, many in this crowd repeating those words right back to him.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, thank you. Terry Moran, when he said that line, Terry, I gave my best to you, in so many words, this country for 50 years, and you could actually see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Governor Walz and the look on Governor Walz's face, truly moved. That first of all, he's on this ticket, this next Democratic ticket, and listening to President Biden on that stage go over his own record and his devotion to his country, saying, I love my job, but I love my country more.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, it was remarkable, David. It was part of what took place on this floor tonight. Almost a family drama, one generation giving way to another. And when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  came out, this eruption of enthusiasm and joy that the Democratic party has found in her candidacy. But then with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  essentially saying goodbye to this party he's served for more than half a century. But those signs that they were holding up, we love you, Joe, I think a lot of people were discovering just how much.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry Moran on the floor for us, thank you. Donna Brazile, longtime Democratic strategist, ABC News analyst. Donna, going into the night, you said this would be a bittersweet but powerful night for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, David, it wasn't a farewell address. I felt it was a call to action. Not for my generation, but my nieces, my nephews, the little ones who still need a champion, a fighter. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a perfect example what it means to be a public servant in America. He's given all of his life to lifting up people and to try to make this a better country and a better world. So, thank you, Joe. And like most Democrats and most Americans, thank you, Joe, for your service.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna Brazile with us tonight as well. Donna, thank you as always. Tomorrow night, night two of the Democratic National Convention here in Chicago. We're expecting to hear from former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , former first lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  among the other names and our entire team will be right here for you tomorrow night. I'm David Muir. From all of us here at ABC News, thank you for watching. "Nightline" and "Good Morning America" coming your way tonight and first thing in the morning. We'll see you right back here tomorrow night. Good night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL CONVENTION\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020240830ek8j00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HALLE BERRY'S NEW ROLE ACTRESS FIGHTING AGAINST STIGMAS & FOR MENOPAUSE RESEARCH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,234 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE, ERNESTO, TRACKING\par \par \uc2 2024, \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , HARRIS, ECONOMY, INFLATION, BIDEN, DEBATE, WALZ, VANCE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, DEAL, MEDICARE, PRESCRIPTION, DRUGS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, PRISON, CAROLINA, CHARITY, TREASON, UKRAINE, TATER, ASSAULT, PATEL\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, ATTACK, RUSSIA, DRONE, MILITARY, AIRFIELDS, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 ESCAPED, MURDERER, ALSTON, TRANSPORT, GUARDS, REWARD, SAUNDERS, BLACKWOOD\par \par \uc2 MPOX, VIRUS, AFRICA, WHO, CHILDREN, SYMPTOMS, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 ROWLANDS, "THE NOTEBOOK", ALZHEIMER'S, CASSAVETES, CAREER, AWARDS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM KANSAS CITY, MISSOURI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM CHILI'S COMMERCIAL ADVERTISEMENT 1997\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, SECURITY, TERROR, PLOT, TAY-GATING, PHILLIPS, MANNAHAN, FANS\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, CAMERA, SKOLNIK, BURKE, WALSH, PARENTS, SAFETY, SCREAM, BACKPACK, POST\par \par \uc2 CHILI'S, JINGLE, HOCHMAN, FAST, FOOD, PRICES, CONSUMER, DEALS\par \par \uc2 MLB, JUDGE, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , BATS, GAMES, HOME, RUNS, RUTH, KINER, CLUB, WHITE, SOX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAVE FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERNESTO, TRACK, BERMUDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, HARRIS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 BRAIN INJURY, STUDY, BRAIN ACTIVITY\par \par \uc2 KIEFER, SCRUGGS, DUBROVICH, FENCING, OLYMPICS\par \par \uc2 "MAMMA MIA", BARANSKI, FIELD, BARINHOLTZ\par \par \uc2 SHOPPING, DEALS, SELF-CARE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, BERRY, SHEPHERD, MAKI, SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LET'S TALK\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, BERRY, SHEPHERD, MAKI, SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 WOMEN (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN DOLGEN (MENOPAUSE EDUCATION ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Remember, suffering in silence is out. Reaching out is in. Time to talk about menopause.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is exactly what we're doing. Welcome back. That was Ellen Dolgen. She is a host of menopause Monday parties, which aim to educate women about menopause. And we are talking about it with Oscar-winning actor and advocate Halle Berry, and menopause experts Dr. Jessica Shepherd and Dr. Pauline Maki. We've got to get to the questions now because you all have been a great audience. I've been seeing you bobbing your head, go on like that. So, let's start with Luz.\par \par \uc2 LUZ GANDULLA (AUDIENCE MEMBER)\par \par \uc2 Hi. So, if you begin hormone treatment during perimenopause and your cycle stops, how do you know when you've moved into menopause? And how long can you be in treatment?\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 I love all these questions. They're perfect. And especially when we're talking about menopause and perimenopause and the differences. You can start hormone therapy in perimenopause, something we have done differently. But even if you stop your cycle and you are in menopause, that still doesn't change the fact that you have symptoms. Therefore, you can stay on hormone therapy in perimenopause, into menopause, into post-menopause.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm going to stay on hormone therapy till the day I die.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Me too.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And can you destigmatize that? Because some people, when they hear that, they're like, ooh.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. There's a cancer thing.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, right.\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Yes. So, we've had a lot of questions on, does it cause certain cancers? And, you know, the research has been better than we've seen before. And now, we know that estrogen does not cause breast cancer. And so, that really gives a lot of reassurance to women who have maybe in their family history or really concerned about that. So, I think that's important to know. But have an important conversation with your health care provider. And if they're not answering your question, find another one.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Here, here. Okay. Our next question is from Renata.\par \par \uc2 RENATA BONAR (AUDIENCE MEMBER)\par \par \uc2 Hello. What are some common misconceptions and myths about menopause that women should be aware of?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Dr. Maki?\par \par \uc2 DR PAULINE MAKI (PROF PSYCHIATRY PSYCHOLOGY & OB/GYN UNIV OF ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 I think this idea that we should be silent is out of date. We need to take action. We need to demand care. They used to say, you should just deal with your symptoms and not talk about it. But it's the new age, right? Menopause is having a moment and we're seeing the Super Bowl commercials about hot flashes. So, let's talk about it, right? So, that's a really important piece of information. Demand care.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yes. And you know what, and demand that when you realize you're in menopause, instead of like, you know, how people have bar mitzvahs and baby showers? Make everybody in your family give you a siesta.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love that. I love that.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 You've got a moment to celebrate. Siesta.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is now a warning, it's a celebration.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. And make it as big as a 13-year-old get for a bar mitzvah. Make it big. Make it big. Celebrate it.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to, I know that Deneise has a question.\par \par \uc2 DENEISE TAYLOR (AUDIENCE MEMBER)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Thank you. What about women like me, that can't take hormones for medical reasons? What options do we have? You know, I try a lot of holistic and nothing seems to be working.\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I love that question because we do have hormones where we have stigma against it or not knowing the right information. So, the important thing is to understand that, even if you didn't want to take hormones, that's okay. There are medications that are newer on the market that actually help with hot flashes that are non-hormonal and actually impact the neuron in the brain that does temperature control. So, talk to your doctor about this. We need more information. We need everyone here to understand what options are available to you.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 And this is why we have to get this bill passed. You have to reach out to your senators, you have to ask for this money, right? So that we can do more studies and we can constantly come up with more holistic remedies for us because not all of us want to do hormones. And that's valid. But then we need solutions. We need more solutions than questions.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. And we need more talk like this.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A big thank you to Dr. Shepherd and Dr. Maki. And they're going to stick around. They're going to get on social with me, and you will as well because we have so many more questions that people want to ask.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. I have a list of, like, six. All right, we're going to give you answers. Everybody's here.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're busy, but you found time to do a movie. What you got? What you got?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It's called "The Union." It airs on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  this Friday. It's with Mark Wahlberg. It's about two old flames coming back together. And what's great about this time, the woman goes and plucks the man from obscurity.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240904ek8f0006c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HALLE BERRY'S NEW ROLE ACTRESS FIGHTING AGAINST STIGMAS & FOR MENOPAUSE RESEARCH\b0\par\par\uc2 3,375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE, ERNESTO, TRACKING\par \par \uc2 2024, \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , HARRIS, ECONOMY, INFLATION, BIDEN, DEBATE, WALZ, VANCE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, DEAL, MEDICARE, PRESCRIPTION, DRUGS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, PRISON, CAROLINA, CHARITY, TREASON, UKRAINE, TATER, ASSAULT, PATEL\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, ATTACK, RUSSIA, DRONE, MILITARY, AIRFIELDS, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 ESCAPED, MURDERER, ALSTON, TRANSPORT, GUARDS, REWARD, SAUNDERS, BLACKWOOD\par \par \uc2 MPOX, VIRUS, AFRICA, WHO, CHILDREN, SYMPTOMS, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 ROWLANDS, "THE NOTEBOOK", ALZHEIMER'S, CASSAVETES, CAREER, AWARDS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM KANSAS CITY, MISSOURI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM CHILI'S COMMERCIAL ADVERTISEMENT 1997\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, SECURITY, TERROR, PLOT, TAY-GATING, PHILLIPS, MANNAHAN, FANS\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, CAMERA, SKOLNIK, BURKE, WALSH, PARENTS, SAFETY, SCREAM, BACKPACK, POST\par \par \uc2 CHILI'S, JINGLE, HOCHMAN, FAST, FOOD, PRICES, CONSUMER, DEALS\par \par \uc2 MLB, JUDGE, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , BATS, GAMES, HOME, RUNS, RUTH, KINER, CLUB, WHITE, SOX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAVE FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE ERNESTO, TRACK, BERMUDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, HARRIS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 BRAIN INJURY, STUDY, BRAIN ACTIVITY\par \par \uc2 KIEFER, SCRUGGS, DUBROVICH, FENCING, OLYMPICS\par \par \uc2 "MAMMA MIA", BARANSKI, FIELD, BARINHOLTZ\par \par \uc2 SHOPPING, DEALS, SELF-CARE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, BERRY, SHEPHERD, MAKI, SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LET'S TALK\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We do welcome you back to "GMA." And this morning, we are doing something special. We're devoting this next half hour to talking about menopause with Oscar Award-winning Halle Berry is here with us. We have two wonderful doctor experts that are going to help us through this. And can we just talk about this audience of women that we have. Thank you for taking the time to be here and talk about such an important subject as menopause. And this woman, she took her message to Capitol Hill. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm in menopause.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALLE BERRY'S NEW ROLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS FIGHTING AGAINST STIGMAS & FOR MENOPAUSE RESEARCH\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Menopause is having its moment.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 The shame has to be taken out of menopause.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oscar winner Halle Berry pushing back against harmful stigmas, supporting a $275-million bill that would fund research and education on menopause.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm here as a citizen of the United States, as a woman, who is demanding that our government give us what we need because we deserve it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 58-year-old encouraging women to step up, to support women's health for years to come.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 You stand up in your own state and you demand that there's a change and that money is allocated for your health in midlife and beyond. Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. We thank you very much. And we're going to have the doctors that are sitting right here, Dr. Shepherd, Dr. Maki. They're going to join us. We have this wonderful audience as well. But can I just talk, can we just talk to Halle just for a minute? Can we do that right now? Because you are our champion right now. And what caused you to go to Capitol Hill to bring this message?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALLE BERRY LIVE IN TIMES SQUARE\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Well, this is my favorite subject, like I can talk about this ad nauseum. But what got me there was realizing that I was in perimenopause and had no idea. No idea. Nobody, I was 54 years old, 54, and no doctor that I had dared to talk to me about it. It was as if this would never happen to me. And I got a misdiagnosis. I've told this story ad nauseum, so I won't do it again. But I got a misdiagnosis of herpes. Right? And that's not an easy thing to hear. And my doctor diagnosed me just by looking at my symptoms, doing an exam. He goes, this looks like the worst case of herpes I've ever seen. What? Herpes? Just to find out three days later it wasn't herpes at all. And I said, what is it then? He didn't know. Right?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 And that is what got me so riled up. And I thought, wow. If I have one of the best doctors, and my doctor truly is an amazing man. It's not his fault. Doctors aren't educated in this subject, so it's not their fault. But I have one of the best doctors in all of California, and he didn't really understand what this was? That made me realize, there must be millions of women that don't understand this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And didn't you even have a doctor who didn't even want to use the word menopause?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yes. I have another doctor, my, my eyes were drying up and my glands in my eyes. And he didn't want to say, well, this is because of menopause. And I was the one to say to him, what's causing this, doctor? And he was like, well, you know, they're drying up. And I said, but why? Tell me why. He couldn't say it. And because he couldn't say it, I also said, this is the problem. He was afraid to say it because he said, if I say this, you know, women are going to punch me. And like, women don't want to hear they are in menopause. And I said, but you're a health care practitioner. You have to talk to us about this. And that made me realize, as women, we have to make it okay. So, how do we make it okay? We say the word. We say, I'm in menopause. That's okay. You know, it's a normal stage of life. It's what's we're going through. Don't make it, it's not a disease. Don't make it a negative.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, exactly.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 And then let's talk about it and let's figure out what we can do. Like in our, I want 35-year-olds to start thinking about, oh, that's coming. And I want them to start understanding through great doctors and great books that are on the market now what they can do to sort of ease into this time of life in the best possible way. Don't be afraid of it. Be fearless about it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because we didn't talk about it. We didn't talk about it.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We, we, it is so...\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Too ashamed.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But not anymore.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 And it meant we are old. And what happens is, when we are out of that 25 to 35 childbearing years, society has taught us, well, we're done because, as women, that's all we're here to do is pump out these babies. Well, hello, that's just not true. I am in the best act of my life, my second act. And we all can be. Right? We all can be.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, yes. I have been around you for many years, I can't remember the last time, the passion that is just oozing out of you right now. And this is an important discussion for all women to have. And in particular women of color because it's different.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 We suffer disproportionately. Right? Our heart disease, we're at risk for, diabetes, dementia, Alzheimer's. All of that we are at greater risk, us ladies of color. And so, we really have to. And this is the time of our life when, traditionally women lean out of the health care. No, we need to lean into our health care. Right? Change our diet, change our exercise. Wait, this tire is going to show up around your belly. It's just going to happen. It's just going to happen and you can't, it's going to happen. I, my ego said, oh, that's not going to happen to me. I work out, I eat well. Guess what? Tire showed up.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 And I had to change my eating, exercise differently and I manage it. But we have to know that we have to start that sooner. Don't wait for the tire to show up. Know that it's coming and be prepared for that.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And talk a little bit more, like, she's just preaching right now. I'm leaning in as well. But what can we, as women, do to be able to do what you're doing and freely discuss this and to know all the things that you are saying and embrace it instead of running from it. Not that all women do that, but some.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 All of us do, I can tell you, because we're afraid. We have to be fearless. We have to define who we are. Right? We have to talk about it with our partners, like your husbands, your wives. Talk about it with them. Talk about it with your children. We have to reclaim this narrative and destigmatize it. When we're comfortable, we make them comfortable. I was at a conference recently called Brilliant Minds in Sweden. And I said some words and these men in this audience looked like they had been gob-smacked. I thought, sorry, but you need to hear it. And they came up to me later and said, that was really hard to hear.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I said, really? Imagine living through that? What would that feel like? So, I'm sorry you can't hear it, but you need to hear it because this is happening to us and we cannot be discouraged. So, what do I say? Talk about it with ease. So what if you make other people uncomfortable? Don't care. Reach out to your senators right now, so we can get this bill passed.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a bipartisan bill.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It's a bipartisan bill.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 So, whoever your senator is, reach out to your senator, tell them you demand health care for midlife women. You demand this act to be passed. You demand funds to be allocated just for menopause. Because if we wait for the \uc2 NIH\uc2  to do it, everything becomes more important than women going through midlife. And we're half the population and we spend half our life because we're starting at 40 and we're living until 80. For half of our life, we're in this period and there's zero information out there for us. We need new therapies, you know, developed. We need information, more studies. We need all of it. And we deserve it. You know, we are responsible for procreation. These babies come out of our bodies. You can't just dump us off after we do that very important act. No. We say no. And we have to say no. We have to stand up and say no.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. We're going to continue this conversation.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yes, let's continue.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are going to continue this conversation. Put it right here.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But right now, let's head over to Ginger right now.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA USER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Today, we're going to talk about the symptoms of menopause and what they mean because I don't think that women talk about this enough. And I was not prepared.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA USER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So, it can include difficulty with memory, forgetfulness.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA USER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Your voice becomes shaky.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA USER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Heart palpitations, stench and smells.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA USER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I was seeing things that I don't think other people are seeing. Is that slight, is like slight hallucination one of the symptoms?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you see the women nodding along here? More and more women are turning to social media to talk about menopause and what they are going through. We're talking about it now with Halle Berry.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are. And we are joined by team menopause experts. Dr. Jessica Shepherd is with us and also Dr. Pauline Maki. Welcome to you both again. So happy you're here. So glad we're all talking about it. And then we're laughing about it because what's the alternative? I mean, here we are. I want to start with Dr. Shepherd. What are the different stages and (inaudible) ?\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 It stages, right? This is what we're talking about, people didn't know about it. Our mothers didn't talk about it. But perimenopause really is that timeframe that can last anywhere from three to seven sometimes ten years. This is when your estrogen is starting to dial down, when you might feel a symptom here or there. Did I feel that? Did I not? Then you go into menopause. And menopause really is a very clinical term. Really what it means is you've gone 12 months consecutively without a cycle.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STAGES OF MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 And at that 12th month, you can be like, I'm in menopause. So, really, if you think about it, that day when you reach menopause and that's when estrogen cannot give you a cycle anymore and you've gone 12 months without a period. And then after that, you're post-menopausal because now you've passed that menopausal phase. And you really do stay post-menopausal until you die. So, I think it should be kind of like a title that you take with pride because you are post-menopausal.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I thought I was in menopause. And then all of a sudden, my period came and I was like, uh-oh. And you (inaudible) all the way back to the beginning. I've got a year longer. What now?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you have (inaudible) to look forward to (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I know. What is that going to be. But, yeah, you know.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We touched about it a little bit, about the symptoms and that. And, Dr. Maki, people, we always think about hot flashes. But, you know, that's just the tip of the iceberg, so to speak. There's so much more.\par \par \uc2 DR PAULINE MAKI (PROF PSYCHIATRY PSYCHOLOGY & OB/GYN UNIV OF ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 Right? 80% of women experience hot flashes. Right? They get real. And people used to say, they were benign. But they last on average for 7.6 years if you're white and ten years if you're black. And they're not benign.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 DR PAULINE MAKI (PROF PSYCHIATRY PSYCHOLOGY & OB/GYN UNIV OF ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 Disproportionately. And they're not benign, right? They wake us up at night? So, we have disturbed sleep. They affect our mood. Right? And they affect our cognitive function. We're a little foggier when we have our hot flashes, right? And they happen night after night. Week after week. Month after month. Year after year. So, how can that be benign? Right? So, we need to talk about hot flashes as something really important for women's health. And if you're a woman who has hot flashes into her 60s, that's a marker of who that increase risk for heart disease later on. And also, Alzheimer's disease. So, we need to really look at the effects of menopause on our body. And when we go through menopause, we have physical body changes, right?\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 We talk about that on (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DR PAULINE MAKI (PROF PSYCHIATRY PSYCHOLOGY & OB/GYN UNIV OF ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 Our bones change, the way fat is distributed. I want Halle's middle. I'll take it. Halle has to work for this middle.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 This middle is not the same middle it used to be. It takes work.\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB/GYN & MENOPAUSE EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 We need to understand that there are estrogen receptors all over your body. In your brain, in your heart, in your muscle. And so, that's when you start to see these bodily changes after losing estrogen it's because the estrogen receptors don't have the estrogen to attach to.\par \par \uc2 DR PAULINE MAKI (PROF PSYCHIATRY PSYCHOLOGY & OB/GYN UNIV OF ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 This whole lack of sleep is really real, what it does to your brain. I have flooded my laundry room three times because I go in there and I forget why I went in there. I turn the water on, heat up the dog food, walk out, get busy. And then I hear (makes noise), my alarm us going off and there's like three feet of water in my laundry, like, three times I've done it. Three times. I would have never done that before this stage.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Brain fog is real.\par \par \uc2 DR PAULINE MAKI (PROF PSYCHIATRY PSYCHOLOGY & OB/GYN UNIV OF ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 That's the brain fog.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 When you don't sleep, then you're angry, you forget things. I'm fighting with everybody. It's crazy.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have a good hack, a tip if you can find it. I found this product that is like a bed, a mattress pad. And it's like air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) sleep?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, this one is called chilly pad. It is, I have it at like morgue level.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Me too. I have a thing called (inaudible) and it's amazing.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It really does help to keep that bed cool. And I know you've been on (inaudible) the middle. I'm right there with you. It's just this annoying thing that won't go away. But what else did you experience that you were like, okay, this is happening? I'm in it.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Well, hot flashes. Speedy heart rate, you know, forgetting information.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you feel like it was anxiety?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Anxiety. So much so that people started to say that I probably needed to get on medication for it. Then I felt really depressed. Somebody, one of my doctors said I need to be on anti-depressants. I said, no, I've managed lots of depressive states. I'm not going to do that. I don't think that's it. So, there's all these things that people are trying to tell us to do because they really don't understand we're in this phase and it's not really about me medicating ourselves. It's finding other ways to get through this, right? It's not depression, it's what comes from not sleeping. You know, so how can we sleep better is the question, which is why this, you know, this mattress that cools you from the bottom up really works. That's really a natural good way to manage this. It's helped me a lot.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whatever it takes. Whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are going to continue this conversation. Because hormone therapy is something that we have to talk about because that's one of the questions that we're getting a lot, too.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240904ek8f0006a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ARIZONA PUTS ABORTION ON THE BALLOT VOTERS TO DECIDE ON REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS MEASURE IN NOVEMBER ELECTION\b0\par\par\uc2 269 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S CONVERSATION WITH ELON MUSK\par \par \uc2 MUSK, 'X', TRUMP, INTERVIEW, TWITTER, TECH, DNC, HARRIS, BIDEN, EMAIL, HACK, IRAN\par \par \uc2 ARIZONA, VOTERS, NOVEMBER, BALLOT, ABORTION, REPRODUCTIVE, RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, George, thank you. New this morning, voters in Arizona will decide on reproductive rights measure in a November. Elizabeth Schulze joins us now with the details. Good morning, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA PUTS ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS TO DECIDE ON REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS MEASURE IN NOVEMBER ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA ABORTION BALLOT MEASURE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAP, ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Michael. Abortion rights will now officially be on the ballot in battleground Arizona in November. Arizona secretary of state tells ABC News that it has enough signatures for a ballot initiative that will ask voters to establish the constitutional right to an abortion up to around 24 weeks of pregnancy. Right now Arizona law bans abortions after 15 weeks, except for a medical emergency. And in May, state lawmakers there narrowly repeal a near total ban dating back to the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Arizona now one of six states with a confirmed ballot measure asking voters to protect or expand abortion rights. Four other states are still considering adding similar measures. And Democrats are hopeful that this will be a galvanizing issue. In every state where abortion has been on the ballot since the end of Roe v. Wade, the abortion rights position has won. Robin?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240904ek8d0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENT NOMINEE SENATOR JD VANCE HARRIS CHARACTERIZES HERSELF AND WALZ AS 'UNDERDOGS'\b0\par\par\uc2 5,180 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2024 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 VANCE, 2024, HARRIS, FUENTES, RACISM, PARENTS, WALZ, ABORTION, VP, IMMIGRANTS\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning. Welcome to "This Week." What a difference a few weeks makes. Before President Biden dropped out of the presidential race, that was three weeks ago today, Democrats were discouraged. Republicans were on a roll, and it looked like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was well on his way to winning back the White House. Now a new "New York Times"/Sienna poll in a poll of the battleground states likely determine who will win, shows that the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 -\uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  ticket has the momentum, leading Trump-Vance by four points among likely voters in Michigan, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRAND NEW RACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SWING STATE POLLING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE NEW YORK TIMES HEADLINE\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a dramatic change after what the "Times" is calling the worst three weeks of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 2024 campaign. After a rough start as Trump's running mate, JD Vance is now leading the Republican effort to define the Harris-Walz ticket and to regain the momentum. I caught up with him this weekend at his home state of Ohio. We started with the state of the race. Thank you for joining us, Senator Vance. So...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS CHARACTERIZES HERSELF AND WALZ AS 'UNDERDOGS'\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I saw, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said that she's the underdog in this race. Is that the way you see it? I mean, are you guys still the, the frontrunners here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that we are gonna have a very tight - election, Jon, and we just have to sprint to the finish line. We have about 88 days to actually persuade the American people that President Trump should be their President, me, their vice president. And I think you just take nothing for granted, you don't believe any polls, whether the ones that are good for ya or bad for ya. You just try to persuade people as much as you can and the chips are gonna fall where they are.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Trump seems to think that he's got a huge lead. I mean, he just said this at the press conference. He suggested it could be a landslide, looking at polls. Do you tell him that you need to kind of, like, temper expectations?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Look, I, I think President Trump is extremely confident. Look, I'm extremely confident. I think we're gonna win. I also think that we have to work as hard as possible for the remainder of the election to persuade Americans to vote for us. That's the name of the game, but yeah. Look, do I think President Trump is actually as confident as he pretends to be in public? Yes, he actually is. But we still have to work very hard.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VANCE'S WIFE ATTACKED OVER RACE BY MAN WHO DINED WITH TRUMP\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, there's been a lot of talk about racism and whatnot. You faced some really nasty stuff. I - saw this thing that Nick Fuentes, of course, he's an avowed White supremacist. He said, "What kind of a man marries somebody named Usha? Clearly he doesn't value his racist identity, his heritage." I mean, this is racist garbage.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this is also a guy that dined with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at Mar-a-Lago during this campaign.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't know anything about him, and frankly doesn't care for. But yeah, look, my, my attitude to these people attacking my wife is she's beautiful. She's smart. What kind of man marries Usha? A very smart man and a very lucky man, importantly.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And, and my, my views, look, if these guys want to attack me or attack my views, my policy views, my personality, come after me, but don't attack my wife. She's out of your league.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, I mean, that dinner that Trump had, and of course, it was Kanye West, the guy that's praised Hitler, who arranged it all, that, that was back a year and a half ago, and Trump still hasn't given a full-throated denouncement of this guy who is, said that terrible stuff, but he said, I mean, he's a White supremacist.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Look, I think he's, President Trump has issued plenty of condemnations on this. The one thing that I like...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But not of Fuentes. He hasn't.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 The one, the one, the one thing I like about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jon, is that he actually will talk to anybody. But just because you talk to somebody doesn't mean you endorse their views. And look, I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent a lot of quality time with my wife. Every time he sees her, he gives her a hug, tells her she's beautiful and jokes around with her a little bit. I'm not at all worried about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I'm worried sometimes about these ridiculous attacks. But again, this is what you sign up for when you come into politics. I wish people would keep it focused on me, but whatever. They're gonna say what they're gonna say. My wife's tough enough to handle it and that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VANCE IN 2021: PARENTS 'SHOULD HAVE MORE POWER' AT THE POLLS\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Let's focus on something that's gotten a lot of attention. I mean, you know, this cat ladies comment, and you talk about Democrat...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Party dominated by childish cat ladies. Put that aside. You also made a policy proposal...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...which was why \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  had you on for that interview and you said, you advocated giving extra votes to people with children, which seems a little...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, Jon...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...it's, it's not a policy proposal. It's the thought experiment, right? Democrats said, we should give children the right to vote. Some Democrats had said we're gonna give children the right to vote. And I said, well, if we're gonna give the rights to the children, then we should actually just allow the parents to cast those votes, right? I trust a parent more with a decision like that than I do say a 14-year-old. So, it's a thought experiment. There are though, policy positions behind my view that the country should become more pro-family, right? One, one thing I, I learned as a dad is after a second home, after our second child we brought home, we have three little kids.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We got these ridiculous surprise medical billings from the hospital because we had chosen an out-of-network provider, of course, at this most stressful of all imaginable moments.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I've actually introduced legislation to stop moms and dads from having to go through those surprise medical billings. We want to expand the child tax credit. There's so much that we want to do, Jon, to make the country more pro-family. And I, I think it is, is frankly shameful that the Harris administration has lied about what I actually said. Look, sometimes family doesn't work out for people...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, so, so...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...and that's okay. But I do think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  herself has pursued some anti-family policies and that is a problem and something we ought to push back against.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I, put aside whatever other people, I mean, I listened to your entire speech.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Please...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I listened to the whole thing, and, you know, I saw, and you did say you weren't talking about people who physically can't have children. I mean, that's not what you were saying.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, and sometimes it just doesn't work out, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and it didn't work out, you did say that.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You called it throat-clearing, I think. You said, you know, I'm, look, I'm not talking about that, but you did say...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, because I knew people would try to misrepresent it, so it's important to be clear.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but let's look at what, let's look at what you exactly said.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Please.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You said, "When you go to the polls in this country as a parent, should have more power. You should have more of an ability to speak your voice in a democratic republic than people that don't have kids." I mean, you are directly saying that people with kids should have more of a voice in our democracy. Thought experiment...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...or not about how that is done, but that is the principle. And you, I mean, I mean, I could read on, but, but you are expressing a principle here.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 A - again, Jon, it's a thought experiment. I'd been a senator for two years. Have I proposed any legislation to that effect? Of course not. Sometimes people make remarks in response to something that somebody else has said. If it was a policy proposal, I would've made the policy proposal in my two years in the United Sates Senate. Now, I, I do think, Jon, to be clear here, 'cause I think it's important to focus on what I actually said. I do think becoming a parent affects you in a very profound way. I've seen friends of mine, certainly in my own life, becoming a father does really give you this incredible perspective. You have this little life that you have to take care of. It's an amazing thing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And it does make you think differently about your responsibilities. So, so yeah, I absolutely do think that being a parent changes you in a profound way. I do not think that we ought to be changing the way that we do votes in this country. I was responding to a Democratic proposal in a very, very thought experiment way.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you predicted that you would get attacked. You said I'm sure...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I did.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ..."The Atlantic," "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " will come out and say, well, doesn't this mean nonparents don't have as much of a voice as parents? Doesn't it mean that parents get a bigger say in how our democracy functions? Yes, absolutely. So, do you regret saying that? You take that back?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Look, Jon, again, I'm talking about a thought experiment here, and do I regret saying it? I regret that the media and the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign has, has frankly distorted what I said. I'm trying to make...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How's that distorting what, I just read your words. How's that distorting what you said?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Because they have turned this into a policy proposal that I never made, Jon. I said I want us to be more pro-family, and do I want us to be more pro-family. And I do think, look, when you're a parent and you have children, you obviously have a different perspective, and that's, and I said that too. And I certainly believe that. But I think that the idea, Jon, that I actually want to change the American voting system to give 12-year-olds the right to vote and then to give control over to their parents, it's ridiculous. I never said that. I was responding to a Democratic line.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. I mean, it was a speech on the, anyway. We'll, we'll, we'll, the speech is online. People can watch the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Please, but, but, but in the, in the beginning, Jon, I say, Democrats have proposed giving these rights to children. I say, if we're gonna give them to children, let the parents cast that vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDIANS TO VOTE ON ABORTION REFERENDUM IN NOVEMBER\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I want to ask you about abortion. There's this proposed amendment that's gonna be on the ballot in Florida that basically codifies the constitutional right, the state constitutional right to have an abortion up to about 24 weeks. I assume this is something you oppose.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, what I believe, and I'm running to be the Vice President of the United States, is that the American people have to make this decision, and President Trump has said explicitly they're gonna make this decision on a state by state level. He won the nomination of our party. He's determined what the platform is, and I think that he's doing something that's actually very, very important, which is to try to help the country find some common ground. Look, California's gonna have a less restrictive abortion policy than Ohio, where we're sitting. Ohio's gonna have a less restrictive policy than Alabama. I think the people have to make these decisions, and that's the way for us to come together as a country over a very tough issue.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but, but this should be, I, I assume this is an easy one for you. I mean, you've have been very clear about how you feel about abortion. I mean, if you've compared it to slavery...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I didn't compare it to slavery, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well, you, the impact on a society to slavery, yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Ask the question. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I mean, just again, this, the idea of codifying, whatever the state, codifying a right to abortion up to about 24 weeks, isn't that something you oppose?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, look, in Ohio we had a referendum in the state, and I...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You made your position clear.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...and, and I aggressively advocated for the pro-life view because I want to save as many babies as possible. Also importantly, Jon, the people of Ohio decided by about a 60-40 margin to reject the pro-life framing and you have to respect the will of the people. That's all that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said. That's what I believe. And as someone who really does want to say is, Jon, I do want us to become a more pro-family country. I want us to have more babies and more families. I want our neighborhoods to be filled with the laughter of children and thriving families.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We in the pro-life community I think have to figure out how to better make our case to the American people and how to make it easier to have a baby. It's way too expensive. Housing is through the roof. Medical costs are through the roof. President Trump and I have actually advanced a series of policies that would make it easier for us to have families. I think that's where we're focused on as a presidential ticket. And as the President has said on abortion policies specifically, the states are gonna make up their minds.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's gonna have a chance to vote on that and he says he's gonna offer his position, didn't say what it was. Would you be disappointed if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says that he supports a constitutional right to abortion?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I'll let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  offer his position and I'll, I'll talk about it then.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What would you encourage him do on that, though? I mean, this is a pretty fundamental idea for you. I mean...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Jon, what I want, what I...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...you've been such a leader in the pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...what I would encourage him to do is to do what he's done up to this point, which is to try to find some common ground here and try to advance some pro-family policy so that people feel like they have more options, right? A lot of the reason women choose to terminate a pregnancy is very often they don't feel like they have any other choice. We want to give them more options to choose to have that baby, to make it easier to raise that baby. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has shown real leadership by focusing on that, and not on the culture war top of what each individual states' abortion policy should be.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VANCE ON WHO HE THINKS IS THE GREATEST VP OF HIS LIFETIME\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the greatest president of your lifetime.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 He is.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Who in your mind is the greatest vice president of your lifetime?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 That's a good question. I haven't thought much about that. I think \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , excuse me, \uc2 George HW Bush\uc2  did a very good job as vice president. You could pick a number of guys who've done a perfectly fine job, but the President really sets the tone for policy. And I think a lot more about the President than the Vice President, and fundamentally my role in the administration is to support him, enact the, in, in enacting the agenda. That's what I'm gonna work on.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Trump was asked recently very directly, would you be ready to be president on day one if the need arised.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it was notable that he didn't answer the question. Instead what he said is, the Vice Presidential candidate doesn't matter. I mean, what did you make when you, when you heard him not answer that directly?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, he said that a million times both in private and many public.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He said, he said the part about his, but, but he was asked really directly...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And he, and he's right.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...are you ready?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP DOESN'T ANSWER IF VANCE WOULD BE 'READY ON DAY ONE'\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Most people, oh, come on, Jon. Most, he's right. Most people, 99 percent of the country, they don't vote on who the Vice Presidential nominee, they're voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , not for JD or \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , right? But what I think that he does believe because he made it the main focus of his vetting process is, do I think this person can be president on day one if God forbid something happens? Yes. So, I absolutely, I'm sure that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is confident in my abilities. I also think that he's right that the politics of this really don't matter that much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP PROMISES MASS DEPORTATION OF UNDOCUMENTED IMMIGRANTS\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One of the proposals (inaudible) in the platform you've talked about it, Trump's talked about it, is a mass deportation. Now, I know, you've said, he said you'll start with the easy ones, the criminals, people who commit crimes, deport them, but he said deporting 15 million to 20 million people. How do you actually do that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, Jon, we have to start with the fact that we have a wide-open southern border because our border czar actually set a lot of open border policies. They suspended...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know she's not actually chosen the border czar, I mean, she...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, that was what the media called her. She assumed the title.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, but...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 She had control over a lot of our border policy at a time when they were suspending...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What was the, the root causes of the migration?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Let me finish, Jon. The time they was suspending deportations...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...they stopped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Remain in Mexico policy, and they reinstituted catch and release. They basically threw open the gates of our country and now we have a ton of fentanyl, a ton of people suffering and a ton of Americans...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But how are you gonna, will you get 10 to 15 to 20 million people out of the country?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, the first thing you have to do is to stop the bleeding, stop the open border, get \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  out of there and actually reimplement the Remain in Mexico policy, rebuild or finish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's border wall. And you do that and you stop the bleeding. Now, you're right. Once you do that, once you stop \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' open border policies, you've got to do something with the people who are already here, and I think that you take a sequential approach to it. You are gonna have to deport some people. If you're not willing to deport a lot of people, you're not willing to have a border when they are 20 million illegal aliens in our country.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But 10 million to 15 million people, this is like, I mean...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, Jon, I think that's the wrong attitude to take of it. Okay, so there, so there are...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, do you go knock on doors and ask people for their papers?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, again, but I, I, let me...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you do?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I think there's a wrong, this is the wrong attitude towards it, right? There's 20 million people here illegally. You start with what's achievable. You do that, and then you go onto what's achievable from there. I think that if you deport a lot of violent criminals, and frankly, you make it harder to hire illegal labor, which undercuts the wages of American workers, I think you go a lot of the way to solving the - illegal immigration problem. But look, President Trump's absolutely right. You cannot have a border unless you're willing to deport some people. I think it's interesting that people focus on, well, how do you deport 18 million people? Let's start with one million. That's where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has - failed and then we can go from there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP, VANCE BLAST WALZ OVER GENDER-AFFIRMING CARE LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and finally before you go, will you commit to this race to kind of sticking to the facts? I mean, I heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  give, give this speech in, in Montana he just gave, and he, he said that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  signed a letter letting the state kidnap children to change their, their gender, that allowing pedophiles to claim, you know, I mean, to - be exempt from crimes. I mean, this is not true. It's not remotely true.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, what President Trump said, and I haven't watched the whole rally. What President...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What, what he said was not true.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 What President Trump said, Jon, is that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has supported taking children from their parents if the parents don't consent to gender resignment. That is crazy. And by the way, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  gets on his high horse about mind your own damn business. One way of minding your own damn business, Jon, is to not try to take my children away from me if I have different moral views than you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He has not signed a law allowing the state to kidnap...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 They have...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...children to change their sexual identity.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 How would you, what I just explained to you I would describe as kidnapping, Jon. He done absolutely this stuff.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's crazy. Come on.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 He's not crazy, Jon. Come on. You should not...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's not what he (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 You shouldn't be able to take people's children away from them if you just...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's not what he's proposed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VANCE STRESSES POLICY DIFFERENCES WITH HARRIS CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...if you disagree with decisions about gender reassignment. Yes, he has proposed that, Jon. He absolutely has. Now, here's, here's the more important thing, Jon, why, why aren't we talking about inflation? Why aren't we talking about the fact that groceries are unaffordable thanks to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' policies and so is housing? We've talked a little bit about the border. Why aren't we talking about the fact the entire world is on fire because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' foreign policy, she's just asleep at the wheel. We have a set of plans. You talk about sticking to the facts. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I have a set of plans to lower the cost of housing...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We should say...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...and food, to bring peace back to the world with American leadership. That is all that we want to do, and I think it's telling that the Harris administration is focused so much on these side issues instead of on the real substance...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, what, to, to...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...of why Americans...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...we're, we're, we're...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...are unhappy with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' leadership.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To be clear, I just asked you about something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought up, not something that the Harris campaign brought up. I was asking you about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's words, but thank you.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 No, I'm saying that, fair...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VANCE CRITICIZES HARRIS FOR LACK OF MEDIA APPEARANCES\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But the Harris, but Jon, the Harris campaign, what are their policy views? They don't have a policy position on their website. Should she sit down and answer tough questions with you? I think she should\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, absolutely. We're, we're...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Where is she? But we respect the American people enough...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I, we, we, we, we hope, we hope she'll be on the show soon.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I hope so too, Jon...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 ...because the person who wants to be our president ought to sit down for some tough interviews. I'm willing to do it and I wish she would too.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. JD Vance, thank you for your time. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Jon. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our thanks to Senator Vance. Coming up, he said the 2024 race would come down to a contest between the cowards, the crooks, and the couch. So what does radio show host Charlamagne THA God think of the race now that Vice President Harris is on top of the Democratic ticket? We'll have that conversation when we come back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM FEBRUARY 18 INTERVIEW\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020240826ek8b00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL, VOEPASS, FLIGHT, 2283, CRASH, FIRE, VICTIMS, WITNESSES\par \par \uc2 NE, DEBBY, TORNADO, FLOODS, SACKRIDER, HURST, COOPER, FLIGHT, DELAYS\par \par \uc2 NORTHEAST, DEBBY, TIMING, HURRICANE, OUTAGE, FLOOD, TORNADO, ERNESTO\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of disruptions. Trevor, thank you. Let's get right to meteorologist Lee Goldberg from our partner station WABC here in New York. Lee, time this all out for us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEBBY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIMING DEBBY\par \par \uc2 LEE GOLDBERG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Linsey. So, Debby's final act is to drench the Northeast. The low that was once a hurricane, that's working through upstate New York and New England. There are still flood watches down the coast. We have to deal with this through the first part of the night. There's a tornado watch until ten o'clock. We're already seeing power outages across Vermont. As it moves across New England and down to Long Island, there's still heavy downpours, so gusts to 50 miles an hour and isolated tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WATCHING THE TROPICS\par \par \uc2 LEE GOLDBERG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But what a change by tomorrow morning. It clears off the coast. We start to lose the humidity, the sun comes back. It starts a great stretch of weather, but then all eyes are on the tropics again, flurry of new activity. Storm could develop over the next seven days as it approaches the lesser Antilles. The next named storm would be Ernesto. Linsey?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now forecasting a highly active hurricane season ahead. Lee, thank you. Tonight, sources tell ABC News \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's plane was diverted in Montana late today because of a mechanical issue. Trump attending his first campaign event. But meanwhile, Vice President Harris and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  campaigned in Arizona. Trump once led President Biden nearby an average of more than five points. That was a different race and some new polls show Harris gaining ground. Here's ABC's Selina Wang.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S PLANE DIVERTED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: AIRCRAFT HAD MECHANICAL ISSUES\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, former President Trump's campaign plane diverted, forced to land in Billings, Montana, due to a mechanical issue, according to the airport. Trump and members of his team seen walking off the plane, onto the tarmac, continuing onto Bozeman on a private jet. Both campaigns heading West.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Hey, guys.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL CAMPAIGN HEATS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS, TRUMP HOLD RALLIES IN THE WEST\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With less than 90 days to go, tonight, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her new running mate, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , rallying in the key swing state of Arizona, in a battle to keep the state blue.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 This is the first time I have felt, like, invigorated and excited about politics.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA POLLING AVERAGE\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In July, former President Trump was averaging a five and a half-point lead over President Biden. Now with Harris in the race, it's a virtual tie. Harris putting the Sun Belt states back in play. Trump in Montana, a solidly red state, in his first stop on the trail this week, making an appearance to boost the Republican candidate trying to unseat Democratic Senator \uc2 John Tester\uc2 . At his Mar-a-Lago news conference, Trump weighing in on abortion, saying, it's going to be a very small issue in the election, while seeming to suggest he's open to blocking access to the abortion pill, mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF DIALOGUE\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Would you direct your FDA, for example, to revoke access to mifepristone? That's one of the things that's been discussed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 You could, you could do things that will be, would, would supplement, absolutely. And those things are pretty open, and humane. But you have to be able to have a vote. And all I want to do is give everybody a vote.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris pouncing, saying he has made clear that he'd use the federal government to rip away access to medication abortion, effectively banning abortion nationwide. We will not let him.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get right to Selina wang at the White House for us. Selina, what more are we learning about former President Trump after that diverted landing?\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sources close to Trump that tell us everyone on the plane is fine, that this was not an emergency. Trump is still planning to hold his rally tonight in Bozeman, Montana. Meanwhile, the White House is announcing that next week President Biden and Vice President Harris will hold their first trip together since Vice President Harris became the Democratic nominee. Linsey?\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020240813ek8900004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  HITS THE CAMPAIGN TRAIL EXPECTED TO NAME HER RUNNING MATE BY AUGUST 7 AS A CONTENDER PULLS HIS NAME\b0\par\par\uc2 784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, STOUT\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, SMOKE, HEAT ALERTS\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, ELECTION, RUNNING MATE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're gonna turn now to the race for the White House. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hits the campaign trail in Georgia today as she closes in on her pick for a running mate. Chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce has the story. Good morning, Mary.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  HITS THE CAMPAIGN TRAIL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXPECTED TO NAME HER RUNNING MATE BY AUGUST 7 AS A CONTENDER PULLS HIS NAME\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. Well, with her campaign jolted into hyper speed, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is now on a very tight timeline, giving herself just a week to announce her VP. And this morning, that list is narrowing.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, one of the leading contenders to be \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' running mate pulling his name from the short list. Governor Roy Cooper saying "this just wasn't the right time for North Carolina and for me to potentially be on a national ticket." With the clock ticking, Harris expected to announce her number two by August 7th. Potentials like Pennsylvania governor Josh Shapiro, are out stumping for Harris and auditioning for the job.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JOSH SHAPIRO (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris has been battle tested. She's not only ready, she's damn ready. And you know who else knows she's ready? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows she's ready. And you know, you know how I know this? 'Cause he's afraid to debate her now.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this morning still waffling on his agreement to debate in September, but insisting overnight that he's not afraid.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP UNDECIDED ON DEBATING HARRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS "I CAN ALSO MAKE A CASE FOR NOT DOING IT"\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 If you're going to have a debate, you've got to do it, I think, before the votes are cast. I think it's very important that you do that. So the answer is yes. But I can also make a case for not doing it.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But privately, his running mate J.D. Vance admitting to donors over the weekend that running against Harris instead of President Biden is more challenging, according to audio obtained by the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 All of us were, were hit with a little bit of a political sucker punch. The bad news is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does not have the same baggage as Joe Biden because whatever we might say, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a lot younger, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is obviously not struggling in the same ways that Joe Biden did.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Out on the trail, Harris summing up her opponents in just one word, calling them out as weird.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALLS OUT TRUMP & VANCE AS "WEIRD," LEANS INTO ABORTION ISSUE\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And you may have noticed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been resorting to some wild lies about my record and some of what he and his running mate are saying is just plain weird.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And she's leaning into the issue she hopes will define the choice facing voters come November, abortion rights. Speaking out against the new six week abortion ban that went into effect in Iowa Monday, placing the blame squarely on Trump after he appointed three of the Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 This ban is gonna take effect before many women even know they're pregnant. And what this means is that one in three women of reproductive age in America lives in a state with a Trump abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One of the states, Georgia. Harris today heading to the critical state where abortion is sure to be a top issue. Biden won the state by just 12,000 votes in 2020 but the President had been trailing Trump in recent weeks. The Harris campaign now hoping to build off her momentum to put the state back in play.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now Harris tonight will be holding a big rally in Atlanta, expected to be the largest event of the race so far. And this morning her campaign is out with their first ad, spending $50 million to blitz the airwaves as they reintroduce her to voters. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Mary. Our thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240822ek7u0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHITE HOUSE RACE HEATS UP HARRIS CAMPAIGN RAISED $200 MILLION+ IN A WEEK\b0\par\par\uc2 1,067 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 RACE, HARRIS, AD, ABORTION, TRUMP, IPSOS, POLL, VANCE, WALZ, SHAPIRO, WHITMER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tonight, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  taking aim at Iowa's new six-week abortion ban, blaming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his choices for the Supreme Court, who overturned Roe. ABC's Rachel Scott leading us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE RACE HEATS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS CAMPAIGN RAISED $200 MILLION+ IN A WEEK\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One full week into her campaign, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  surging in the polls, pulling in more than $200 million since entering the race. And tonight, Harris seizing on an issue she hopes will help define the choice in November, abortion. Speaking out against Iowa's six-week abortion ban, set to go into effect today. Placing the blame squarely on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who appointed three of the Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM HARRIS CAMPAIGN AD\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 This ban is gonna take effect before many women even know they're pregnant. And what this means is that one in three women of reproductive age in America lives in a state with a Trump abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Harris campaign also jumping on Trump's controversial comments to a Christian audience, where he suggested if they vote this time, they won't have to vote again.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP-VANCE CAMPAIGN APPEALS TO CONSERVATIVE CHRISTIANS\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Christians, get out and vote, just this time. You won't have to do it anymore. Four more years, you know what? It'll be fixed. It'll be fine. You won't have to vote anymore, my beautiful Christians. I love you Christians. I'm a Christian, I love you. Get out. You got to get out and vote. In four years, you don't have to vote again. We'll have it fixed so good, you're not gonna have to vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAVORABILITY\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Harris campaign calling this further proof "democracy is under assault." Trump, who was counting on a battle with President Biden, now trying to redefine Harris, even as voters warm to her. Tonight, the new ABC News/Ipsos poll shows in just one week, her favorability rating jumped eight points from 35 percent to 43.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We have a new victim now, Kamala. We have a new victim. We have a brand new victim. And honestly, she's a radical left lunatic.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris and her team now honing a new one-word message, calling Trump out as "weird."\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM JULY 27\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 You may have noticed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been resorting to some wild lies about my record. And some of what he and his running mate are saying, it's just plain weird.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats seizing on the backlash triggered by Trump's running mate, Senator JD Vance and his 2021 comments about women who don't have children.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM 2021 NEWS COVERAGE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 We are effectively run in this country via the Democrats via, via our corporate oligarchs by a bunch of childless cat ladies who are miserable at their own lives and the choices that they've made, and so they want to make the rest of the country miserable, too.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, Vance playing clean up.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM THE MEGYN KELLY SHOW, JULY 26\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it was a sarcastic comment. I've got nothing against cats.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But not backing down.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM JULY 28\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JD VANCE (OH) (REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 It's a fact that the Democratic Party has become explicitly anti-family in some of their policies.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM JULY 27\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats, like Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , sensing an opening, and there's that word again.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 These guys are just weird. That's who they are. So, we're not afraid of weird people.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 We, we're a little bit creeped out, but we're not afraid.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's bring in Rachel Scott tonight from Washington. Rachel, Vice President Harris we know heading to Georgia tomorrow. The latest Presidential poll show the race there tightening, as well, in this race. Now that we're dealing with a race between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This as her campaign continues to vet possible running mates. Everyone is watching this very closely. We know just a short time ago here, one leading contender, Pennsylvania's popular Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , was out campaigning for Vice President Harris, saying she's ready, and he brought up the debate. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JOSH SHAPIRO (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 She's not only ready, she's damn ready.\par \par \uc2 VOTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah!\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JOSH SHAPIRO (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 And you know who else knows she's ready? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows she's ready. And, you know, you know how I know this? 'Cause he's afraid to debate her now. Do you see that?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michigan's Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  right there by his side on that stage, also campaigning for the Vice President. And, and Rachel, Vice President Harris expected to pick her running mate by August 7th. That's just a little more than a week from now.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, David. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has to make this decision, and she has to make it quickly. We are told that the vetting process is now fully underway. Sources say that Harris is considering roughly a dozen Democrats for the job, but among the leading contenders is Pennsylvania Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  as well as Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 . Someone who is not going for this position, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . The Governor of Michigan said she plans to finish out her term as governor in that state, David.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020240803ek7t00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 2,722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2024 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 2024, BIDEN, HARRIS, DELEGATES, JEFFRIES, PELOSI, OBAMA, VP, PICK, TRUMP, VANCE\par \par \uc2 MOORE, BIDEN, RACE, IPSOS, POLL, HARRIS, GOP, AD, DEI, IMMIGRATION, VP, PRITZKER\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU, ELECTION, TRUMP, BIDEN, HARRIS, REUTERS, IPSOS, POLL, VOTES, VANCE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ADDRESS, POLL, HARRIS, CAMPAIGN,TRUMP, VANCE, BORDER, INFLATION, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's been the honor of my life to serve as your president. But in the defense of democracy, which is at stake, I think it's more important than any title. Nothing, nothing can come in the way of saving our democracy. That includes personal ambition. So, I've decided the best way forward is to pass the torch to a new generation.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden addressing the nation on Wednesday. Let's bring in our Powerhouse Roundtable, former DNC chair Donna Brazile, former special assistant to President Trump Mark Lotter, NPR White House correspondent Asmad Khalid, and PBS Newshour White House correspondent Laura Barron-Lopez. Welcome to all of you. It seems a lot longer ago that just last week, Donna Brazile, that we were sitting here at this exact time talking about the growing calls for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to step aside. He obviously did that, said he's passing the - torch to a new generation, but he was really pushed out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN STEPS ASIDE AFTER WEEKS OF SPECULATION, URGING\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, Martha, when I sat here last Sunday, all I was thinking about, and Mark, you'll appreciate this. I was thinking about the delegates because later that afternoon, the Credentials Committee, we had a vote to confirm the delegates. He decided to step back a few minutes before our vote, but still, his delegates held, and the money didn't dry up. It's been a very interesting week, a selfless act of patriotism on behalf of the - President. But I wanna tell people that the Democratic Party, we're, we're the party of the future.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're not prisoners of the past. We're not sitting around writing ads or drawing a picture that's dark and dreary. We are pioneers of the future. And I think if Vice President Harris is able to go out there and speak to the American people, speak to the people who don't feel seen or respected or heard, if she can be the voice of those who wanna be a part of the circle of opportunity, then she can and will win this race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VP HARRIS GARNERS DELEGATES TO CLINCH DEMOCRATIC NOMINATION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Asma, but were you shocked, let's go back to, to President Biden. Were you shocked at either how long it took, or that he finally did it?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S DEPARTURE FROM RACE COMES 24 DAYS AFTER FIRST DEBATE\par \par \uc2 ASMAD KHALID (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I think I was shocked on two fronts, one, that he did this by a paper statement, a letter issued on social media on Sunday. We all in the newsroom had been potentially expecting some news, but I will say not in that way, on a Sunday afternoon. I was getting ready to take my kids to the pool.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) None of us were. None of us were.\par \par \uc2 ASMAD KHALID (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, that was shocking. But equally shocking to me, Martha, because I had closely covered the Vice President, Vice President Harris, for the last three years, is how quickly the Democratic Party has coalesced around her. I remember going out to events, hearing from voters, "What has she been doing?" You know, frankly, hearing calls from within the Democratic Party that she ought to be dropped from the ticket altogether. So, to witness how quickly the party, the money, the volunteers have coalesced around her, has also been a surprise to me. It is not what I heard covering her these last two, I would say, particularly the first two years of her vice presidency.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAMPAIGN: HARRIS RAISED MORE THAN $200M SINCE START OF SOLO BID\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ (PBS NEWSHOUR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 That, that's what's been stunning, and I, I've talked to Democratic organizers in Arizona, in Wisconsin, in Georgia, you know, people who are not technically attached to the Harris campaign, and they've said that the, the change is night and day, that when they were out knocking doors when Biden was the candidate, they were met with reluctant voters, voters who wondered if he was gonna drop out, ones who said they weren't sure if they were gonna vote.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ (PBS NEWSHOUR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And now when they're knocking doors in the days since, they said that the energy is totally different, it's palpable, and that these voters are much more excited, they're asking questions about Harris, and that they're saying that they think that they may actually vote again this election cycle. So it's big difference.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 But, you know, one thing I think...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Mark, I'm sure you're loving hearing all of this. But, but look, the voters in large part, did not want the Trump/Biden rematch, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/IPSOS POLL: 48% ENTHUSIASTIC FOR A NOMINEE HARRIS, 39% FOR TRUMP\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I don't think it really matters either way because, I mean, the, the donors, the volunteers, the door knockers may change. The policies don't. I mean, there was a reason why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the most unpopular president in the 70 years, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was the most unpopular vice president since they've tracking that metric. It's because of inflation and grocery prices, gas prices, the border.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS DROPPED OUT OF 2020 RACE BEFORE DEMOCRATIC PRIMARIES\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  owns all of those policies and when you look actually start looking at what her plans are, we can go back to her 2020 presidential campaign, she's even more radical than \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , defund the police, anti-fracking. She does, she believes in decriminalizing the illegal border crossing, wants seniors to wait in line for Medicare along with illegal immigrants. I mean, those are radical policies that aren't going well.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know Marc...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm gonna go right to you, Donna. Wait one second. We'll let you finish you thought. Finished? Go.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I'm ready for this. I, I'm ready for this. I'm so ready for this because you know what? I mean, we're talking about a mean old man, a mean, old former president who has not stepped back. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did the right thing for the country. He did the right thing for the party. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should now take a look at himself and say, am I the future? Am I the person who's gonna lower those gas prices? Make sure that people can make ends meet? No, he will not. We, we, we...\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 He already did it.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He did nothing of the kind.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Everything was more affordable under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He did...\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 The world was safer under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And yeah, we got COVID and, and more people died because they could not get the relief.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  than under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) More people couldn't get the relief that they needed. Look, we're, we're ready to fight this battle because the battle is between the past that the Republicans want to hold onto and the future that the Democrats are going to pass onto their children. We're ready for this fight. We're ready for on the border because you won't sign a deal that will close the border, secure the border, or even fix our broken immigration system. We're, we're ready for this fight.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Okay, Donna, I'm...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, I'm campaign mode. I'm in campaign mode.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have a preview, we, we have a preview just when you look at this, Laura, this is clearly a preview. We had a preview earlier in the show as well from the Democrats. How do you see this race playing out? I mean, you also have a candidate in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who the union mission, message has disappeared.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP PIVOTS ATTACK TOWARD HARRIS AFTER BIDEN DROPS OUT\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ (PBS NEWSHOUR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 No, it's totally disappeared. I mean, he just in his speeches yesterday, was calling \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  evil, saying, he repeatedly says that she's dumb. He even said that, that oh, people think I've changed. I haven't actually changed. I've gotten worse. So, that unity message, if it ever existed, is gone. I think that, look, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said this, the Vice President said this at her fundraiser last night. She said, I'm an underdog, and I think that's true across the board, and Democrats know that they have an uphill battle in this race.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ (PBS NEWSHOUR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 I mean, even with the excitement that they have and the enthusiasm that they have and the money that they're raising in the time that she's been in so far, she still is not necessarily jumping ahead of Trump in the polls by any means or in the battleground states. So, they're trying to really target in specific ways, on abortion, suburban women, women across the board. She, the campaign isn't necessarily totally worried about the immigration argument. They do understand that she's on her back foot with it, but that they feel as though they have the argument that Donna made which is that essentially, Republicans killed the deal that they started.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Asma, you have as you mentioned, covered \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for more than three years. You also had thoughts that in the beginning, people did not think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was ready to be president, and that there were some stumbles. How did that change?\par \par \uc2 ASMAD KHALID (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think it changed a lot around the time of the midterms and when the Supreme Court struck down Roe versus Wade, and the primary reason is instantly the Vice President became the administration's main messenger on reproductive rights. You know, Biden does not and has not historically felt as comfortable speaking out about abortion. She was rallying the troops on that issue. Democrats saw better than expected results in the midterms. And I think that Harris' fortunes changed as a result.\par \par \uc2 ASMAD KHALID (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, I want to go back to what Marc said because I firmly believe, I spent a lot of time talking to voters about inflation. I do think that the economy remains a weakness for Democrat that they have to navigate, but I think that the dynamic is different with Harris at the top of the ticket because she wants to, and she will lean heavily into abortion. And that is an issue for which I do think Republicans are on their back foot particularly with JD Vance the running mate.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I was just gonna go to you on JD Vance, not only his abortion stance which he wants to, he said he wants to make it illegal all across the nation. We know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a different stand on that. But on abortion and on generally what he has been saying this week, he says that was a long time ago. It wasn't that long ago he made those statements. It was 2021. The cat lady saying now, oh, I was just trying to say the Democrats are anti-family and anti-child.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. VANCE'S COMMENTS ABOUT WOMEN, CHILDLESS RESURFACE\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a lot I just threw at you, Marc, but...\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, yeah, no, JD Vance is obviously very pro-family and he believes that the government should be strengthening families. And we're gonna contrast that with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who is, thinks the government, not parents, should not be deciding where kids go to school. She actually wants to repeal the tax, the Trump tax cuts which doubled the child credit, made it more affordable for people. I mean, these are legitimate arguments that we're gonna have. But if, if we're gonna focus in on JD Vance saying families make, you know, our, make people think differently, I mean, that's really what he was saying, is once you have children, once you have a family, you tend to view things a little bit differently than others.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you should pay fewer taxes...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And even when you don't have children.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even when you don't have children, you do care about kids. You care about their future. I've never had one child, and yet I think I can claim hundreds of them because I love them. I want to protect them. I want to help feed them. I want to give them a head start and a healthy start. And JD Vance is against that because that's what Project 2025, he won't give kids like me who grew up in poverty. He grew up on the wrong side of the Hill. I grew up on the wrong side of the track.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she's gonna prosecute that case about the kids who need a head start and a healthy start. So we're ready. This is beyond just abortion which is a, an economic issue. It's reproductive health. It's giving women access. The fact that my nieces right now are in red states, if they have if they have, if they have a miscarriage, they'll bleed out and die before they get the health care they need. That's what this election is gonna be about, and that's why Harry can, Harris can prosecute.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Marc, I'm gonna give you 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 I know that's the issues you guys wanna, you guys wanna to fight this on. We're gonna talk about kitchen table issues, making groceries more affordable for that family.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So do we.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 Gas more affordable for that family, and stop 12 million illegal immigrants streaming across our border who by the way, are threatening the lives in some cases of those...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's down. It's down.\par \par \uc2 MARC LOTTER (FMR PRESS SECRETARY TO VP MIKE PENCE)\par \par \uc2 ...of those young children (inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. This is a, this is a preview...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Down.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...for sure of what's to come. Thanks for all of you, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Up next, exclusive new reporting on the law enforcement response to the attempted assassination of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020240812ek7s00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAST CANCER STUDY COMPARES OUTCOMES OF DIFFERENT SURGERIES\b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE WILDFIRE\par \par \uc2 WEST, PARK, SPOT, FIRE, COUNTIES, EVAC, RUSSELL, RANCH, CATTLE, SMOKE, HUMIDITY\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, ROCKETS, HEZBOLLAH, LEBANON, SOCCER FIELD, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, DAY, HARRIS, TRUMP, CAMPAIGN, VOTERS, WALZ, POLICE, VANCE, TAXPAYERS\par \par \uc2 2024, IPSOS, RATING, HARRIS, TRUMP, VANCE, RNC, DNC, VP, PICK, SHAPIRO, KELLY\par \par \uc2 SWAT, USSS, WOODS, TRUMP, SHOOTING, PLANNING, COMMUNICATION, FAILURE\par \par \uc2 BREAST, CANCER, CELLS, STUDY, SURGERY, MASTECTOMY, RISKS, SURVIVAL, SCREENINGS\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now we go to new research regarding breast cancer treatment and what it may mean for women making surgery choices. Joining us now is ABC medical correspondent and our friend, our favorite doctor, Dr. Darien Sutton. Nice to have you as always.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPARES OUTCOMES OF DIFFERENT SURGERIES\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, pretty much everyone knows someone affected by breast cancer, and this new study is looking at the different ways women are treated for breast cancer and the outcomes. Break this down for us.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, this study is focusing on the surgical procedure of mastectomies. That's technically the surgical removal of breast tissue. The goal is to reduce the risk of a new diagnosis of breast cancer later on in life. Some women, a hundred thousand women are undergoing this procedure each year, some women choosing to do the more aggressive approach of removing both or bilateral breast. And this study looks to see what is the benefit of that? It found that although it reduced the risk of breast cancer, it doesn't change the overall survival.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: RISK OF DEATH SAME AFTER SINGLE & DOUBLE-SIDED SURGERY\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In this study, they followed more than 300,000 women with a diagnosis of breast cancer and they looked at those who chose different options. And for those who got a full or bilateral breast mastectomy, they found that they did have a reduced risk of breast cancer, but the overall survival among all groups was relatively the same. Now, these are important, but it's important a factor, to understand that women have different risk factors and this wasn't truly considered in this study. So when you're making that decision, it still comes down to that patient and their provider.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what does the study mean for women who are newly diagnosed?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think, number one, the option and the understanding of a mastectomy is a personal one, it is unique, and it is complex. And when you're making that decision, you should take into effect all of the other risk factors, for example, genetic history, family history, a personal history of cancer, even a prior history of radiation, and then that goes into that decision of weighing up against the risk of surgery.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, it's unique and personal and complex for each and every person, and there is no wrong answer. Rhiannon, I think what's most important is that regardless of your decision, you make a plan for proactive screening of all cancers after that, and you want to make sure that you're staying up to date with all recommended screenings.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, very important, information. Is it surprising?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's not surprising. I would say that the risk reduction in terms of risk of breast cancer reducing is surprising or, or helpful to know, but the actual risk of death is not, and why? Because mastectomies although they remove breast tissue, they don't prevent the risk breast cancer cells going to other parts of the body. And so, when we know that, then that understands the risk, or that creates a risk for a women in general, so we have to pay attention to these factors.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Have to pay attention. Dr. Darien Sutton, thank you as always. Always a pleasure. Gio?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240809ek7s00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KYLIE KELCE GETS CANDID REVEALS PAST MISCARRIAGE & URGES SENSITIVITY\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN BOWS OUT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, ENDORSE, DEMOCRATS, NOMINATION\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, REPUBLICANS, CAMPAIGN, CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, NOMINATION, CAMPAIGN, POLLS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, NOMINATION, ENDORSEMENTS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, DEMOCRATS, DONORS, NOMINATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRE SMOKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, NOMINATION, ATTORNEY GENERAL, SENATE\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, NOMINATION, CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 SOUTHWEST, LOW ALTITUDE, ATC, INCIDENTS, \uc2 FAA\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2 , ALL-STAR, REESE, SHOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOOD THREAT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ELECTION, DROP OUT, HARRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN DROPS OUT OF 2024 RACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENDORSES VP \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  FOR DEMOCRATIC NOD\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, CALIFORNIA, DAMAGE, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WESTERN WILDFIRES RAGING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT & DRY CONDITIONS FUEL MULTIPLE, FAST BLAZES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE, PRINCE, BIRTHDAY, 11, PHOTOGRAPH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRINCE GEORGE TURNS 11\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL & KATE RELEASE NEW PHOTO TO MARK BIRTHDAY\par \par \uc2 KELCE, PREGNANCY, MISCARRIAGE, SENSITIVITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYLIE KELCE GETS CANDID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REVEALS PAST MISCARRIAGE & URGES SENSITIVITY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Please, have a seat, Lara, just come walking, strolling in like that, love it. Back now with our "GMA" cover story. Kylie Kelce, Jason Kelce's wife, addressing speculation surrounding pregnancy for herself and others and revealing something very personal that happened before their first daughter was born. Eva Pilgrim is here and has much more for us. Good morning to you, Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Robin. It's one of those questions we are told you should never ask, but for some reason, people still do. Kylie Kelce addressing pregnancy speculations, calling on people to stop asking others if they're having a baby, sharing her own loss and her journey.\par \par \uc2 KYLIE KELCE (INTERNET PERSONALITY)\par \par \uc2 I have kept my mouth shut on this for quite some time now, and my lack of filter is kicking in, and we're just going to nip this in the bud.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, Kylie Kelce speaking out shutting down recent pregnancy rumors in the media and revealing she suffered a miscarriage before the birth of her first child.\par \par \uc2 KYLIE KELCE (INTERNET PERSONALITY)\par \par \uc2 I cannot stand people writing such insensitive articles about such a sensitive topic. It really lights my fire. I had a miscarriage before Wyatt. I went in for my 13-week ultrasound, and there was no heartbeat, and I had to have a D&E a few days later.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The mother of three and wife to former \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  center Jason Kelce adding that she does not take pregnancy lightly.\par \par \uc2 KYLIE KELCE (INTERNET PERSONALITY)\par \par \uc2 We need to just be in agreement that this is not a topic that anyone needs to be first on reporting. Let the parents say it when they're good and ready.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , early pregnancy loss is common, occurring in 10 percent of known pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (OBGYN)\par \par \uc2 Now, miscarriages in general are very common, and the most common cause of a miscarriage, usually occurring in the first trimester, is because of chromosomal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts stress that when discussing pregnancy, it should be up to the expecting parents to lead the conversation.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (OBGYN)\par \par \uc2 It is important never to ask, even if you suspect, to allow a woman to share that special news with you, being comfortable with knowing that it is her news to share and being happy for her when she shares that.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and there are so many reasons we shouldn't ask people if they are pregnant. What if they had a miscarriage? What if they're having trouble getting pregnant? What if they just ate a cheeseburger? It's exciting news, and the decision on when to share that news can be complicated, so we really need to give people space to do it on their terms, guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Eva.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240815ek7m0008u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PREGNANCY & HEALTHY DIET STUDY: MOTHER'S NUTRITION LINKED TO LOWER AUSTISM RISK IN BABY\b0\par\par\uc2 874 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP-VANCE RALLY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , VANCE, RALLY, SHOOTING, BIDEN, HARRIS, VOTERS, T-SHIRT, CLYBURN, POLLS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ATTACK, CROOKS, USSS, CHEATLE, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 IT, OUTAGE, WARDLE, CROWDSTRIKE, UPDATE, FLIGHT, CANCELLATIONS, PASSENGERS\par \par \uc2 MIAMI, AIRPORT, WOMAN, CRITICAL, STABBED, SUSPECT, ARRESTED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIAMI AIRPORT STABBING INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN HOSPITALIZED IN CRITICAL CONDITION, SUSPECT ARRESTED AT THE SCENE\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, WAR, MISSILE, YEMEN, DRONE, HOUTHIS, IRAN, NETANYAHU, BIDEN, ARGAMANI\par \par \uc2 BANGLADESH, VIOLENT, PROTESTS, QUOTA, SYSTEM, CURFEW, TRAVEL, ADVISORY\par \par \uc2 MINNESOTA, TRAIN, DERAILMENT, CONSUMER, GOODS\par \par \uc2 UTAH, WEST, WILDFIRES, SMOKE, FORECAST, AIR, QUALITY, HEAT, WAVE\par \par \uc2 OLYMPICS, \uc2 WNBA\uc2 , ALL-STAR, TEAM USA, OGUNBOWALE, CLARK, REESE, STEWART, WILSON\par \par \uc2 BURLINGTON, \uc2 BEN & JERRY'S\uc2 , ICE CREAM, FOUNDERS, NICKNAME, GREEN, MOUNTAIN\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "WHEN HARRY MET SALLY"\par \par \uc2 READ, O'KEEFE, SUV, MURDER, MISTRIAL, CANNONE, COURT, DUI, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 FLASH, FLOOD, RAINFALL, BEACH, FORECAST\par \par \uc2 STUDY, PREGNANCY, DIET, AUTISM, COVID, VACCINE, BIRTH, DEFECTS, VIRUS\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now with the week's top medical news, including two studies on pregnancy that are making a lot of headlines. Dr. Darien Sutton is here to talk about both of those. And I have three children, and I remember eating a lot of Pop-tarts when I was pregnant. That was my number one pregnancy craving. But this new study is really interesting because it finds a link between eating a healthy diet, prenatal diet, and a lower risk of autism.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PREGNANCY & HEALTHY DIET\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: MOTHER'S NUTRITION LINKED TO LOWER AUTISM RISK IN BABY\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, this is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON PREGNANT WOMEN & HEALTHY DIETS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Incredibly big deal. Incredibly important news. When we look at studies like this, it's clear that it's an association, but we don't yet know a cause and effect. So this study looked at mothers who stuck to a strict healthy diet. We're talking about whole grains, fish, vegetables, and low in red meat and low in processed foods. And they found that those mothers who stuck to that strict healthy diet had a 22 percent reduced likelihood of having a child diagnosed with autism and a 24 percent reduced risk of having a child diagnosed with communication difficulty.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, although, again we see studies like this, this is an association, it does not define cause and effect. This diagnosis is very complicated, but it does believe at a point that having a better diet low in processed foods will always benefit us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Important information. And something else that has a lot of parents concerned is the impact of COVID during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID VIRUS & VACCINATION DURING FIRST TRIMESTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: NO CONNECTION TO BIRTH DEFECTS FOR BABY\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What more are we learning about that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW PREGNANCY STUDY\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another common question, do, does COVID infect, do COVID infections cause an increased risk of something called birth defects? And so, they looked at more than 300 thousand moms, some of whom had gotten vaccinated in their first trimester and some of whom have gotten naturally infected. And they found that there was no association with an increased risk of birth defects. Now, this is still important to continue to monitor, but it is an important reminder that COVID infections alone increase the mother's risk of pregnancy loss and pregnancy failure. So, this is a time, especially during this new - COVID surge to mask up and make sure you're being cautious.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's talk about COVID real quick, because I don't know about you, but I just keep hearing about people who are getting COVID now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVID-19\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID SUMMER UPTICK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: 26 STATES HAVE VERY HIGH OR HIGH LEVELS OF VIRUS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President has COVID.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What's up, what's the latest on this uptick here?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we're currently in a surge. In the emergency room, I'm seeing more patients coming in with cough, fever, runny nose, and so it's just naturally a surge. Now, are we as bad as what we were before, during Delta or the winter? Absolutely not. But this is a time where we have to make sure we carry our masks during high-risk periods and especially if you're high risk, making sure you're updated on your vaccine. And then, in the fall, there will likely be a new vaccine associated to the circulating variants and that, that one I would recommend for everyone else.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: \uc2 WNBA\uc2  ALL-STAR GAME\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Okay. Dr. Darien Sutton, thank you. Coming up on "Good Morning America," all the highlights from last night's WNBA All-Star Game.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240815ek7l00037\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT BIDEN DROPS OUT OF 2024 RACE ENDORSES VICE PRESIDENT HARRIS\b0\par\par\uc2 22,240 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And we are coming on the air tonight with breaking news in the race for the White House. The Democratic campaign turned upside down. President Biden on this Sunday dropping out of the race for president, ending his campaign, bowing to tremendous pressure growing in recent weeks from members of his own party to pass the torch to a younger generation of leaders. President Biden making his decision in the last 24 hours, releasing a letter earlier today, to "my fellow Americans." Biden calling being president, quote, "the greatest honor of my life." The President goes on to reveal, "it is in the best interests of my party and the country for me to stand down."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President facing growing calls to withdraw following his difficult debate performance against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that was 24 days ago now. The President had called it a bad night. He said he was exhausted. Then we learned in recent days he has COVID. We saw him moving slowly getting off Air Force One. He went into isolation at his home in Rehoboth Beach, Delaware. He did hold multiple Zoom calls, we have learned, with members of his family over the weekend, supporting him in whatever decision he came up with, what was best for the country.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden immediately endorsing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  late today, to be the party's nominee. The Vice President tonight now vowing to run, to win, to earn this nomination. The Democratic National Convention just under a month away. Will she get the support she needs? And who will be her pick, if she gets the nomination, for her running mate? All of this of course unfolding just days after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and JD Vance accepted the Republican nomination. The Trump campaign already responding tonight, saying if President Biden isn't fit to run, he isn't fit to serve. So there is a lot unfolding at this hour. ABC's chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce leading us off. Mary?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) 24 days after his disastrous performance on the debate stage, battling COVID and relentless pressure from members of his own party, President Biden finally giving in. Abandoning his bid for a second term with this historic Sunday night announcement. In a letter addressed to "my fellow Americans," the President writing, "it has been the greatest honor of my life to serve as your President. And while it has been my intention to seek reelection, I believe it is in the best interest of my party and the country for me to stand down and to focus solely on fulfilling my duties as President for the remainder of my term." The President adding, "I will speak to the nation later this week in more detail about my decision."\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then, minutes later, as the news was still setting in, the President endorsing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to take his place atop the Democratic ticket, calling picking her as his number two, the best decision I ever made, posting on social media today, "I want to offer my full support and endorsement for Kamala to be the nominee of our party this year. Democrats, it's time to come together and beat Trump. Let's do this." The Vice President has taken on an outsized role on the campaign trail since the debate.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Do we believe in the promise of America? And are we ready to fight for it?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight in a statement, Harris saying, "with this selfless and patriotic act, President Biden is doing what he has done throughout his life of service, putting the American people and our country above everything else." Saying, "I am honored to have the President's endorsement, and my intention is to earn and win this nomination. We have 107 days until election day. Together we will fight and together we will win." It took Biden more than three weeks to get to this point. In the days after the debate, he was defiant.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I am running and gonna win again. I am all in. My word, I am all in.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In an interview with George Stephanopoulos, the President insisted nothing short of an act of God would get him out of the race.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  come down and say, we're worried that if you stay in the race, we're gonna lose the House and the Senate, how will you respond?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I'd go into detail with them. I've spoken to all of them in detail, including \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , every one of them. They all said I should stay in the race. Stay in the race. No one said, none of the people said I should leave.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But if they do?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, it's like, they're not gonna do that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you sure?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I'm sure. Look, I mean, if the Lord almighty came down and said, Joe, get out of the race, I'd get out of the race. The Lord almighty's not coming down.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the pressure only grew. Actor and Democratic mega donor \uc2 George Clooney\uc2 , who had seen Biden just days before the debate, penning an op-ed in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," urging the President to drop out, writing, "it's devastating to say it, but the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  I was with three weeks ago at the fundraiser was not the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  of 2010. He wasn't even the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  of 2020. He was the same man we all witnessed at the debate." Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  told Biden to his face, they were concerned he would lose the election and take Democrats down with him in November. Former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  did that and more, though Biden made it clear he was firmly in the race, Pelosi going on TV, calling it still an open question.\par \par \uc2 REP NANCY PELOSI (FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 It's up to the President to decide if he is gonna run. We're all encouraging him to make that decision because time is running short.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The flood gates had opened. Ultimately 40 Democrats in Congress called on Biden to drop out of the race, urging him to do it for the party, for his legacy, for the country he loves so much.\par \par \uc2 CONGRESSMAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 ...Is there a clear path to victory here? And can we honestly say that we're (inaudible) defeating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and holding on to the Senate? (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 REP ADAM SCHIFF (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 I do not think he is the best person to go into this election. I think if he is our nominee, I think we lose.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden fighting on, refusing to give in, but in recent days, sources telling us he was willing to listen to arguments from the other side, and then that new blow, Biden testing positive for COVID, heading home to Delaware to recuperate. It was there, isolating, the first lady at his side, that sources tell us last night the President finally began to come to his decision. Today he started making calls to his Vice President and his closest aides. At 1:45 telling his senior advisers gathered for a call, and just one minute later, Biden releasing his letter to the world.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today Pelosi calling him "a patriotic American who has always put our country first and one of the most consequential Presidents in American history." Pelosi sending "love and gratitude to President Biden for always believing in the promise of America and giving people the opportunity to reach their fulfillment." Those words echoed tonight by former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who called Biden "one of America's most consequential presidents, as well as a dear friend and partner to me," and "a patriot of the highest order." \uc2 Obama\uc2  saying, "It's a testament to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's love of country and a historic example of a genuine public servant once again putting the interests of the American people ahead of his own, that future generations of leaders will do well to follow." No mention from \uc2 Obama\uc2  on whether Vice President...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in Mary Bruce live at the White House. Mary, an extraordinary 24 hours. With your sources, take us inside the Biden campaign, inside the President's thinking here. This decision was made over the course of the last 24 hours, and you paid very close attention to his language today.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and David, this, of course, was a decision the President was adamant he was not gonna make, but the pressure ultimately just too much to bear. He began to make this decision just last night. Then, of course, everything unfolding very quickly over the last 24 hours. The President huddling last night with just that inner circle of aides, of course his closest family members, the first lady by his side throughout it all, coming out today, making this historic decision and announcement that he is gonna put his personal interests aside, he said, to do what is best for the party and the country he loves so much, decided to pass the torch to the next generation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's take our viewers inside how this unfolded today. It's our understanding that President Biden and Vice President Harris spoke a number of times today. Obviously, she was clued in to this announcement, that it was coming at least at some point today. Eventually his staff was told, just minutes before, if not a minute before that message was sent?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, the President was on the phone with his senior staff just a minute, one minute before that announcement went out to the entire world. Yes, we are told he did speak several times throughout the day with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . What a tremendous whirlwind this must be for her to now not only learn that the President is stepping down, but that we are very much seeing not only the President but also other party leaders lining up behind her. Tonight, she is now running very much in this race to win it, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She says she wants to earn and win this nomination. In the meantime, Mary, one more question for you before we move on, Vice President Harris has faced a unique challenge in these past couple of very difficult weeks politically on the campaign trail for the Biden-Harris campaign, out there defending the President every single day.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There has been no daylight, David, between the Vice President and the President as all of this drama has unfolded over the last several weeks as he has faced this mounting tremendous pressure to drop out of the race. She has been firmly standing by him. Now she will be, she hopes, atop the ticket.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce, leading off our special here. Mary, thank you. I want to bring in ABC senior White House correspondent Selina Wang, she's with the President in Delaware, outside his Rehoboth Beach home. Selina, we know Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is already trying to rally support among Democratic leaders. She's been working the phones all afternoon and evening?\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is already full steam ahead. We're learning that in just these last few hours, she's been working to shore up support, making calls to Democratic leaders, party leaders, and members of Congress. Now Vice President Harris, she's already been this administration's leading voice when it comes to these issues that are central to this election, like immigration and abortion. And David, if she does become the nominee, this would be historic. She would be the first black woman and the first Asian American. She's also already been in critical meetings with President Biden throughout her vice presidency and this week she is expected to meet with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as you say, David, she is full steam ahead. She is already fundraising off of this announcement. She says she will do everything in her power to unite the Democratic party and defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Harris is clearly already hitting the ground running. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Selina, I know you're there in Rehoboth Beach. Any indication from the White House, from the Biden campaign, what was the Biden campaign, as to when they think the President will make the trip back to Washington, DC, back to the White House and when we might perhaps see, hear him address the nation?\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, I am learning from a senior administration source that the President is expected to address the nation sometime later this week, not in the coming days, I'm told later in the week. We did hear from the President's doctor today. He said he is still isolating but his symptoms are improving significantly. So that is a step up from what we heard the day before, when the doctor said he still had some hoarseness and a loose cough. The President though, he has been here for several days already. You can just imagine how difficult it was for him to make this decision, one of the hardest political decisions of his very, very long career, while he was isolated all alone and sick with COVID.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Selina Wang with us, she's in Delaware with the President. Selina, thank you. The Trump campaign already responding to President Biden's decision to step aside. The campaign preparing for Vice President Harris to become the Democratic nominee. They've already released a campaign ad tonight, going after Harris's record. So here's Rachel Scott on that for us.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's response to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's historic decision to bow out of the race tonight, attack, attack, attack. Trump, who just days ago pledged to unify the country, blasting Biden as "the worst President by far in the history of our nation," adding, "whoever the left puts up now will just be more of the same." It was just over a week ago after the attempt on Trump's life...\par \par \uc2 SECRET SERVICE AGENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Get down. Get down. Get down.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...that Trump and Biden shared what both men described as a good, respectful chat. Biden calling his rival.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I spoke with him last night. I'm grateful he's doing well, and Jill and I keep him and his family in our prayers.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Trump declaring Biden was "not fit to run for president, and is certainly not fit to serve." Now some of the nation's top Republicans following Trump's lead, calling on Biden to resign from office. House Speaker Mike Johnson saying, "If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not fit to run for President, he is not fit to serve as President. He must resign the office immediately." The Trump campaign now having to shift course though they've been preparing for weeks. Trump polling the crowd in Michigan yesterday.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 So who would you like to most run against if you are us, if we want to win? Ready? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP SUPPORTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Boooo.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP SUPPORTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Alright. I don't think we have to go too much further.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  already out with an ad attacking Vice President Harris, though she's not yet the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL VOICEOVER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Warning. The following may upset liberals who don't want voters to know the truth about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, Trump's running mate, Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2 , bluntly saying, "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been the worst President in my lifetime and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been right there with him every step of the way. She owns all of these failures."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So let's bring in Rachel Scott for our primetime special. You've been covering this race with the President. You've been out there at the Trump rallies. You were there for that awful rally in Butler, Pennsylvania that we watched transpire in the last week and a half. Let me ask you, the Trump campaign, former President Trump, were they actually hoping that the candidate they'd be running against would be President Biden?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, David. This was a curveball that they were certainly preparing for, but this is not a curveball that they wanted. Given all the questions about President Biden's age, mental fitness, also that debate performance, they thought \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was best suited against President Biden. Now they are sort of scrambling, trying to readjust to it being possibly Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Big questions ahead on whether or not we see possibly Harris, if she is the - Democratic nominee, face off against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on that debate stage. A reminder here that Harris is a former prosecutor, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She is a prosecutor. I know Democrats who have supported her publically already, have pointed to her time as Attorney General in California, her time on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , and say she would be a formidable opponent at least on the debate stage. The former president tonight indicating that perhaps the debate, he's not necessarily gonna show up for. I don't want to speak for him, but there have been some hints at that tonight, so we'll have to see if there is a debate, if she is the nominee, the two of them will meet on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, there's one planned for ABC. We'd love to see it still play out. We'll wait and see in the coming weeks and months. In the meantime, what is their playbook if the nominee is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at the end of this convention?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're gonna focus in on really what she concentrated on during this administration. So attacking her specifically on the border. She was sort of in the lead of heading up the situation addressing the migrant crisis at the border. So expect a lot of attacks on immigration. But also, on the Democratic side, remember, Harris is a leading voice on the issue of abortion rights. And that's why Democrats also feel like she'll be very effective on this issue, an issue they hope defines this election in November.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she sees a stark contrast with the running mate chosen by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , particularly when it comes to abortion rights. She's already been out on the campaign trail on that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. When you look at Senator JD Vance who is obviously serving as former Vice President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's (sic) running mate, he has been very vague on what he supports in terms of abortion restrictions. We know that he has pushed back against the restrictions in his own state. He's even raised the possibility that he wouldn't support exceptions for rape or incest. Expect Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to continue to hammer him hare on those issues.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One more question before I let you go, Rachel, so inside the Trump campaign tonight, not only are they working with the calculation that they could be up against Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  here. Given the endorsements the Democratic party looking as though it's coalescing behind her. She says she wants to earn this, to win this, but if it is her, they are also trying to figure out the second part of that calculation, which would be, who is her running mate gonna be.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And a lot of Democrats are saying, look at the battleground states. These are the states that are gonna decide the election. A lot of Democrats already weighing in, talking about Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro, for instance, who they believe can be very critical to serving as a possible running mate should Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  be the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Josh Shapiro, obviously very popular first term governor in Pennsylvania. Rachel, thank you. That's where we're gonna take our viewers next. So if Democratic leaders, they say Biden's decision, many of them say, to step aside, will help energize the party in this sprint to the finish here in November. If the Vice President, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , prevails in this nomination process, the question, as Rachel just mentioned, who will be her running mate. The Trump campaign waiting to try to figure out what that will look like. If she doesn't get the nomination, who would try to challenge her at the nomination as well? All of this is unchartered territory. So we asked our chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  to take a look.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She doesn't have a single pledged delegate yet, but Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is quickly emerging as the presumptive Democratic nominee. Even before Harris made it official that she would be running to replace Biden at the top of the ticket, she had locked up key endorsements, starting with Biden himself and former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . Members of Congress quickly endorsing, too.\par \par \uc2 CONGRESSMAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's in the interests of continuity, stability and unity for the Democratic party to clearly coalesce around Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Including key figures in the House, the political arm of the Congressional Black Caucus, and \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , widely seen as the most important endorsement Biden himself received four years ago. And more than a dozen senators, some of the party's top donors, too. Many of them who had cut off support for Biden have quickly come out to endorse Harris. But, again, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  doesn't have a single pledged delegate yet. Shortly after Biden bowed out, DNC chairman \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  declared that the party's process for picking his successor, quote, "will be governed by established rules and procedures."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's unclear if there will be any major challenge to Harris. Attention is already turning to who she could choose as a running mate. Some of them potential contenders, including Josh Shapiro, the Governor of Pennsylvania. Roy Cooper, Governor of North Carolina, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , Governor of New Jersey, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , Governor of Kentucky. Governor of Maryland, \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 . Governor of Illinois, \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , and Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 . Also potentially on the list, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  from the battleground state of Michigan, who told George just days ago...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 I think Michigan is always gonna be a state that you can't take your eye off of. People in Michigan are good, hard working people. We expect our leaders to be as good if not better, and work just as hard if not harder. And it's always gonna be a close race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, you're watching ABC News live coverage tonight of President Biden's decision to step aside in this race for president. John Karl with me now. Of course, John, this assumes that she is able to fight and to win this nomination, this discussion of who she would pick as a running mate. Obviously a lot of people are going there already. And I was curious, the list that you just presented for our viewers. Josh Shapiro, the Governor of Pennsylvania, Roy Cooper from North Carolina, \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  from Arizona, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  from Michigan, no surprise that we're talking about key battleground states with many of these names you just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. Look, the running mate is not only about trying to win over one of those battleground states, but it certainly doesn't hurt. And it's notable that the Democrats have significant stars in their party in each of those battleground states you mentioned. But look, I think that you see how the entire dynamics of this race, David, have changed with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  getting out. And what you hear now from Democrats across the board is they are breathing a sigh of relief. They get to have a reset for this race and on the Republican side, you can clearly see some anxiety, clearly in the statements you've heard today from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself. That unity tone gone.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) John, have they sort of flipped the script on the Republicans tonight?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, they have. They were ready to run against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and, frankly, you know, with all of the attention on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's age, now we have a new oldest party nominee in the history of American politics, and it's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really interesting, so the age question, the fitness question now is squarely focused on the former President, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , for those who are moving forward and are looking at age as an issue. Even President Biden had always said, it's a perfectly fair question. John, you were on the air with me all throughout the Republican National Convention last week. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told you that he had thrown out the script, it was gonna be about unity after that horrific event in Pennsylvania, that he survived the rally in Butler, Pennsylvania. We took note that he did speak of unity, particularly at the beginning of the speech.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But then the speech took a turn and a very long turn, and it was a reminder for those who might have been looking for a speech solely about unity, that many of the other things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  talked about during his acceptance speech at the convention were very common sort of arguments he makes on the campaign trail. And Democrats, we had asked that night after the speech while we were doing analysis among us, you included, John, whether or not Democrats would look at that and say, well, wait a minute, that actually is a familiar playbook?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, absolutely. The unity talk, that was Monday that I spoke to former President Trump. He had said he had ripped up the old convention speech and that when he came out on Thursday that week that it would be a note of unity. And you saw a degree of unity during the beginning of the convention. There was no talk of a stolen election. There were no real hard-edged attacks or very few hard-edged attacks on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That was gone by the time he got halfway through or a third of the way through \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's speech. It was gone when he was out campaigning in Michigan with JD Vance over the weekend, just yesterday. But now I think you really see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the tone of his social media postings. He is fully back to the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that we knew well and saw well in both of his previous presidential campaigns and frankly as president.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) John, let me ask you about the campaign as it moves forward. Obviously they're assuming on the Trump side that it's gonna be \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  given members of the Democratic party who have come out to endorse her, former President Clinton, former Secretary of State Clinton among the many. I did take note here that the former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , they have not come out to endorse, they have very warm, supportive words for President Biden and what they say he did for the nation when it mattered most. Also talking about his decision to step down, and the weight of that decision and what that signals from him. But in the meantime, what do you think that they make as far as moving forward, the calculation on the Trump side as far as going after \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and trying to figure out who she might select if it is her?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, first of all, I would not take the lack of an endorsement from \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  or \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as a real, really about having doubts about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Look, Pelosi and \uc2 Obama\uc2  are really arguably the two most important senior - statesmen in the Democratic party right now. There needs to be a process going forward. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  herself has referred to a process going forward of not simply being anointed, but having to be - the delegates have to vote for her, as we pointed out.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She doesn't have a single pledged delegate. So I think that Pelosi and \uc2 Obama\uc2  are playing the role as party leaders and ultimately will be entirely and very enthusiastically on board with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , assuming she emerges as the nominee, which it sure seems she is gonna do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that was a two-parter. You addressed part of it. This would seem as though it is part of the strategy that they're fully aware of optics here involving a man who was in public life for decades, served as Vice President under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Now deciding to step down, which is an historic decision of course in and of itself. They're aware that the Vice President has to fight for this. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has signaled that as well. And your suggestion here is that they're fully aware of that, that's why they're waiting here?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, absolutely. And this is also very important, as you just pointed out, this is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's moment. This was an extraordinary move. It was his decision alone and he has now just given up something that politicians never give up. I mean, he's President of the United States. He's his party nominee, presumptive nominee. He's got all those delegates. 14 million votes and he gave it all away. You know, he was pressured, let's face it, he was pressured to do so, but it was his decision. And ultimately, it was a decision of self-sacrifice to pass the baton, and he clearly didn't really want to pass.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about that. Deeply personal decision, obviously an historic decision. John, one last question before I let you go, because I always pepper you with questions even if we're not on TV. It was clear that President Biden wanted to see the data, he wanted to see the polling. Do we have any reporting yet on whether or not he was shown polling involving Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , how she would perform up against Trump, not only nationally, but in those key battleground states?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know that the - campaign, the Biden/Harris campaign had gone into the field to do polling. I haven't seen exactly what that polling shows, but I also know that when \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  had the meeting you and I spoke about last week, when he went to Rehoboth to meet with President Biden and to urge him to make this decision to get out of the race, that he went armed with polling showing that by staying in the race, he was doing damage to Democratic Senate candidates in the states that they would need to win to keep control of the Senate. So there was a lot of polling going up to Rehoboth, Delaware, addressed to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I don't know exactly what the polling he saw on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In some of those key Senate races, the Democrats are actually outperforming President Biden.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But in recent weeks, they were starting to see some erosion even in that respect.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can outperform the top of the ticket a little bit but there's only so much. If the President's really going down, that's the top of the ticket, made it very hard for those Senate candidates, those sitting senators running for reelection to win. That's why some of the first senators you saw, David, come out to say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , publically to say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  needs to get out were the senators running in tough states. \uc2 John Tester\uc2  in Montana, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  in Ohio, chief among them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) John Karl with us here tonight for our special coverage. John, thank you. So, what's this gonna look like? We are under a month now until the Democratic National Convention, hard to believe. We just left Milwaukee and the Republican National Convention. The big story of this weekend, of course, President Biden deciding to step down in this race for President. Let's bring in our senior national correspondent, Terry Moran. And Terry, it's kind of an impossible question to answer, but what will this convention look like? There have been all sorts of ideas floated about whether or not we would see sort of a mini primary, if you will. It would appear at least so far that there has been sort of a coalescing around \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  from several big names from within the Democratic party and also some key Democratic donors.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, David. That means we could get a normal convention on August 19th, but there's a lot of time between now and then. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , President Biden has endorsed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to be the Democratic party nominee for president. But that's not the end of the story because he's not the decision maker. As you and John Karl were just talking about, the Democratic delegates to the Democratic convention, they are the decision maker. There - are more than 4,500 of them. About 3900 of them are already pledged to vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but they, all of them, are now free under Democratic party rules to vote their conscience, and that means that Vice President Harris or anyone else is gonna have to earn a simple majority, 1,895 delegates, to get the nomination.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The first test of that is gonna come in early August and that is when the Democratic party is expected to have a virtual vote, a virtual roll call, it was recently planned to deal with an Ohio state ballot deadline that came early. They may use it this time to test her strength. If she gets that 1895 delegates, that's it, the convention has its nominee and that's that. If she doesn't, then you have the prospect of an open convention. No one comes to Chicago with a majority of the delegates. That hasn't happened since 1968, and that didn't go too well. Because what it means is constant lobbying of each and every delegate. It's gonna mean maybe back room deals, it's gonna be, Lord knows what. And the Democrats desperately want to avoid that. And it looks like, as you say, the party is gonna coalesce around Vice President Harris. She has to earn those delegates' votes. They're the decision makers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is there any suggestion that this turns into a mini primary, at this point?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, they do have time. She's need other candidates. So far, I believe only \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  has said that she wants to be represented at the Democratic National Convention. So that mini primary would have to be organized in a hurry. It would consist primarily of a few televised debates region by region. That was the original design when people were talking about whether or not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would drop out. If they have that time, if they have those candidates, that may be one way for Vice President Harris or anyone else to earn the nomination with the delegates who would be the primary audience.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But also with the country. One of the things that Democrats lack here is a candidate who was tested in the crucible of those primaries. Look at how the Republicans went about it. Obviously, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the frontrunner, but you saw all those other Republicans lined up and at the end of the day, after all those debates, it was \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  who emerged from that crowd as Trump's main rival. Who would have predicted that from the beginning? Primaries test candidates, they show the country who these people are. Vice President Harris is a known commodity. No one else is.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry, stick with us here. I'll get you to react to this, because we're just learning here, Rachel Scott is now reporting that Senator Joe Manchin is now considering reregistering as a Democrat to run against Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the presidential nomination, according to two sources with knowledge of his decision. He had indicated earlier that it was time to unify, has changed sort of his position as the day has gotten further along here on the news that President Biden is sort of stepping aside. So perhaps that would be the first name that people at home might know. Joe Manchin saying he's at least considering challenging Harris. The question, Terry, you know, people had long pointed to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and have said that chaos sort of follows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Does any of this sort of messiness or in the moment attention actually help Democrats with voters sort of focus on them for a while?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a great point, David. Voters really didn't want a repeat of Trump and Biden. After the convention. we hit the road, Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, talked to voters. One of the things we heard from everybody, Democrats, Independents, undecided, Republicans, that they didn't, they weren't happy about the choice before them. Democrats may now be able to generate some excitement about someone new. Looks like Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , could be Joe Manchin, it could be someone else. And that could help the party. The problem is, if it goes to the convention, and you have that kind of fight, the bad blood between the candidates can really wreck candidates' chances.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's what happened in 1968. That's what happened in 2016. Bernie Sanders came roaring into that convention. It was really \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and what were called then the super delegates, the party leaders that helped clinch the nomination for her. And there was bad blood. They don't want to face \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  divided. They want to face \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  united, but Manchin will be able to make his case, if he chooses, as will others. And the voters might like that. They might like to see what Vice President Harris is made of in that situation, what Manchin is made of, and anyone else. That, as I say, is the traditional way in this country. Other countries don't do it this way. But there is virtue in our long campaign. You get to know them and get to see what they are made of. In the end, it might get good ratings, and generate a little excitement for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Might generate a little excitement. And a little follow-up for you, because we know that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , again, the Vice President, has said she wants to get out there and win this nomination, earn the nomination. I mentioned Joe Manchin simply because he's sort of changed his tune late today suggesting that he might consider that. I don't want to get ahead of ourselves, because right now, a number of key Democratic leaders have come out and very quickly gotten behind the Vice President, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But even the Vice President has signaled that she wants to run and secure this nomination, earning it through the delegates, but really in front of the voters.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Terry, it's interesting. Obviously we follow all of our own reporting within ABC News, but you and I are both voracious readers of everybody else's reporting out there too. And you know, sometimes on these key political stories, you go into the comments, obviously on this, involving President Biden, should he step down, should he continue. It was really interesting in the last few days, I've seen a lot of voters across the country signal that they weren't completely comfortable with what some have said appears to be sort of the power brokers in the Democratic party putting pressure on President Biden to do this. So perhaps that is what Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is trying to signal here when she has explicitly said, I want to earn this.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That could well be. Look, this is a painful episode in American political history, in the history of the Democratic party. They had to push. They had to push \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  because the consensus of the party, of the country, was that he was too old to serve or to win, which is what's on Democrats' minds right now, so that could well be. It's also, I think, Democrats are now gonna watch every single step, every single step \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  takes, every syllable that she says. She's gonna have to meet the moment. She's gonna have to look like a president. If not, there will be Democrats who look around.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, she did not fare well when she ran in 2020. She had a great moment in the debate where she popped \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , holding him accountable for his position on bussing in the 1970s. She said, I was on one of those buses. And then everybody turned their attention to her. And she kind of evaporated. If she cannot hold her own, she will invite a challenge, and the delegates, if they get concerned about winning against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if she gets out there, - she may be a star. Has been in the past. But if she raises concerns about whether she can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you may see other people get in before that virtual vote. Time is short. Things are moving at a real fast pace, but politics is a tough business.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry, one more quick follow-up to that. You talked about her stumbles when she ran for president herself, before being selected by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to be his running mate. She's faced very stiff scrutiny as Vice President. On the flip side of that, just to ask you about this. There are some out there who say the scrutiny that comes along with being sort of the number two, the Vice President, is very difficult. It's not always a clearly defined role and perhaps the bar is a - little low here as far as actually hearing from her. Being the top of the ticket is very different from being the supporting character, the supporting cast member who stands back, watches it and defends the President, which she's done. A real opportunity, much more scrutiny. Much more difficult running when you are the top of the ticket. But it comes with greater exposure, greater opportunity to let people see what you can do.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. Very different being a vice presidential candidate than being a presidential candidate, as you point out. And there have been plenty of vice presidents who thought they were goings to be president, and didn't become president. \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 , and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and countless others. Now, Vice President Harris has the blessing of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  here. She's got the party coalescing around her, but at the end of the day, she really has to demonstrate that whatever happened to her in the primary, she was one of the first to drop out well before Iowa, that that was a fluke and she's a different person, she certainly has been tested as vice president.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She's been given some important responsibilities. She's close to power. She gets it now in a way she couldn't have when she was a United States senator. If voters see that confidence, that readiness to seize, to have power, then they'll go with her. But if they see her stumble again, look, it's - we're in uncharted waters obviously right now. There are, believe me, sharks in those waters. They are willing to get in and if they can, steal a march on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and get ahead of her perhaps, but that's a long way off. Right now the Democratic party is talking about unity, and you can feel it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so funny you say that. I'm gonna wrap this up, Terry and move on. We have a lot of others to get to with some new reporting here. But on your point there, we've got a lot of time. It's just funny to hear that, as we're in July. We know there isn't, in a conventional campaign, a lot of time when you are looking at November staring down election day. But in this country, with this 24 hour news cycle that we've had in the year 2024, every day it seems like it's a new episode. So your point on, there's a lot of time to sort of change the narrative here, that is certainly true particularly in these times.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Might not have been true four years, eight years, in presidential campaigns past. But that is the country we're living in now. Terry, stick by and we'll get back to you later in the evening. I do want to get to this news that's crossing moments ago. California's governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has now endorsed Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . He said, tough, fearless, tenacious, with our democracy at stake, and our future on the line, no one is better to prosecute the case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's dark vision and guide our country in a healthier direction than America's Vice President, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was talking about this with Rachel Scott, this notion that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was an attorney general in California. He talks about her role as a prosecutor. She, of course, was on the Senate Judiciary Committee for Supreme Court nominations. The country has seen her in that role. It's been some time, though, seeing her front and center, as sort of a prosecutor, prosecuting a case. Now, if given this opportunity, if she earns it, in her words, this nomination, that will be her task moving forward. When we come back here, the question many of you might have at home, the state of the race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So if it is Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , how does she do in the polls against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , not only nationally but in some of the key battlegrounds. Rick Klein is standing by with the numbers. Martha Raddatz also has new reporting inside sort of the mindset of President Biden in Delaware this weekend, recovering from COVID. A deeply personal, agonizing decision after decades in public service, to step aside. What is his frame of mind tonight? What was the evolution to get here. She has new reporting here in a moment. Our live coverage of President Biden's decision to withdraw from the race for president will continue in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back tonight. ABC News live coverage of President Biden's historic decision to step down in this race for president. I want to bring in former head of the DNC, ABC News contributor Donna Brazile. Donna, always good to have you. I would have reached out to you but we're on the air, so I'll do it here on the air for our viewers to listen to as well. This virtual roll call that's coming in the coming weeks, even before the convention begins, that is still on track, and do you believe it'll be the Vice President, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who is the eventual nominee here?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well first of all, I'm very saddened, like many Democrats, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decided to step back, but I'm also very excited that he has passed the torch on to a proven leader, someone who is battle tested. She has been Vice President for the last three and a half years. She has met with foreign leaders. She has done the job. And I know she's ready to take on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . With regards to our nominating process. David, as you well know, there are several states that have deadlines to get on the ballot before our convention is gaveled in on August 19th, so we're trying to ensure that our nominee will have the ability to get on the ballot in all 50 states and the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we speak, my friends are sending me a note. So I want to let John Karl know that tomorrow the Nebraska delegates will make their consideration. Alabama has made theirs, my beloved home state of Louisiana. I'm gonna get on the phone with all of the DC delegates to ensure, ensure that she's able to get the delegates that she needs to have her name placed in nomination. This process is open. It's open and transferable. Look, the President today at 1:45, I may not have the right time, but he decided to drop out of the race. He also decided to endorse \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's name is not on the ballot with regards to delegates.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So she will have to secure their nomination. She needs 300 delegates to put her name into nomination and she will need over 1980 delegates in order to be the next nominee of the Democratic party. We're gonna work very hard. I support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I know there may be some other candidates that decide to get in the race, but I will support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in an open and transparent process.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So just to explain this to the viewers, Donna, and you did a great job there, but I just want to make sure everybody'd clear on this, even before the nomination, because there are some states and the dates on the calendar, you have to make sure that their delegates are heard even before the convention so that their numbers count, too, in coming up with a nominee. So even though that process begins now, some are gonna say, well wait a minute, we just heard the Vice President say I want to earn this, I want to win this nomination. If the process begins so soon, where does the earning and winning come, or is that just simply part of this sort of rollout of this process?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, as you well know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as his running mate in 2020. They received over 81 million votes. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  this year as his running mate, and they received over 14 million votes. Those votes are now translated into delegates. There are over 4,000 delegates to the 2024 Democratic convention, but in order to get on the ballot in all 50 states, and the District of the Columbia, the nominee of the party must be handed this nomination before August 7th. And when I saw August 7th, we have to take into consideration the Ohio deadline, also the Washington state deadline, the Oklahoma, Montana, as well as the great state of California, with 54 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Their deadline is actually on the night that the next nominee would - be given that title. So we want to get this done. We want to ensure that the nominee's on the ballot, but first, the person has to, they have to earn the delegates. They have to earn the votes of the delegates. That is what the Vice President is gonna announce. She's calling up - delegations. She's calling up delegate leaders. She's calling up the leaders because she wants to secure the nomination. She's working the phone. I suspect that she'll be working it all night until she gets to 1980 votes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you've been an informal adviser to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  along the way in her political career. It's really interesting, Donna, and thanks for the explanation. You mentioned Ohio, Washington, Oklahoma, Montana among the states that have deadlines, even before the Democratic National Convention. So take our viewers inside the convention, if you will. I know you don't have sort of a crystal ball, kind of impossible at this point because we're in uncharted territory here. But it's less than a month away. By night four of the convention, which of course is the big night with the nominee, do you believe it's gonna be the Vice President?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think she has a head start for two reasons. She crisscrossed this country. She was able to connect with voters, and like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020, she was able to get so much support out there. There are people who are literally calling me, David, saying, how can I sign on? I said, we need pledge delegates. We need delegates. We encourage everyone to go out and raise some money and again, to take their assignments in terms of helping to build a kind of momentum. David, we have spent three weeks and three days contemplating whether or not the President of the United States would step back.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He decided today, about 1:45. I was on the call at 2:00 to certify the delegates. We certified the delegates around 2:45 pm today, Eastern time. Now that we have an official list of delegates, then it is up to the Vice President or anyone else who wants to toss their hat or gloves into the ring, to go out there and secure the delegates they need in order to get this nomination. We're encouraging them to do it fast, because I don't think we're gonna slow down the process, we've just got to quicken the pace, so to speak.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Any chance it's gonna be a contested convention?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I don't know the candidates. I talked to Joe Manchin a few minutes ago. He wanted to inquire about the process. I had to remind him that in order to put his name in nomination, he has to sign a form that he's a Democrat. He left the party a couple of months ago...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did he say he was willing to do that?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to make sure that every- well, David, I had to come and talk to you. You're actually my (inaudible) tonight. So I had to, (inaudible) later...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You put me before Manchin, is that what you're saying?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'll get back with him. Look, I know so many members of this party. It's a great party. It's gonna unify around a nominee. But if it's \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the Vice President knows she has to earn it. We're Democrats. We make you work for it. We make you tell us what you want to do, and how you're gonna do it, so she's gonna have to step up and run this campaign the way \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has run his campaign. And I do believe that she will be able to finish the job that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  started.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna, as you know, you were part of the coverage last week. We spent a week on the Republicans last week, now we have this major story on the Democratic side. You know, you and I have both obviously studied the polling here. We know that the numbers were very high when voters were polled, particularly among the Democrats, about concerns when it comes to President Biden's age, fitness for another four years, but you also know, Donna, I always love to flip the coin. I'm mindful that 14 million people did vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in this primary. What do you say to voters out there tonight who are upset, who are saying, well, wait a minute, I voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and he's now dropped out?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, they were very aware that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  were running together. Before the primary season began, the Vice President, along with the President, they were raising money. They were building infrastructure. They were opening up campaign offices. She has been out of this city practically every week, two, three times a week, trying to help the President secure the nomination and raise the money. So I think the voters out there are familiar with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . They're familiar with her record.They're familiar with the knowledge that she has brought to this ticket. Don't forget, in 2020, with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  support, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was able to secure 306 electoral votes, 81 million votes. So I'm confident that she's gonna work very hard to try to pull together the Democratic party over the next 72, 76 hours and she's gonna secure that nomination.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've seen some of the polling. The national polling, and some of the polling, it shows that she might do a little bit better than President Biden up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The question, Donna, is in the key battlegrounds, as you know, and in some of those states, there are a couple where she does a little bit better. But in some of the states in the key battlegrounds, she doesn't do as well as President Biden. Can she pull off these battlegrounds?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think with the help of President Biden and many, many others you're seeing the type of support that continues to trickle in from all across the Democratic party. I do believe she will be able to close the gap and once again, bring the Democratic party to victory in the fall. But this is also about uniting not just the Democratic party. We've started talking last week about uniting the country. I hope that she will come forward with her message, her vision, because the country really wants change.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alright, Donna Brazile with us for our live coverage. President Biden's decision to back away, this race for president. Donna, thank you. Stick with us here. We're gonna continue with other members of the team. Rick Klein, our political director, Washington bureau chief. And Rick, you heard what I just asked Donna, you know, it all comes down to the polls. We know that President Biden wanted to see some of this data. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  performs about the same, maybe a little better nationally up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There are some bright spots I think on the Democratic side for the Democrats in some of the battlegrounds, but make no mistake, the numbers that I've seen show that she has a very stiff challenge ahead if she's the nominee in these key battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's exactly right, David. This does not solve all of - the Democrats' problems, in fact, it could make them worse in some states, if you believe the very early battleground state polls. There hasn't been much out there. We have seen a national poll that came out just in the last couple of days that had a dead tie between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , sorry, between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We also had some new polling out from ABC News and Ipsos that showed favorability ratings for some of the major candidates. Check that out. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's number a little bit better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but not as good as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Trump got a significant boost out of his convention and out of the assassination attempt. These numbers still have to be concerning to Democrats. But they have a lot of time to try to build up Harris. And it was significant in your interview with Donna right there, that they are gonna continue to push to have a virtual roll call ahead of the actual convention. The deadline is actually gonna be August 7th, according to Donna, who's part of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 . That pushes things up. That tells you some of the urgency now among Democrats. They want to get this squared away, to stop any challenges that could happen on the convention floor. That remains the plan of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  at this hour, to try to get this all wrapped up in a bow before they arrive in Chicago. That is gonna be a tough task with Senator Machin and others circling about other opportunities.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HYPOTHETICAL VOTE PREFERENCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAVORABILITY RATINGS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have just a few moments left in this hour. Thank you, Rick. We have a whole hour coming up. But I want to give Martha Raddatz the last word tonight, because Martha, with just a few moments left, the mindset tonight for President Biden, these last 24 hours have been deeply personal and difficult for not just the President, but his family.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For all of them. We saw how badly \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wanted to stay in this race. We were reporting last week that he was lashing out at people who would suggest he'd leave. He truly thought he could get over the hump here and make up for that disastrous debate. So it was a difficult decision. He does not think, according to a senior official, that he is mentally impaired. He thinks this is all about energy, but he wouldn't want to say mentally impaired. He's still the President.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha Raddatz still with us this hour. Martha, thanks. And thanks to our entire team. They're all still reporting this story out. We have another hour in primetime. President Biden stepping down in this race for president. What does it mean moving forward against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and if the Vice President becomes the eventual nominee on the Democratic side. I'm David Muir. I'll see you just moments from now, for another hour in primetime.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, an ABC News primetime special. Live coverage, President Biden bowing to pressure and withdrawing from the race for the White House. The President tonight calling it in the best interest of the party and the country, and giving his full endorsement to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , upending the race for president. Tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican party launching attacks on the Vice President, assuming she's the nominee. A special edition of ABC News and our coverage of the race for president begins right now.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Tonight, President Biden stands down. The race for the White House. The President's historic decision to withdraw from the race against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Pressure mounting for weeks following his poor performance at the debate. The flood gates opening, with more Democrats calling on him to drop out. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  now in the spotlight. What will Democrats do now? Will they rally behind her or take their fight to the Democratic National Convention 29 days away? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican party already on the attack. How will American voters respond? This is an ABC News special. Now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN STANDS DOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening again on this consequential night. We are coming back on the air this evening with the breaking news in the race for the White House. The campaign turned upside down tonight. President Biden on this Sunday, dropping out of the race for president, bowing to tremendous pressure that had been growing in recent weeks from people within his own party, to pass the torch to a younger generation of leaders. President Biden making his decision in the last 24 hours, releasing a letter earlier today to "my fellow Americans."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Biden calling being president, quote, "the greatest honor of my life." The President goes on to reveal, "it is in the best interest," in his opinion, "of my party and the country for me to stand down." The President facing growing calls to withdraw from the race, following his difficult debate performance against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that was 24 days ago. The President had called it a bad night, saying he was exhausted. Then last week, during the Republican National Convention, we learned President Biden had COVID. He came off the campaign trail. We saw him moving slowly there, getting off Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He went into isolation to get better at his home in Rehoboth Beach, Delaware. Over the weekend, he held multiple Zoom calls with his family, they were supporting him in whatever he decided would be best for the country. President Biden revealing that decision today and then immediately endorsing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to be the Democratic party's nominee. The Vice President vowing to run, to win, to earn this nomination tonight. The Democratic National Convention less than a month away now. So many questions to get to in this hour. Will she get the support she needs? Will the party unify behind her?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if so, who would her pick be for running mate? All of this unfolding just days after President Trump and JD Vance accepted the Republican nomination. Tonight, the Trump campaign already responding, saying if President Biden isn't fit to run, they're arguing now he isn't fit to serve in these coming months. So again, a lot unfolding at this hour. I want be to bring in our chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce leading us off. First off, Mary, let's go inside the last 24 hours. What your sources have told you about the evolution of this decision from the President. Because we know that he made this decision just over the weekend here. This wasn't something he had in his mind late last week. There was increasing pressure, he was dealing with COVID, he was on the campaign trail, came off it to recover. But very much indicated he was still in this fight. So take us through the weekend, the evolution, the private meetings with those closest to him. How did this work?\par \par \uc2 REP NANCY PELOSI (FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, this was the decision that the President did not want to make. For weeks he was adamant that he wasn't going anywhere, that he was all in on fighting for a second term, that he was gonna beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but the political pressure, it was clear. It was just gonna be too much to bear. And then just over the last 24 hours, the President, isolated with COVID, recuperating. Huddling in his Delaware beach house with just his closest advisers, those who have been by his side for decades. And of course, his wife and family members.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, the President just alerting his staff of this, one minute before he made it public to the world. The President ultimately saying that he had to put his personal interests aside to do what he believes is best for the party and the country that he loves so dearly.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, 24 days after his disastrous performance on the debate stage, battling COVID and relentless pressure from members of his own party, President Biden finally giving in, abandoning his bid for a second term with this historic Sunday night announcement. In a letter addressed to "my fellow Americans," the President writing, "it has been the greatest honor of my life to serve as your president, and while it has been my intention to seek reelection, I believe it is in the best interest of my party and the country, for me to stand down, and to focus solely on fulfilling my duties as President for the remainder of my term." The President adding, "I will speak to the nation later this week in more detail about my decision."\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then minutes later, as the news was still setting in, the President endorsing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to take his place atop the Democratic ticket, calling picking her as his number two "the best decision I ever made." Posting on social media, "today I want to offer my full support and endorsement for Kamala the be the nominee of our party this year. Democrats, it's time to come together and beat Trump. Let's do this." The Vice President has taken on an outsized role on the campaign trail since the debate.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Do we believe in the promise of America? And are we rely to fight for it?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight in a statement, Harris saying, "with this selfless and patriotic act, President Biden is doing what he has done throughout his life of service, putting the American people and our country above everything else." Saying, "I am honored to have the President's endorsement, and my intention is to earn and win this nomination. We have 107 days until election day. Together we will fight and together we will win." It took Biden more than three weeks to get to this point. In the days after the debate, he was defiant.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I am running and I am gonna win again. I am all in. My word, I am all in.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In an interview with George Stephanopoulos, the President insisted, nothing short of an act of God would get him out of the race.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  come down and say, "We're worried that if you stay in the race, we're gonna lose the House and the Senate," how will you respond?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I'd go into detail with them. I've spoken to all of them in detail, including \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , every one of them. They all said I should stay in the race. Stay in the race. No one said, none of the people said I should leave.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But if they do?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, it's like, they're not gonna do that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you sure?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I'm sure. Look, I mean, if the Lord almighty came down and said, Joe, get out of the race, I'd get out of the race. The Lord almighty's not coming down.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the pressure only grew. Actor and Democratic mega donor \uc2 George Clooney\uc2 , who had seen Biden just days before the debate, penning an op-ed in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," urging the President to drop out, writing, "it's devastating to say it, but the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  I was with three weeks ago at the fundraiser was not the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  of 2010. He wasn't even the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  of 2020. He was the same man we all witnessed at the debate." Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  told Biden to his face, they were concerned he would lose the election and take Democrats down with him in November. Former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  did that and more, though Biden made it clear he was firmly in the race, Pelosi going on TV, calling it still an open question.\par \par \uc2 REP NANCY PELOSI (FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 It's up to the President to decide if he is gonna run. We're all encouraging him to make that decision because time is running short.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The flood gates had opened. Ultimately 40 Democrats in Congress called on Biden to drop out of the race, urging him to do it for the party, for his legacy, for the country he loves so much.\par \par \uc2 CONGRESSMAN (MALE)\par \par \uc2 What's on everybody's mind, is there a clear path to victory here? And can we honestly say that we're on a path to defeating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and holding on to the Senate and holding on the to the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 REP ADAM SCHIFF (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 I do not think he is the best person to go into this election. I think if he is our nominee, I think we lose.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden fighting on, refusing to give in, but in recent days, sources telling us he was willing to listen to arguments from the other side, and then that new blow, Biden testing positive for COVID, heading home to Delaware to recuperate. It was there, isolating, the first lady at his side, that sources tell us last night the President finally began to come to his decision. Today he started making calls to his Vice President and his closest aides. At 1:45 telling his senior advisers gathered for a call, and just one minute later, Biden releasing his letter to the world.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today Pelosi calling him "a patriotic American who has always put our country first and one of the most consequential Presidents in American history." Pelosi sending "love and gratitude to President Biden for always believing in the promise of America and giving people the opportunity to reach their fulfillment." Those words echoed tonight by former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who called Biden "one of America's most consequential presidents, as well as a dear friend and partner to me," and "a patriot of the highest order." \uc2 Obama\uc2  saying, "It's a testament to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's love of country and a historic example of a genuine public servant once again putting the interests of the American people ahead of his own, that future generations of leaders will do well to follow." No mention, though, from \uc2 Obama\uc2  on whether Vice President Harris should now become the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Two people who are getting behind Harris, Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . In a statement, the former President and Secretary of State calling Biden the country's "North Star," saying "we are honored to join the President in endorsing Vice President Harris, and will do whatever we can to support her." Tonight, First Lady Jill Biden forwarding her husband's announcement with a heart. Her communications director telling us the First Lady was "supportive of whatever road he chose. She's his biggest believer, champion, and always on his side."\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And from the President's son Hunter, an emotional statement. "For my entire life, I've looked at my dad in awe. How could he suffer so much heartache and yet give so much of whatever remained of his heart to others? He is unique in public life today, in that there is no distance between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the man and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the public servant of the last 54 years. I'm so lucky every night I get to tell him I love him and to thank him. I ask all Americans to join me tonight in doing the same."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Deeply personal moment for the Biden family, and we all have to be mindful of that no matter where we are on the political spectrum. For our viewers at home, you know, he's been in the public eye for many decades, Mary Bruce. This must not have been an easy decision for the President and his family, who we know were some of the people he turned to in these last few days. Sources telling you he began to come to this decision last night and he says he'll serve out the remainder of his term. He's also indicated, Mary, that he's gonna come before the American people to share more with them, how he came to this decision. Do we know when?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, we are told that the President in the coming days will be coming before cameras to explain himself this remarkable decision to the American people. And this has been, I think, as you noted, a deeply painful, frustrating experience for many, not just in his family but those closest to him, that inner circle of his advisors who have been with him throughout all of this. There is a sense tonight coming from those close to Biden, to his family, close to this campaign, of real relief. I think seeing the pressure build on him politically, seeing him, you know, going through all of this so publicly was really difficult.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you do have to remember that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spent a lifetime trying to get right here, trying to get to the White House. He spent more than 50 years in public service and in politics. He was just 29 years old when he was elected to the Senate and now he's going to leave the White House the oldest President to ever serve. He's going to explain that decision publicly to the American people in the coming days. For now, David, he remains isolating at his beach house in Delaware. We know he's been working the phones. We know he's reached out several times to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  today. What a whirlwind day she must've had and what a whirlwind this has been for the entire Biden world and the Biden family, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and on that point, Mary, this was such a closely held, personal decision for the President that his closest, you know, administration officials and certainly campaign officials did not know until just before the message went out and when it comes to the Vice President, you and your team now reporting that they did speak multiple times today. Obviously, she had to be prepared for the possibility that this would be coming but she didn't know, as far as our reporting so far, until today.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. She didn't know, either. This difficult decision, the most difficult decision of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's life, was made huddled with just a very, a few, a handful of his closest advisors over the last 24 hours. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  learning of this today and then, having multiple conversations with the President. Of course, I'm sure she has thought of this, given all of what is going on in the public conversation here but over the last several weeks, she has been out there hitting the trail hard, showing no kind of daylight, of course, between her and the President.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She has been out there championing, pushing the message of this campaign, especially reaching out to critical constituencies who they know they will need come November, especially reaching out to black voters, young black voters who polls show their support may be slipping. She's been out there hitting the top issue of abortion rights, reproductive rights, which they know will be central in this campaign and now, David, we know she is expected to hit the trail in the coming days but what a different set of circumstances it will be when she gets out there before voters, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the scrutiny will be harsh. It's much different to be potentially at the top of the ticket, obviously, than to be the number two. But in these last couple of weeks, Mary, when this campaign was facing some very difficult days, that very tough debate performance from President Biden up against former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and yet, the Vice President faced a very unique test in just the last couple of weeks before she faces this even larger test now ahead of her.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But these last couple of weeks, knowing that this question was out there, that the polling was out there, that people were saying and asking serious questions about the President's age, about whether he should stay in this race and she had to go out there, defend him, stand behind him, obviously not knowing if she was gonna get that phone call today.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a tremendous circumstance. To be out there on the trail, you know, fiercely defending this candidacy, adamant that the President was not going anywhere, that he was staying at the top of the ticket all while knowing and perhaps thinking that she might be in this exact scenario, knowing the pollsters were out there asking questions, you know, to see how she would fair at the top of the ticket against Trump.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There were some polls that actually showed her faring slightly better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  but the entire time, she was a loyal soldier, David, she was out there fighting for what is at the root of this campaign, a message that she is going to continue to make, because, David, tonight, the Biden for President campaign is now officially the Harris for President campaign. Her campaign, it's telling me, they are now full steam ahead for Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Biden campaign now called the Harris campaign from this point forward?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is, that is the message tonight. They've actually officially filed the paperwork. They are now the Harris For President campaign and David, just in the last few hours, we are told some small dollar donors, they have raked in over 27 and a half million dollars just in the last five hours since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced he is dropping out. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is already out there making fundraising appeals and in fact, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has also been making fundraising appeals for her, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alright, the breaking news right there from Mary Bruce. It is now officially the Harris campaign moving forward. So, let's get the Republican reaction to that. Michael Whatley, of course, co-chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . First of all, Mr. Whatley, congratulations on the Republican National Convention. I know you were very happy with those four nights in Milwaukee. We were glad to be there reporting on it all. Obviously, a brand new chapter tonight with President Biden deciding to get out of this race. How does it change the strategy moving forward for the 