own for the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's the unknown and that is what's so dangerous here, that they've been working for months and months to build this whole campaign, this whole operation against President Biden and now they're trying to have to switch gears at this point. So, yes, they're looking at some of the polling. Either way, David, this is such a close election and now you have people really tuning in now that you have this major shakeup on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really, a generational shift on the Democratic side it would appear tonight and again, as you heard me ask Mr. Whatley there about this sudden shift, with all of the focus on President Biden's age, with him stepping out of the race, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at 78, obviously, is the oldest candidate in this race now, moving forward. Rachel Scott with great reporting all afternoon. Rachel, we do appreciate it. As we have been reporting here, many Democratic leaders say President Biden's decision tonight will help energize the party.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  prevail in this nomination process? Will she be challenged? These questions are still outstanding, but if she does get the nomination, with President Biden now fully behind her tonight, the question already, who will be her running mate? Here's our chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  going down the list tonight.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She doesn't have a single pledged delegate yet, but Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is quickly emerging as the presumptive Democratic nominee. Even before Harris made it official that she would be running to replace Biden at the top of the ticket, she had locked up key endorsements, starting with Biden himself and former president \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Hilary Clinton\uc2