 some point you'll be talking about, where he goes after his political opponent, 'cause he can't beat him fair and square.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 You have 80 seconds left. My question was, what do you say to those voters who believe that you - violated your constitutional oath through your actions, inaction on January 6th, 2021, and worry that you'll do it again?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I didn't say that to anybody. I said peacefully and patriotically. And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , if you just watched the news from two days ago, on tape to her daughter, who's a documentary filmmaker, they say, but she's saying, oh, no, it's my responsibility, I was responsible for this, because I offered her 10,000 soldiers or national guard, and she turned them down. And the Mayor of in write, by the way, the Mayor, in writing, turned it down, the mayor of, of DC, they turned it down. I offered 10,000 because I could see, I had virtually nothing to do. They asked me to go make a speech.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I could see what was happening. Everybody was saying they're gonna be there on January 6th, they're gonna be there. And I said, you know what, that's a lot of people coming. You could feel it. You could feel it, too. And you could feel it. And I said, they ought to have some national guard or whatever. And I offered it to her and she now admits that she turned it down. And it was the same day, she was, I don't know, she can't be very happy with the daughter, 'cause it made her into a liar. She said, I take full responsibility for January 6th.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Look, he encouraged those folks to go up on Capitol Hill, number one. I sat in the dining room off the Oval Office, he sat there for three hours. Three hours watching, begging being begged by his vice president and a number of his colleagues on the Republican side, as well, to do something, to call for a stop, to end it. Instead, he talked, talked about these people being patriots and, and, and great patrons of America. In fact, he says he'll now forgive them for what they've done.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They've been convicted, he says he wants to commute their sentences and say that, no. He went to every single court in the nation. I don't know how many cases, scores of cases, including the Supreme Court And they said, they said, no, no, this guy, this guy, this guy is responsible for doing what is being, was done. He didn't do a damn thing, and these people should be in jail, and they should be the ones that are being held accountable, and he wants to let them all out? And now he says if he loses again, such a whiner that he is, that there could be a blood bath?\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 What they've done to some people that are so innocent, you ought to be ashamed of yourself, what you have done, how you've destroyed the lives of so many people. When they ripped down Portland, when they ripped down many other cities, and you go to Minnesota, Minneapolis, what they've done there, with the fires all over the city, if I didn't bring in the National Guard, that city would've been destroyed. When you look at all of the - they took over big chunks of Seattle. I was all set to bring in the National Guard. They heard that, they saw them coming and they left immediately.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 What he said about this whole subject is so off. Peacefully patriotic. One other thing, the unselect committee, which is basically two horrible Republicans that are all gone now, out of office, and Democrats, all Democrats, they destroyed and deleted all of the information they found, because they found out we were right. We were right. And they deleted and destroyed all of the information. They should go to jail for that. If a Republican did that, they'd go to jail.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Trump. President Biden, I want to give you a minute.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The only person on this stage that is a convicted felon is the man I'm looking at right now. And the fact of the matter is, he is, what he's telling you is simply not true. The fact is that there was no effort on his part to stop what was going on up on Capitol Hill. And all those people, every one of those that were convicted deserves to be convicted. The idea that they didn't kill somebody, just went and broke down doors, broke the windows, occupied offices, turned over desks, turned them over, statues, the idea that those people are patriots? Come on.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 When I asked him, the first two, two debates we had and debates we had the first time around, I said, will you denounce the Proud Boys? He said, no, I'll tell them to stand by. The idea he's refusing, will you denounce these guys? Will you denounce the people we're talking about now? Will you denounce the people who attacked the Capitol? What are you gonna do?\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 I'm gonna to give you a, a, minute, President Trump, for a follow-up question I have. After a jury convicted you of 34 felonies last month, you said if reelected, you would, quote, "have every right to go after," unquote, your political opponents. You just talked about members of the Select Committee on January 6th going to jail. Your main political opponent is standing on stage with you tonight. Can you clarify exactly what it means about you feeling you have every right to go after your political opponents?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I said my retribution is going to be a success. We're gonna make this country successful again, because right now it's a failing nation. My retribution is going to be a success. But when he talks about a convicted felon, his son is a convicted felon at a very high level. His son is convicted, gonna be convicted probably numerous other times, should've been convicted before, but his Justice Department let the statute of limitations lapse on the most important things. But he could be a convicted felon as soon as he gets out of office.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Joe could be a convicted felon, with all of the things that he's done. He's done horrible things. All of the death caused at the, the border, telling the Ukrainian people that we're gonna want a billion dollars or you change the Prosecutor, otherwise, you're not getting a billion dollars. If I ever said that, that's quid pro quo, that we're not gonna do anything, we're not gonna give you a billion dollars unless you change your prosecutor, having to do with the son. This man is a criminal. This man - you're lucky. You're lucky. I did nothing wrong. We have a system that was rigged and disgusting. I did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Trump. President Biden, you have said, I'm coming right to you, sir. You - well, you want to respond? Go ahead. I'll give you a minute to respond.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The idea that I did anything wrong relative to what you're talking about is outrageous. It's simply a lie, number one. Number two, the idea that you have a right to seek retribution against any American just because you're president is wrong, is simply wrong. No president's ever spoken like that before. No president in our history has ever spoken like that before. Number three, the crimes that you are still charged with, and think of all the civil penalties you have, how many billions of dollars do you owe in civil penalties for, for molesting a woman in public, for doing a whole range of things, of having sex with a porn star on the night that, while your wife was pregnant? I mean, what, well, what are you talking about? You, you have the morals of an alley cat.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Give you a minute, sir.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I didn't have sex with a porn star, number one. Number two, that was a case that was started and moved. They moved a high-ranking official, a DOJ, into the Manhattan DA's office to start that case. That case is gonna be appealed and won. We had a very terrible judge, horrible judge, Democrat. The prosecutor were all high-ranking Democrats, appointed people, and both the civil and the criminal. He basically went after his political opponent, because he thought it was gonna damage me, but when the public found out about these cases, 'cause they understand it better than he does. He has no idea what these cases are.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But what he did, when they found out about these cases, you know what they did? My poll numbers went up, way up, you know that 'cause you're reporting it, and we took in more money in the last two weeks than we've ever taken in, in the history of, of any campaign, I don't think any campaign has ever taken. Hundreds of millions of dollars came pouring in, because the public knows it's a scam, and it's a guy that's after his political opponent, 'cause he can't win fair and square.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Trump. President Biden, you have said, quote, "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his MAGA Republicans are determined to destroy American democracy." Do you believe that the tens of millions of Americans who are likely to vote for President Trump will be voting against American democracy?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The more they know about what he's done, yes. The more they know about what he's done. And there's a lot more coming. He's got a lot of cases down the road coming around. He's got, he's got a whole range of issues he has to face. I don't know what the juries will do, but I do, I do know he has a real problem. And so, the fact that - could you ever think you hear any president say that I'm gonna seek retribution? You ever hear any president say that they thought Hitler got some good ideas? What got me involved to run in the first place, after my son had died, I decided - in Iraq, because of Iraq, I said I wasn't gonna run again.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Until I saw what happened in Charlottesville, Virginia, people coming out of the woods, carrying swastikas on torches, torches and, and singing the same anti-Semitic bile they sang back when (inaudible) back in Germany, and went to - and a young woman got killed and I spoke to the mother. And she, they asked him, they said, what, well, what do you think of those people? The people who, the ones that got killed, one who tried to stop it, and the ones he said, I think they're fine people on both sides.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 What American president would ever say Nazis coming out of fields carrying torches, singing the same anti-Semitic bile, carrying swastikas, were fine people? And this is a guy who says Hitler's done some good things. I'd like to know what they are, the good things Hitler's done. That's what he said. This guy has no sense of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Jake, both of you know that story has been totally wiped out. Because when you see the sentence, it said, 100 percent exoneration on there. So, he just keeps it going. He says he ran because of Charlottesville. He didn't run because of Charlottesville. He ran because it was last chance at, at - he's not equipped to be president. You know it and I know it. It's ridiculous. We have a debate, we're trying to justify his presidency.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 His presidency, he's, without question, the worst president, the worst presidency in the history of our country. We shouldn't be having a debate about it. There's nothing to debate. He made up the Charlottesville story, and you'll see it's debunked all over the place. Every anchor has - every reasonable anchor has debunked it. And just the other day, it came out where it was fully debunked. It's a nonsense story. He knows that. And he didn't run because of Charlottesville. He used that as an excuse to run.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) and debunked. It happened. You just have to listen to what was said at the time. And the idea that somehow that's the only reason I ran, I ran because I was worried a guy like this guy could get elected. If he thought they were good people coming out of that, that forest, carrying those, those woods, carrying those torches, that he didn't deserve to be president. Didn't deserve to be president at all. And the idea that he's talking about all of this being fabricated, we saw it with our own eyes.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We saw what happened on January 6th. We saw the people breaking through the windows. We saw people occupying - his own vice president, look, there's a reason why 40 of his 44 top Cabinet officers refused to endorse him this time. His vice president hasn't endorsed him this time. So, why? Why? They know him well. They served with him. Why are they not endorsing him?\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. We're gonna be right back with more from the "CNN Presidential Debate."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the "CNN Presidential Debate" live from Georgia. Let's talk about persistent challenges you both faced in your first terms and you'd certainly face again in the second term. President Biden, while Black unemployment dropped to a record low under your presidency, Black families still earn far less than white families. Black mothers are still three times more likely to die from pregnancy-related causes, and Black Americans are imprisoned at five times the rate of white Americans. What do you say to Black voters who are disappointed that you haven't made more progress?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They acknowledge we made a lot of progress, number one. The facts of the matter is, are more small Black businesses that have been started than any time in history. Number two, the wages of Black - Black unemployed is the lowest level that it's been in a long, long time. Number three, we find, (inaudible) providing housing for Black Americans and dealing with the segregation that exist among these corporate, these corporations that collude to keep people out of their houses. In, in addition to that, we find that the impact of, on the, the choice that Black families have to make relative to childcare is incredibly difficult.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 When we did the first major piece of legislation that passed, I was able to reduce Black childcare costs. I cut them in half, in half. We got to make sure we provide for childcare costs. We got to make sure, because when you provide those childcare protections, you increase economic growth, because more people can be in the, in the job market. So, there's more to be done, considerably more to be done. But we've done a great deal so far, and I'm not letting up, and they know it.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 You have 49 seconds left. What do you say to Black voters who are disappointed with the progress so far?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I say, I don't blame them for being disappointed. Inflation is still hurting them badly. For example, I provided for the idea that any Black family, first-time homebuyer should get a $10 thousand tax credit to be able to buy their first home so they can get started. I made sure that we're in a situation where all those Black families and those Black individuals who provided had to take out student loans that were ballooning, that if they were, are engaged in nursing, doctor, and anything having to do with volunteerism, if they paid their bills for ten years on their student debt, all the rest is forgiven after ten years. Millions have benefited from that. And we're gonna do a whole lot more for Black families.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And he caused the inflation. He's blaming inflation. And he's right, it's been very bad. He caused the inflation and it's killing Black families and Hispanic families and just about everybody. It's killing people. They can't buy groceries anymore. They can't - you look at the cost of food, where it's doubled and tripled and quadrupled, they can't live. They're not living anymore. He caused this inflation. I gave him a country with no, essentially no inflation. It was perfect. It was so good. All he had to do is leave it alone.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 He destroyed it with his green new scam and all of the other, all this money that's being thrown out the window. He caused inflation. As sure as you're sitting there, the fact is that his big kill on the Black people is the millions of people that he's allowed to come in through the border. They're taking Black jobs now. And it could be 18, it could be 19, and even 20 million people, they're taking Black jobs and they're taking Hispanic jobs. And you haven't seen it yet, but you're gonna see something that's gonna be the worst in our history.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 There was no inflation when I became president. You know why? The economy was flat on its back, 15 percent unemployment. He decimated the economy, absolutely decimated the economy. That's why there was no inflation at the time. There were no jobs. We provided thousands and millions of jobs for individuals who were in all communities, including minority communities. We made sure that they have health insurance. We have covered with - the ACA has increased. I made sure that there're $8,000 per person in a family to get written off in health care, but this guy wants to eliminate that. They've tried 50 times.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 He wants to get rid of the ACA again, and they're gonna try again if they win. We find ourselves in a position where the idea that we're not doing any - I've put more, we put more police on the street than any administration has. He wants to cut the cops. We're providing for equity, equity in making sure people have a shot to make it. There's a lot going on. But inflation, he caused it by his tremendous malfeasance in the way he handled the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Another persistent challenge is the climate crisis. Twenty twenty-three was the hottest year in recorded history, and communities across the country are confronting the devastating effects of extreme heat, intensifying wildfires, stronger hurricanes, and rising sea levels. Former President Trump, you've vowed to end your opponent's climate initiatives. But will you take any action as president to slow the climate crisis?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, let me just go back to what he said about the police, how close the police are to him. Almost every police group in the nation, from every state, is supporting \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , almost every police group. And what he's done to the Black population is horrible, including the fact that for ten years he called them super predators. We can't - in the 1990s, we can't forget that. Super predators was his name and he called it to them for 10 - and they've taken great offense at it, and now they see it happening. But when they see what I did for criminal justice reform and for the historically Black colleges and universities, where I funded them and got them all funded, and the opportunity zones with, with Tim.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 As you know, Tim Scott was incredible. He did a great job, great senator from South Carolina. He came to me with the idea, and it was a great idea. It's one of the most successful economic development acts ever in the country, opportunity zones. And the biggest beneficiary are Blacks. And that's why we have the best numbers with them in maybe ever. They're saying ever. I read this morning where, ever, the best numbers. He's lost much of the Black population because he's done a horrible job for Black people. He's also done a horrible job for Hispanics. But wait until you see these millions of people pouring into our country, and they're gonna take the jobs and it's already started. And you haven't seen anything yet. It's a disaster.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 You've 38 seconds left, President Trump. Will you take any action as president to slow the climate crisis?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 So, I want absolutely immaculate, clean water, and I want absolutely clean air, and we had it. We had H20. We had the best numbers ever, and we did, we were using all forms of energy, all forms, everything. And yet, during my four years, I had the best environmental numbers ever. And my top environmental people gave me that statistic just before I walked on the stage, actually.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I don't know where the hell he's been. The idea that anything he said is true. I passed the most extensive, the most extensive climate change legislation in history, in history. We find ourselves - and by the way, Black colleges, I, I came up with $15 billion for HBCUs, Historic Black universities and colleges, because they don't have, they don't have the kind of contributors that they have to build these laboratories and the like. Any Black student is capable in college of doing what any white student can do. They just have the money but now they'll be able to get those jobs in high tech.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're in a situation where the idea that he's claiming to have done something that had the cleanest water? The cleanest water? He hasn't done a damn thing for the environment. He pulled out of the Paris Peace Accord, the Climate Accord. I immediately joined it, because if we reach 1.5 degrees Celsius at any one point, there's no way back. The only existential threat to humanity is climate change. And he didn't do a damn thing about it, and he wants to undo all that I've done.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 The Paris Accord was gonna cost us a trillion dollars, and China nothing, and Russia nothing, and India nothing. It was a rip-off of the United States. And I ended it because I didn't want to waste that money, because they treat us horribly. We were the only ones - it was costing us money. Nobody else was paying into it. And it was, it was a disaster. But everything that he said just now, I'll give you an example. I heard him say before insulin. I'm the one that got the insulin down for the seniors. I took care of the seniors.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 What he's doing is destroying all of our medical programs, because the migrants coming in, they want everybody - and look, I have the, I have the biggest heart on the stage, I guarantee you that. And I want to take care of people. But we're destroying our country. They're talking over our schools, our hospitals, and they're gonna be taking over Social Security. He is destroying Social Security, Medicare, and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) from? The idea is that we, in fact, we were the only ones of consequence who were not, are not members of, of the Paris Accord. How can we do anything if we're not able, the United States can't get (inaudible) under control, one of the largest polluters in the world, number one. We're making significant progress. By 2035, we will have cut pollution in half. We have made, we've made significant progress. And we're continuing to make progress. We set up a Climate Corps for thousands of young people to learn how to deal with climate, just like the Peace Corps. And we're gonna, we're moving in directions that are gonna significantly change the elements and cause of pollution.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 But the idea that he claims that he has the biggest heart up here and he's really concerned about, about pollution and about climate, I've not seen any indication of that. And by the way, with regard to prescription drugs, one company agreed that they would reduce the price to $35, which I was calling for. One, voluntarily. I made sure every company in the world, every pharmaceutical company, cannot have to pay.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And by the way...\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 So, every day, millions of Americans struggle just to make ends meet. For many older Americans, Social Security provides a critical lifeline. President Biden, if nothing is done to Social Security, seniors will see their benefits cut in just over ten years. Will you name tonight one specific step that you're willing to take to keep Social Security solvent?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Make the very wealthy begin to pay their fair share. Right now, everybody making under $170,000 pays 6 percent of their income, of their paycheck, every single time they get a paycheck, from the time the first one they get when they're 18 years old. The idea that they're gonna - I'm not, I've been proposing that everybody, they pay, the millionaires pay one percent, one percent. So, no one after, I've not raised the cost of Social Security for anybody under $400,000. After that, I'd begin to make the wealthy begin to pay their fair share, by increasing from one percent beyond, to be able to guarantee the program for life.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 So, you still have 82 seconds left. Are there any other measures that you think would be able to help keep Social Security solvent, or is just, is that one enough?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, no, that, that one enough will keep it solvent. But the biggest thing I'll do, if we defeat this man, because he wants to get rid of Social Security. He thinks that there's plenty to cut Social Security. He's wanted to cut Social Security and Medicare both times, and that's - and if you look at the, the program put forward by the House Republican Caucus that he, I believe, supports, is, in fact, want to cut it, as well. The idea that we don't need to protect our seniors is ridiculous. We put, we - and by the way, American public has greater health care coverage today than ever before.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And the ACA, as I said, you're in a circumstance where 400,000 people, 400, I mean, 40, 40 million people would not have insurance because they have a pre-existing condition. Only thing allows them to have that is the fact that they, in fact, are a part of the ACA. And by the way, the other thing is, we're in a situation where I talk about education for Black communities. I've raised the number, the amount of money for Pell grants by another $8,000, so anybody making under $70,000 a year are gonna be able to get $15,000 towards their tuition. I, he, he just doesn't know what he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 So, I've dealt with politicians all my life. I've been on this side of the equation for the last eight years. I've never seen anybody lie like this guy. He lies. I've never seen anything - he can look you in the face. So - and about so many other things, too. And we mentioned the laptop, we mentioned "Russia, Russia, Russia," "Ukraine, Ukraine, Ukraine," and everything he does is a lie. It's misinformation and disinformation. The "losers and suckers" story that he made up, it's a total lie on the military. It's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But Social Security, he's destroying it. Because millions of people are pouring into our country, and they're putting them on to Social Security, they're putting them on to Medicare, Medicaid. They're putting them in our hospitals. They're taking the place of our citizens. They're, what they're doing to the VA, to our veterans is unbelievable. Our veterans are living in the street and these people are living in luxury hotels. He doesn't know what he's doing. And it's, it's really coming back. I've never seen such anger in our country before.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The idea veterans are not being taken care of, I told you before - any by the way, when I said "suckers and losers," he said, he acknowledged after that he fired that general. That general got fired because he's the one that acknowledged that that's what he said. He was the one standing with Trump when he said it, number one. Number two, the idea that we're gonna be in a situation where all these millions and millions, the way he talks about it, illegal aliens are coming into the country and taking away our jobs, there's a reason why we have the fastest-growing economy in the world, a reason why we have the most successful economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're doing better than any other nation in the world. And by the way, those 15 Nobel laureates he talked about being phony, those 15 Nobel laureates, economists, they all said that if Trump is reelected, we're likely to have a recession, and, and inflation is gonna increasingly go up. And by the way, worst president in history? A hundred and fifty-nine presidential scholars voted him the worst president in history of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Biden, thank you so much. Let's turn to the cost of childcare, which many American families struggle to afford. President Trump, both you and President Biden have tried to address this issue, but the average cost of childcare in this country has risen to more than $11,000 a year per child. For many families, the cost of childcare for two children is more than their rent. In your second term, what would you do to make childcare more affordable?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Just to go back, the General got fired because he was no good. And if he said that, that's why he made it up, but we have 19 people that said I didn't say it and they're very highly respected, much more so than him. The other thing is, he doesn't fire people. He never fired people. I've never seen him fire anybody. I did fire a lot. I fired Comey 'cause he was no good. I fired a lot of the top people at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , drained the swamp. They were no good. Not easy to fire people. You pay a price for it, but they were no good. I inherited these people. I didn't put him, I didn't put Comey there. He was no good. I fired him. This guy hasn't fired anybody.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 He never fires - he should've fired every military man that was involved with that Afghan, the Afghanistan horror show, the most embarrassing moment in the history of our country. Did - he didn't fire - did you fire anybody? Did you fire anybody that's on the border, that's allowed us to have the worst border in this history of the world? Did anybody get fired for allowing 18 million people, many from prisons, many from, from mental institutions, did you fire anybody that allowed our country to be destroyed? Joe, our country is being destroyed as you and I sit up here and waste a lot of time on this debate. This shouldn't be a debate. He is the worst president.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 He just said about me, because I said it. But look, he's the worst president in the history of our country. He's destroyed our country. Now, all of a sudden, he's trying to get a little tough on the border. He come out, came out with a, a nothing, a nothing deal, and it reduced it a little bit, a little bit, like this much, it's insignificant. He wants open borders. He wants our country to either be destroyed or he wants to pick up those people as voters. And I don't think, we, we just can't let it happen. If he wins this election, our country doesn't have a chance, not even a chance of coming out of this rut. We probably won't have a country left anymore. That's how bad it is. He is the worst in history by far.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Trump. President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're the most admired country in the world. We're the United States of America. There's nothing beyond our capacity. We have the finest military in the history of the world, the finest in the history of the world. No one thinks we're weak. No one wants to screw around with us. Nobody. Number one. Number two, the idea that we're talking about worst presidents, I wasn't joking. Look it up or go online. A hundred and fifty-nine or 58, don't hold me to the exact number, presidential historians, they've had meetings and they voted who is the worst president in American history, one from best to worst.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They said he was the worst in all of American history. That's a fact. That's not that's a, that's not conjecture. He can argue they're wrong, but that's what they voted. The idea that he is (inaudible) doing anything to deal with childcare, he did very, virtually nothing to childcare. We should significantly increase the childcare tax credit, we should significantly increase the availability of women and men, or single parents to be able to go back to work, and we should encourage businesses to hold, to have childcare facilities.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. President Trump, the question was about what would you do to make childcare more affordable, if you want to take your minute.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Just so you understand, we have polling, we have other things that do, they rate him the worst because what he's done so bad. And they rate me, I'll show you, I will show you. And they rate me one of the best, okay? And if I'm given another four years, I will be the best. I think I'll be the best. Nobody's ever created an economy like us. Nobody ever cut taxes like us. He's the only one I know. He wants to raise your taxes by four times. He wants to raise everybody's taxes by four times.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 He wants the Trump tax cuts to expire so everybody, including the two of you, are gonna pay four to five times - nobody heard of this before. All my life, I grow up and I see politicians talking about cutting taxes. When we cut taxes, as I said, we did more business. \uc2 Apple\uc2  and all these companies, they were bringing money back into our country. The worst president in history by far, and everybody knows it.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Look, the fact of the matter is that he's dead wrong about - he's increased (inaudible), he's increased, he will increase the taxes on middle class people. I said I'd never raise the taxes on anybody making less than $400,000. I didn't. But these tariffs, his 10 percent tariffs, everything coming into the country, you know what the economists say? That's gonna cost the average American $2,500 a year more because they're gonna have to pay the difference in food and all the things that are being imported.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Number two, he's in a situation where he talks about how he has not raised, he, he's, he somehow helped the middle class. The middle class has been devastated by you. Now you want a new tax cut of $5 trillion over the next ten years, which is gonna fundamentally bankrupt the country. You had the largest deficit of any president in American history, number one. Number two, you have not, in fact, made any contact, any, any, any progress with China. We are at the lowest trade deficit with China since 2010.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You were at the highest. You lost (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. Let's enter, let's discuss an epidemic impacting millions of Americans, that both of you have made a top priority in your first term, the opioid crisis. And for both of you, the number of overdose deaths in this country has gone up. Under your term, it went up. Under your term, it has gone up. Former President Trump, despite the efforts that both of you have made, more than 100,000 Americans are dying from overdoses every year, primarily from fentanyl and other opioids. What will you do to help Americans right now in the throes of addiction, who are struggling to get the treatment they need?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 To finish up, we now have the largest deficit in the history of our country under this guy. We have the largest deficit with China. He gets paid by China. He's a Manchurian candidate. He gets money from China. We have -so, I think he's afraid to deal with them or something. But do you notice he never took out my tariffs, because we bring in so much money with the tariffs that I imposed on China. He never took them away. He can't because it's too much money. It's tremendous.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And we saved our steel industries, and there was more to come. But he hasn't done that. But he hasn't cut the tariffs because he can't, because it's too much money. But he's got the largest deficit in the history of our - of our country, and he's got the worst, the worst situation with China. China's gonna own us, if you keep allowing them to do what they're doing to us as a country. They are killing us as a country, Joe. And you can't let that happen. You're destroying our country.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 So, President Trump, you have 67 seconds left. The question was, what are you gonna do to help Americans in the throes of addiction right now, who are struggling to get the treatment they need?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Jake, we're doing very well on addiction. Until COVID came along, we had the two-and-a-half, almost three years of - like nobody's ever had before, any country, in every way. And then we had to get tough. And it was the drugs pouring across the border where it started to increase. We got great equipment, we bought the certain dog that's the most incredible thing that you've seen, the way they can spot it. We did a lot. And we had, we were getting very low numbers. Very, very low numbers.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Then, he came along, the numbers, have you seen the numbers now? It's not only the 18 million people that I believe is even low, because they, the got-aways, they don't even talk about got-aways, but the numbers of - the, the amount of drugs, and human trafficking in women, coming across our border, the worst thing I've ever seen at numbers that nobody's ever seen under him because the border's so bad. But the, the number of drugs coming across our border now is, is the largest we've ever had by far.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Trump, thank you. President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Fentanyl and the byproducts of fentanyl went down for awhile. And I wanted to make sure we use the machinery that can detect fentanyl, these big machines that roll over everything that comes across the border. And it costs a lot of money. That was part of this deal we put together, this bipartisan deal, more fentanyl machines, more, more being able to detect drugs, more, more numbers of, of agents, more numbers of all the people at the border. And people - when we had that deal done, he went and he called his Republican colleagues, said, don't do it. It's gonna hurt me politically.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 No, he never argued it's not a good bill. It's a really good bill. We need those machines. We need those machines. And we're coming down very hard in every country in Asia in terms of precursors for fentanyl. And Mexico is working with us to make sure. They don't have the technology to be able to put it together. That's what we have to do. We need those machines.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. President Trump, and again, the question is about Americans in the throes of addiction right now struggling to get the treatment they need.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, this, 'cause this does pertain to it. He ended Remain in Mexico. He ended catch and release. I made it catch and release in Mexico, not catch and release here. We had so many things that we had done, hard negotiations with Mexico, and I got it all for nothing. It's just like when you have a hostage, we always pay $6 billion for a hostage every time there's a hostage. Now we have a hostage, "The Wall Street Journal" reporter, I think a good guy, and he's over there because putin is laughing at this guy, probably asking for billions of dollars for the reporter.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I will have him out very quickly, as soon as I take office, before I take office. I said, by literally as soon as I win the election, I will have that reporter out. He should've had him out a long time ago. But Putin's probably asking for billions and billions of dollars, because this guy pays it every time. We had two cases where we paid $6 billion for five people. I got 58 people out and I paid essentially nothing.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Trump. Dana?\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Let's turn to concerns that voters have about each of you. President Biden, you would be 86 at the end of your second term. How do you address concerns about your capability to handle the toughest job in the world well into your 80s?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, I spent half my career being, being criticized being the youngest person in politics. I was the second-youngest person ever elected to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And now I'm the oldest. This guy's three years younger, and a lot less competent. I think that just look at the record. Look at what I've done. Look at how I've turned around the horrible situation he left me. As I said, 50 million new jobs, 800,000 manufacturing jobs, more investment in America, over million, billions of dollars in private investment and, and enterprises that we are growing. We've, by the way, we brought an awful, a lot of people (inaudible) the whole idea of computer chips.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We used to have 40 percent of the market. We invented those chips and we lost it, because he was sending people to cheap - to find the cheapest jobs overseas and to bring home a product. So, I went, so I went to South Korea. I convinced \uc2 Samsung\uc2  to invest billions of dollars here in the United States. And they're guess what? Those fabs they call that - to build these chips, those fabs pay over $100,000. You don't need a college degree for them. And there's billions, about $40 billion already being invested and being built right now in the United States, creating significant jobs for Americans all over, from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Biden, you have 40 seconds left. Would you like to add anything?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I would. The idea that somehow we are this failing country, I never heard a president talk like this before. We're, we're the envy of the world. I, name me a single major country president who wouldn't trade places with the United States of America. For all of problems and all our opportunities, we're the most progressive country in the world in getting things done. We're the strongest country in the world. We're a country in the world who keeps our word. And everybody trusts us, all of our allies. And our, and our - and our, those who he coddles up to, from Kim Jong-Un who he sends love letters to, and Putin, et cetera. They don't want to screw around with us.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Former President Trump, to follow up, you would be 82 at the end of your second term. What do you say to voters who have concerns about your capabilities to serve?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I took two tests, cognitive tests. I aced them, both of them, as you know. We made it public. He took none. I'd like to see him take one, just one, a real easy one. Like, go through the first five questions, he couldn't do it. But I took two cognitive tests. I took physical exams every year. And, you know, we knock on wood, wherever we may have wood, that I'm in very good health. I just won two club championships, not even senior, two regular club championships. To do that, you have to be quite smart and have to be able to hit the ball a long way. And I do it. He doesn't do it. He can't hit a ball 50 yards. He challenged me to a golf match. He can't hit a ball 50 yards.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I think I'm in very good shape. I feel that I'm as in good a shape as I was 25, 30 years ago. Actually, I'm probably a little lighter. But I'm in as good of shape as I was years ago. I feel very good. I feel the same. But I took, I was willing to take a cognitive test, and you know what? If I didn't do well - I aced them. Dr. Ronnie Jackson, who's a great guy, when he was White House doctor. And then I took another one, a similar one. And both, one of them said they've never seen anybody ace them.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You can see he is six-foot-five and only two hundred and twenty-third pounds, or two hundred and thirty-five pounds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, he said six-four, two hundred...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, anyway, that's in your - anyway. Just take a look at what he says he is and take a look at what he is. Look, I'd be happy to have a driving contest with him. (Inaudible) I got my handicap, which, when I was vice president, down to a six. And by, by the way, I told you before, I'm happy to play golf if you carry your own bag. Think you can do it?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 That's the biggest lie that he's a six handicap of all.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I was an eight handicap.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Eight.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Never.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And I had, you know many, how...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I've seen your swing. I know your swing. Let's, let's not act like children.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Hey, let's...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Do you know how many rounds...\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Trump, we're going to turn...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 ...21 rounds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Let's not act like children.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You are a child.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 ...to a specific concern that voters have about you. Will you pledge tonight that once all legal challenges have been exhausted that you will accept the results of this election regardless of who wins, and you will say right now that political violence in any form is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I shouldn't have to say that, but of course I believe that. It's totally unacceptable. And if you would see my statements that I made on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at the time, and also my statement that I made in the Rose Garden, you would say it's one of the strongest statements you've ever seen. In addition to the speech I made, in front of, I believe, the largest crowd I've ever spoken to, and I will tell you, nobody ever talks about that. They talk about a relatively small number of people that went to the Capitol, and in many cases, were ushered in by the police. And as \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said, it was her responsibility, not mine. She said that loud and clear.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But the answer is, if the election is fair, free, and I want that more than anybody. And I'll tell you something, I wish he was a great president, because I wouldn't here right now. I'd be at one of my many places enjoying myself. I wouldn't be under indicted because I wouldn't have been his political appoint - you know, opponent, because he indicted because I was his opponent. I wish he was a great president. I would rather have that. I wouldn't be here. I don't mind being here. But the only reason I'm here is, he's so bad as a president that I'm gonna make America great again. We're going to make America great again. We're a failing nation right now. We're a seriously failing nation. And we're a failing nation because of him.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 His policies are so bad. His military policies are insane. They're insane. These are wars that will never end with him. He will drive us into World War III, and we're closer to World War III than anybody can imagine. We are very, very close to World War III and he's driving us there. And \uc2 Kim Jong-Un\uc2 , and President Xi of China, Kim Jong-Un of North Korea, all of these, Putin, they don't respect him. They don't fear him. They have nothing going with this gentleman, and he's gonna drive us into World War III.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You want a World War III, let him follow and win and let Putin say do what you want \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Just do what you want. There's a thing called Article Five, an attack on one is an attack on all, a required response. The idea, the idea, I can't think of a single major leader in the world who wouldn't trade places with the job I've done with what they've done. Because we are a powerful nation.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We have wonderful peace because of the people, not me, it's because of the American people. They're capable of anything and they step up when they're needed. And right now, we're needed. We're needed to protect the world, because our own safety is at stake. And again, you want to have a war, just let Putin go ahead and take Kyiv, make sure they move on, see what happens in Poland, Hungary, and other places along that border. Then you have a war.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 President Trump, as I come back to you for a follow-up, the question was, will you accept the results of this election regardless of who wins?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Just to finish what he said, if I might, Russia, they took a lot of land from Bush. They took a lot of land from Obama and Biden. They took no land, nothing, from Trump. Nothing. He knew not to do it. Not gonna play games with me, he knew that. I got along with him very well, but he knew not to play games. He took nothing from me. But now he's gonna take the whole thing from this man right here. That's a war that should've never started. It would've never started, ever, with me. And he's going to take Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 And, you know, you asked me a question before, would you do this, would - he's got us in such a bad position right now with, with Ukraine and Russia, because Ukraine's not winning that war. He said, I will never settle until such time - they're running out of people, they're running out of soldiers, they've lost so many people. It's so sad. They've lost so many people, and they've lost those gorgeous cities with the golden domes that are 1,000 years old, all because of him and stupid decisions. Russia would've never attacked if I were president.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 President Trump, the question was, will you accept the results of the election regardless of who wins, yes or no, please.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 If it's a fair and legal and good election, absolutely. I would've much rather accepted these, but the, the fraud and everything else was ridiculous. And if you want, we'll have a news conference on it in a week, or we'll have another one of these on a, in a week. But, I will absolutely - there's nothing I'd rather do. It would be much easier for me to do that than I'm running again.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't really going to run until I saw the horrible job he did. He's destroying our country. I would be very happy to be some place else, in a nice location some place. And again, no indictments, no political opponent stuff, because it's the only way he thinks he can win. But unfortunately, it's driven up my numbers and driven them up to a very high level, because the people understand it.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, let's see what your numbers are when this election is over.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Let's see.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let's see. You're a whiner. When you lost the first time, you, you continued, you appealed and appealed the courts all across the country. Not one single court in America said any of your claims had any merit. State or local. None. But you continue to promote this lie about somehow there's all this misrepresentation, all this stealing. There's no evidence of that at all. And I tell you what, I doubt whether you'll accept it, because you're such a whiner. The idea, if you lose again, you accepting anything, you can't stand the loss. Something snapped in you when you lost the last time.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back with more from the "CNN Presidential Debate" live from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 It is now time for the candidates to deliver their closing statements. As predetermined by a coin toss, we're gonna begin you with, President Biden. You have two minutes.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We've made significant progress from the debacle that was left by President Trump in, in, in his last term. We find ourselves in a situation where, number one, we have to make sure that we have a fair tax system. I ask anyone out there in the audience, or anyone out watching this debate, do you think the tax system is fair? The fact is that I said, nobody making under $400,000 had a single penny increase in their taxes and will not. And if I'm reelected, that will be the case again. But this guy is, has increased your taxes because of the deficit. Number one, he's increased in inflation because of the debacle he left after the way he handled the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And he finds himself in a position where he now wants to tax you more by putting a 10 percent tariff on everything that comes into the United States of America. What I did, well, for example, he wants to get away with, get rid of the ability of Medicare to, for the ability to, for the, us to be able to negotiate drug prices with the Big Pharma companies. prices. Well, guess what? We got it, we got it down to 15, excuse me, $35 for insulin instead of $400, no more than $2,000 for every senior no matter what they, how much prescriptions they need. You know what that did? That reduced the federal deficit, debt by $160 billion over ten years, because the government doesn't have to pay the exorbitant prices.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I'm going to make that available to every senior, all, all along or is happening now, and everybody in America. He wants to get rid of that. We have, I'm gonna to make sure that we have child care. We're gonna significantly increase the credits people have for child care. I'm gonna make sure we do something about what we're doing on lead pipes and all the things that are, that are causing health problems for people across the country. We're gonna continue to fight to bring down inflation and give people a break.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, President Biden. President Trump, you now have two minutes for your closing statement.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Like so many politicians, this man is just a complainer. He said, we wanna do this, we wanna do that, we wanna get rid of this tax, that tax, but he doesn't do anything. He doesn't do - all he does is make our country unsafe, by allowing millions and millions of people to pour in. Our military doesn't respect him. We look like fools in Afghanistan. We didn't stop Israel. It was such a horrible thing that would've never happened. It should've never happened. Iran was broke. Anybody that did business with Iran, including China, they couldn't do business with the United States. They all passed. Iran was broke. They had no money for Hamas or Hezbollah, for terror, no money whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 Again, Ukraine should've never happened. He talks about all this stuff, but he didn't do it. For three and a half years, we're living in hell. We have the Palestinians and everybody else rioting all over the place. You talk about Charlottesville, this is 100 times Charlottesville, 1,000 times. The whole country is exploding because of you, because they don't respect you. And they have to respect their president. And they don't respect you throughout the world. What we did was incredible. We - rebuilt the military. We got the largest tax cut in history, the largest regulation cut in history.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 The reason he's got jobs is because I cut the regulations that gave jobs, but he's putting a lot of those regulations back on. All of the things that we've done, nobody's ever never, they've never seen anything like - even from a medical standpoint, right to try, where we can try Space Age materials instead of going to Asia, or going to Europe and trying to get - when you are terminally ill, now you can go and you can get something. You sign a document. They've been trying to get it for 42 years.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE)\par \par \uc2 But, you know what we did for the military was incredible. Choice for our soldiers, where our soldiers, instead of waiting for three months to see a doctor, can go out and get themselves fixed up and readied up and take care of themselves, and they're living. And that's why I had the highest approval rating in the history of the VA. So, all of these things - we're in a failing nation. But it's not gonna be failing anymore. We're gonna make it great again.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN DEBATE MODERATOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, former President Trump, President Biden. Stay with us, because we have full analysis of this debate. Anderson Cooper and Erin Burnett starts now on CNN.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) we watched as a nation now, the first presidential debate of this election cycle. This is the earliest Presidential debate that we've witnessed as a country. We're staying on this shot to see if they acknowledge one another. One of the telling parts of this evening was from the very start, the walkout onto that debate stage in Atlanta, both the current president, President Biden, and former President Donald Trump walking out onto that stage, there was no hand shake. It wasn't anticipated, though there wasn't even a look toward one another as this debate began. The tone was set right away.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They debated on issues including the economy, inflation, jobs, handling of COVID, both during the Trump years and during the Biden years, foreign policy, Afghanistan, how both handled Afghanistan, the role of NATO and talking about Ukraine, what the future of Ukraine looks like. They talked about January 6th and whether or not former President Donald Trump would denounce those who stormed the Capitol. That was asked of him by the current president, President Biden. There was not an answer to that particular question, though they did spar going back and forth multiple times, the split screen allowing the country to examine both of these men.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And one of the main questions going into the night, because, again, as we had mentioned, they, they both had been president now. Americans know their records. They've studied them. They feel like they know them. This was going to be a test tonight in aptitude. There was that moment, not far from the beginning of the debate, when President Biden was talking about the debt and talking about taxing billionaires in this country, where he was describing what the affect of the policy that he was proposing would have.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then there was that pause about five, six seconds where he didn't say anything, and then he went on to say, we finally beat Medicare, a bit of a non sequitur it seemed. We'll be waiting to see how the Biden team explains that out. Let's get to, to both camps and find out how they believe their candidates did. Rachel Scott live in Atlanta in the spin room. Rachel, what are we hearing from Donald Trump's campaign tonight?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I'll tell you that a senior adviser to the Trump campaign tells me they let out a huge exhale from the moment that President Biden answered that very first question. Sources within the Trump campaign said that they were asking what was wrong with President Biden, what was wrong with his voice. They do feel like the former president tonight stayed on message. And so, much of the talk heading into this was, which version of Donald Trump were we going to get. Were we gonna get the combative Donald Trump that we saw in that debate in 2020, where he's interrupting. It turned into a shouting match. We did not get that version of Donald Trump tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is exactly what his advisers wanted to see. They wanted to see, see the former president take questions on tough issues like January 6th, even his legal troubles, and then pivot back to the issues they believe are winning issues in this election, immigration, crime, the economy. We saw the former president do that many times. I will tell you, David, that out on the campaign trail, the former president does use a lot more harsh rhetoric when it comes to talking about certain issues, including immigration. He vows of the largest deportation in American. He says that immigrants are poisoning the blood of this country.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He didn't go so far as to repeat ending of, any of that incendiary rhetoric that we hear a lot of times on the campaign trail. One thing was clear, though, David, this turned personal rather quickly. You had President Biden call him a convicted felon, and then you had the former president fire back and call his son a convicted felon. And we are told that during these commercial breaks, these candidates did not look at each other. They did not talk, and obviously, they did not shake hands on that debate stage tonight, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that was another first that we witnessed. Rachel Scott, thank you. The Trump campaign happy with Donald Trump's performance tonight. The moment when President Biden did point out the fact that his opponent is a convicted felon, another split-screen moment when he talked about what he was convicted of. There was some head nodding from the former president, which, again, made for a moment unlike and there were many moments here that we have seen before.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I am curious about that particular moment in the beginning, because so much had been said about President Biden going into tonight, a lot of eyes across the country watching, again, this sort of aptitude test for both gentlemen. One is 78. One is 81 years old. There was the moment, the pause, and, and, and sort of a non sequitur there, just to finish the, the thought. Any reaction from the Biden campaign tonight, from the White House tonight on, on that, and the rest of the evening?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Look, there is no question, this is not the night that the Biden campaign wanted or needed. They were going into this hoping that the President's performance would put to rest those remaining concerns about his age, and his fitness for office. It is hard to see how what we just saw did that, how, how it put to bed those concerns. He had a rough start, as you noted. He was rambling. He struggled at times to, to collect his thoughts. There was that pause. Now, we are told that the President had a cold tonight. Perhaps that did play a role, somehow. But the Biden campaign knows that this was not a strong night for them.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are stressing, this was just one night. They know that this race is not gonna be determined or won in one night, that they can still go on offense in the days ahead. But this was the largest audience that he had in this race so far. It was a missed opportunity. And I think that is the reaction that you are hearing from a lot of Democrats privately. You know, look, Biden did have strong moments. He did try to hold Trump accountable. They had strong exchanges. He tried to hold his feet to the fire on abortion. He called him out for being a convicted criminal. I think the concern and the real risk here is that the substance, the message is being lost in how it is being delivered.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But even on abortion rights, there was a moment when they were talking about...\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Donald Trump and the overturning of Roe, Donald Trump on the campaign trail, and as president took credit for that. He did deliver. That was a promise he made to his supporters and delivered on that promise. Now he has to answer to that, given he's running for president again. And, and it seemed in that particular moment, for example, President Biden turned to immigration instead of spelling out the stark differences between these two, and we know this is a deeply personal issue that does divide Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And he did try to call out Donald Trump, who, by the way, was spewing a lot of false statements and mistruths, and you did see the President trying to set the record straight, defend his agenda, and what he has done and really make clear to voters, you know what the former president did when he was in office, and what he could do if he were to win reelection again. But then there were those moments where he would pivot to another topic, or he would seem to, to, to you know, lose his train of thought a little bit. The question is, how did it land? How are voters perceiving this? And that is what we really need to see now in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, I, I, I think we have to be, be very clear. This was a very bad night for Joe Biden. I have been talking to Democrats as this debate progressed, and you are starting to see here at least privately discussions among Democrats about whether or not Biden can effectively go on with this campaign against Donald Trump. Donald Trump did go through rapid fire lies, I mean, a machine gun of lies at times during this debate. He said things like, everybody wanted Roe v. Wade repealed, that, that everything was rocking good when he was president, there were no wars, the unselect committee deleted all the evidence that he, Nancy Pelosi refused 10,000 National Guard troops.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) None of that was true. And Biden was not effectively able to bring the argument back to him. I, I talked to one member, Democratic member of Congress who is a strong supporter up 'til now of, of Joe Biden saying to me, "I am in a state of shock. He is diminished to an extent that has become undeniable." Biden's gonna have to answer these questions. This was not just one night in the campaign. This was the night in the campaign so far. Another Democrat said, "I wish this performance had been during the Democratic primary. If it were, we would've had a different nominee."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question in your mind given what you're hearing from your sources that this is a conversation that's now underway?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a conversation that is being conducted privately at this point, David. The big question is, will any of it become public? Because it is very difficult to imagine a scenario where Biden can be replaced as the nominee. You would have to somehow open up the convention, Biden would have, you know, be the one leading it and saying, I'm gonna release my delegates. There's absolutely no indication that he is willing to do this. And nobody, no Democrat is going to want to further damage Biden beyond what we saw tonight by being the first to come out and say that publicly, unless it's determined there's truly a viable path to find another nominee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And certainly, we don't want to get ahead of ourselves as reporters either.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's bring in Donna Brazile, ABC News analyst, former DNC chair. Donna, what do you make of President Biden's performance tonight?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think there were many missed opportunities. We wanted a couple of doubles, a few singles, and, and yes, we wanted him to hit it out of the ballpark. But the fact is, is that I think the President was trying to not only get his message across, which, at times, he had a hard time. But he was also trying to set a narrative, set a pace, and he never really got an opportunity to, to reach that point. So, I believe it was a missed opportunity. Will it change the trajectory of the race? Perhaps on the margins, but you know, that's why we have 131 days.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Many Democrats are disappointed. Some Democrats are telling me, well, look what the other guy did. He didn't hit it out the ballpark, and half of what he said, well, everybody knows tomorrow will be fact check and we know it was not true. The bottom line is, this race is gonna continue. There's no effort underway right now, that I know of, and I think I would know, at least, on the third or fourth phone call if there was an effort to replace the Nominee. He's still the most experienced person in the race, and I do believe the American people want steady hands at the wheel.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you thought that was steady tonight? You're comfortable with his performance?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Like I said, he didn't, he didn't score the home run. We wanted him to hit it out the ballpark so we can enjoy the rest of the summer and maybe, in my case, just have another vacation. But look, the fact is that Joe Biden, you know, tried to get his message out to the American people that I'm here to fight for you, I'm here to lower the cost, but most importantly, I'm here to continue to make America strong and, and, and more united. He couldn't get that message out. He was busy fact-checking, busy looking, saying, I can't believe it. Joe Biden had to talk to the American people and hit out of the ballpark. It was a missed opportunity, but he's still in the race. This race is not over.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really interesting, the point you make there, Donna, and I just wanna underscore that. 'Cause it's important that I follow up on the idea that - and you heard Mary and Jon talk about this, the falsehoods that might've come from the other podium, that, that you believe that the President, President Biden was, in some ways, surprised or trying to fact-check in real time what he was witnessing on that stage, and, and, and lost his own message in the process.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That, that's the problem. Donald Trump came in as the rally Trump. You wanna know what Donald Trump showed up? The one that I get to see every weekend at a rally, because I watch Donald Trump. I listen to Donald Trump. He didn't have to make it up, because that's what he's been saying. And it's been good enough for over 40, 50 percent, maybe 50 percent of the American people.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Joe Biden had a difficult task tonight, and that inquires to tell the American people, give the narrative, here's what I'm doing, and here's what I plan to do over the next four years. Instead, I think he was trying to fact-check, and I, I, I believe it was a missed opportunity. But he scored a lot of singles. And you'll see a lot of, you'll see a lot of pundits, including some of my colleagues on the DNC that will be a little nervous. I'm not nervous. I know Joe Biden. He'll come back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna Brazile, our thanks to you as always. Reince Priebus, I want to get your reaction, as well. You heard Donna use the word disappointed in the performance tonight, though she said he a number of singles. She also took aim at the opponent on the stage there, former President Trump, saying that so much of what he said wasn't based in fact, that the President was trying to take incoming in that way, and also trying to get his message out. What do you make of President Biden's performance tonight, and what do you make of former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I thought President Trump did what he needed to do, which was, number one, I, I think, for the most part, he was civil. I think, secondly, he stuck to the issues. I think he did very well on immigration and the economy, pointing out where Joe Biden failed, and then he let Joe Biden talk, which is what we're talking about right now. This was an absolute dumpster fire, train wreck. I think the DNC's, Donna's gonna get her invitation to a Zoom call tonight from the DNC. All the talk is about replacing Joe Biden on the ballet. If you're Gretchen Whitmer or Gavin Newsom, you'll probably be waiting by the phone. And it's not easy.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I now that. But it's going to take Joe Biden deciding that he should step aside. I don't know if he's going to do that, but I think there are going to be a lot of people talking to him about stepping aside. Look, Joe Biden needed to do two things, and we talked about it before the debate. He needed to articulate a clear vision for what he is going to do as president moving forward, and he needed to do it in a way that gave people confidence that he had the mental acuity to deliver. He failed on both of those marks.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The spin after this debate is going to be more important in influencing those people in the middle, the very tiny fraction in the middle. The spin afterwards is going to be more important than even what they saw there, and it's gonna be over and over and over about Joe Biden's mental acuity being in the toilet.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Reince, appreciate it. Donna, I'll let you respond. He says you're gonna get a Zoom invite and, and very soon, at that matter.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't care. I'm gonna stick with Joe Biden. Let me just say something. Donald Trump also had to convince Independents that he will protect the integrity of our elections. There are, there are lots of police officers in the Metropolitan DC area who works for the Capitol Police and the DC Police. They are still dealing with their injuries. They're still dealing with the trauma. The notion that those Americans came to, for a peaceful protest is wrong. And that's why I think Joe scored a single on that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Donna and Reince, we appreciate it, as always. I want to bring in our senior justice correspondent, Pierre Thomas. Pierre, you, you heard President Biden tonight reference the former president for the first time on, certainly on a debate stage, of course, but, for the first time, face-to-face, as a convicted felon. You saw Donald Trump's response in that split screen.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Indeed, David, and I thought that President Biden got most animated when he was talking about January 6th in terms of the fact that Trump won't acknowledge that many of the people there committed criminal acts. Biden was clearly angry about that, but he did not really bore in on that, because of some of the things that Donna was talking about. Also, the fact that Donald Trump is now a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden tried to bore in on that. Former President Trump ignored him pretty much on that count, but he did, Trump, over and over again, talk about how Biden had tried to weaponize the Justice Department. The Justice Department continues to maintain that they've been pursuing the facts and the law and they will continue to do so, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pierre up on Capitol Hill tonight. Pierre, thank you as, as always. Linsey Davis, I, I think about something you said before we went into the debate tonight, which is, America knows these two men, that this is, in some ways, and not to sound superficial, but it is an image contest.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) An image contest tonight about aptitude, ability, mental acuity. What do you make of what you saw?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENTAL FITNESS TO BE PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Image perhaps even more so than the issues with regard to Joe Biden, at least. And I have to go back to that FiveThirtyEight/Ipsos poll that, again, it came out today, but prior to the debate, and so, I think that we have those numbers. But Biden mental fitness to be president, only 27 percent of those who were polled said it was good or excellent, poor or terrible, 54 percent, so, more than half prior to this debate tonight, were concerned, and, and feeling that he's poor or terrible mental acuity. As far as on the side, Trump mental health fitness to be president, good or excellent, 39 percent, poor or terrible, 46 percent. So, so nearly half.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I think that, that Biden had a missed opportunity when he was asked directly by Dana Bash about his age and being 81, how he's going to be going forward mentally. And, and he talked about how the first half of his career he spent being criticized about being so young, and then he said, look at my record, look at what I've done. I think that a number of people don't want to look to the past, they want to look, can you do it now? The same time, when Donald Trump was asked the same question, he, he really just devolved into a, a, how about their, their golf game.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's hard to imagine, Martha, with just a few moments left in this segment, when he pivots from age, when he's asked the central question of the night.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. And he pivots from age an, and he did that many times. He just had those pauses. I want to go back to those voters that we started the show with, did they get their questions answered? One of the few things they got validated, there was this question of age, especially with Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, all. We'll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now after our coverage of the first Presidential debate, a lot of talk about both of their performances tonight. But of the President, President Biden, the White House tonight saying...\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The campaign is saying that he did find his groove. They said Trump lied a lot and people don't like that. They feel like the President was solid on substance and that that is where he had his best performance.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All right. Mary Bruce, thank you. We're gonna continue our coverage on parts of the network in the West, and certainly on ABC News Live. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020240705ek6r0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE BIDEN VS TRUMP: THE PRESIDENTIAL DEBATE PRE-TALK\b0\par\par\uc2 10,029 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, BIDEN, TRUMP, 538, POLL, ISSUES, VOTERS, ABORTION, UKRAINE, ISRAEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS: THE RACE FOR THE WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, and it's great to have you with us here for the presidential debate. It starts in just under an hour now. We will carry the debate live right here on ABC, of course. A presidential debate the likes of which we've never seen before, a sitting president, a former president. The race is a dead heat, essentially tied. The polls out tonight, and we'll show you. And as you know, the stakes are enormous. President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are about to square off in the first of two scheduled debates. For the first time a sitting president will debate a former president. And this will be the earliest presidential debate ever.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Both campaigns looking to shake up the race tonight. Both candidates looking to make their case and to try to pull ahead. President Biden and former President Trump standing on the same stage together, and standing before a divided nation amid polls showing this race essentially locked even. Election Day five months away. We have our Powerhouse political team right in the studio tonight. Our correspondents standing by across the country in the key battlegrounds, in Ukraine, in Israel. And you will also hear from the American voters right here tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight's debate happening earlier in the election cycle than we've seen before. Both President and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  agreeing to move up the timetable, essentially, before early voting begins in the fall. Tonight's debate also features the two oldest presidential candidates in US history. President Biden is now 81. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is 78. Polls repeatedly show that many Americans don't want either one of these candidates, which is why tonight could prove pivotal. Who can make the case? Who will show they are up to the job? Will either of them be able to change minds tonight?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is also now the first convicted felon to run on a major party ticket while simultaneously battling criminal trials in Washington, Florida, and Georgia. Some voters have signaled this has changed their thinking, but many more have said it makes no difference at all. President Biden has spent several days now in debate prep at Camp David leading up to tonight, holding several 90-minute mock debates before taking the stage this evening. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  holding several battles, rallies I should say, ahead of tonight's showdown. Also, behind the scenes, some policy sessions with his advisers. The debate tonight moderated by CNN. It will air right here on ABC.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We should also note here tonight that ABC News will moderate the presidential debate this September. Linsey Davis, who's sitting here right to my side, she and I will moderate. And we have offered that debate to all of the networks to carry as well, just as we're doing right here tonight. So, we are about 57 minutes or so from the start of this debate. We will carry it live again here on ABC. Our Powerhouse political team right here to set up the night. What to watch for, what could make a difference, what the stakes are. This will very likely, as we all know in this room, be a consequential night in this race for president. There is a lot on the line for both of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get right to ABC's Rachel Scott. She's gonna kick it off for us tonight. She's live in the spin room in Atlanta, the setting for tonight's debate. And Rachel, the debate there will have no audience, the microphones will be muted, only on when a particular candidate, it's their turn to speak. And you've learned more tonight about the distance between these two candidates, about notes or no notes, and whether or not they'll have interaction with advisers during the actual debate?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, David. This debate is going to look and feel a whole lot different for the candidates and also for the American people that are watching. So, the candidates themselves will be eight feet apart on that debate stage. Their microphones will only be turned on when it is their turn to speak. They will not be allowed to communicate or talk to their aides or advisers during commercial breaks.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They will also not be allowed to take anything prewritten onto that debate stage. They will be allowed to write things down once they get behind the podium. Of course, there will be no studio audience tonight, no one to cheer them on. That's something \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has complained about, but his advisers are hoping that it does keep him more on message, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, let me ask you about that, Rachel, because I know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's team behind the scenes, you've got new reporting on this in recent days that they actually hope that not having an audience sort of egging on the candidates, things they like to hear, might actually help, they believe, keep their candidate, the former President, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , on message in a way that perhaps we haven't seen?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we know that the former president, David, feeds off of a crowd. When you look at those past debates, he really does feed off of the crowd cheering after he delivers some of those attack lines. And so, that's why we've seen the former president complain about not having a studio audience there. But his advisers do tell me they believe that this will help keep the former president on message, focus on the critical issues, crime, immigration, inflation, things that they believe are top of mind to voters.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, a big question is, what version of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  shows up tonight? Is it the combative former president who's on the attack? Or, is it a much more measured version of the former president who wants to stay on message, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And let me ask you, Rachel, about President Biden himself. He'll be on the stage, of course, opposite \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It was four years ago, we remember the debates back then, the sort of, the cross-talk, the interruptions. Is it the hope of the Biden campaign, of President Biden himself, that this new rule about the mic being off when you're not speaking will actually help the President get his message across on some of these key issues?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is the hope, David. But I will tell you, I've been talking to advisers for the Biden campaign who have been here in the spin room and they do acknowledge one thing. That even though the viewers may not hear an interruption because the former president's mic might be turned off when it's not his turn to speak, that does not mean that President Biden will not hear it himself. And so, his advisers are hoping that the President ignores sort of any distractions. But they do know that the former president could still make some sort of off-handed comment, some type of attack line if he wanted to. They're hoping that the President stays focused, stays on message. But we'll have to see how this all plays out, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Rachel Scott right there in Atlanta for us leading our coverage off. Rachel, thank you as always. Fifty-three minutes until the debate. We're going to carry it right here on ABC. But who's counting? Everybody in this room, and everybody at home, we know these presidential debates, Mary, have outsized audiences. This is the earliest Presidential debate ever. This was part of the strategy of the White House, the Biden campaign, and the Trump campaign. They both agreed to do this earlier to reach voters, and particularly because they know this race has been a dead heat for months.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) America knows both of these men. So, what is the President hoping to do tonight to try to convince people who've already made up their minds that perhaps they should give him another chance?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, they know that not everyone has been engaged in this race yet, exactly as you said. And that is why they wanted to have this debate so early. The President's goal here is really to make clear to Americans, especially those critical Independent voters who maybe haven't been fully engaged yet, what is really at stake here. The President's goal is to hold \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accountable for what he did when he was in office. He wants to remind voters of the chaos of the Trump years.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And yes, he will argue that another Trump term would represent and present a danger to American democracy. He's gonna tout his record, of course, but he really wants to remind everyone of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was like when he was in office. They see this as a jump start moment to try and get folks engaged, and convince those voters that he can do this. But perhaps more important than substance, David, is going to be the President's performance. He knows age is a factor. He has to show voters he is up for the job and that he can win.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that really is one of the key issues here, if not the key issue, the issue of age, of fitness, if he's up to a second term. We just saw the images of him arriving in Atlanta late today, then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  arriving shortly thereafter. We know the First Lady, Jill Biden is there. She's offered reassuring words for the President, asking the American people to listen to her husband but also to his heart. But bottom line here tonight, what is the biggest issue the President and this team believes he faces? Is it age? Mental fitness? Is it the economy? What are they looking at?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, the President has been making his case out on the trail for weeks now. This is the largest audience, though, that he is going to have so far in this race to make that case. Yes, they know he needs a strong performance. But they feel that this is the best chance they have to really wake up voters and explain what is at stake here. They also feel, look, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters, they're not moving. They're not gonna budge. They aren't someone they can persuade. But they do feel that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn't going to be able to, to grow much and that's where they see opportunity for them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We also have images of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  arriving there late today. He talked about a very different sort of prep work going into this debate for the former president. He said he has spent his whole life getting ready for this. There have been some policy sessions, as I mentioned earlier, with advisers. But he went out and held rallies. He polled the crowds as he, we have seen him do in years past, on which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should show up tonight, the combative one or, or the more statesman-like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But Mary Bruce, the question for both the former President, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and for President Biden, does President Biden bring up the fact that he is now a convicted felon, his opponent?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I certainly think he is going to bring it up. Look, he's running a campaign ad that is centered on this. We have seen the President comment on this. I think the question is, how much emphasis does he put on it? How far does he go in hitting the, the former president on this? This is a very different country and a very different set of circumstances than the last time that they faced off. You have had January 6th, you have had now the former president convicted of those 34 felony counts, the President is going to remind voters of all of that as they go into this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the question is, how much do you lean in on that and...\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...what will the counterpunch be from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if you go there? Let's bring in Linsey Davis. So, Linsey, you and I are gonna be taking notes off to the side on this debate.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we sure will.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we've got a few months to prepare for, for the, the September debate between these two men. Let me ask you, though, because you and I have talked about this before. I mentioned it earlier. This country knows these two men. They both have been president in front of the country, and most people have made up their minds on them and so many Americans have said in poll after poll, that we don't want either one of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And there's a name for these highly coveted voters. They are called the "double haters," right? It might sound like a...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The "double haters."\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...a song. It's a familiar tune. These are the people who don't like either of the choices. This is really the jump ball. This is the target for these two men on the stage. Because they potentially, based on what they see or don't see on that stage tonight, could perhaps hold their nose and go and vote and choose one of them come Election Day. If you look at our 538/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll out just today, of those polled, 21 percent feel that they are double haters. They don't like either of the choices. But nearly half of those voters consider themselves triple haters. They don't like any of the choices, including RFK Jr. And so, when you consider that our last presidential election was decided by 44,000 votes in three swing states, these double haters matter.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, and the double haters isn't a surprise to us. That's the, that's the political phrase they've given to all the political pundits. We, we are pundits for the night, but that's the phrase that's out there. But I, I think just regular folks, you hear it all the time, you know, how did we get here again?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How, how is it possible that President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up on that stage all over again? But it is. And so, let's get to our political director, Rick Klein, who has been looking at the numbers over the course of many weeks, but most importantly, going into tonight, several major national polls showing how close this race is.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2024 GENERAL ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL POLLING AVERAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, David, you get anything from Trump leading by six in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll to Biden losing by two in a Fox News poll from just a couple of days ago, and what it adds up to is a very tight race. This is the 538 polling average. And check that out, that is two-tenths of a percentage point that separate Trump and Biden. And what's striking to me is that it is barely changed for months. You have the two of them just running almost exactly even. The last couple of weeks, the last month since the Trump conviction, got Biden a little bit of a bump, but really not that much. It's essentially a tied race nationally.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But everyone wanted to know that if there was a conviction in any of these cases, now obviously the New York City case just happened to come first, the hush money case, conviction on multiple felony counts, if there would be any movement. Really the only major poll, if you go back to the other screen, was the Fox News poll that showed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  benefiting. That's really the only poll that shows him pulling out ahead in this way. The rest of them tend to show \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with a little bit of wind behind his back.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, and it's really all within the margin of error. And I don't think there's anyone that would doubt it's gonna be an enormously close race for the same factors that you're talking about. Linsey mentions people that don't like either one of the candidates. That's how it manifests itself, is you have a very tight race where there isn't a lot of room for movement really for either candidate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You also have been studying the key issues, and we're about to see something very telling from the voters in battlegrounds on all of these issues. If you go down each one of these issues, Rick, show us what they're saying is the most important issue, and then which candidate voters are saying in these polls actually would do a better job than the other?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP ELECTION ISSUES\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, David, this is again from our Ipsos/538 poll that, that came out just today, and inflation is the big one. Half of voters say that's gonna be important to their vote. That is an enormous number. You've got about a third of voters, little more than that, that say immigration is the number one issue. And guess who that advantages? That is Trump, trusted by a more than, by a 10-point edge on the issue of, of inflation and 12 points on the issues of, of immigration and the border.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The issues that benefit \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are a little bit lower down on the list, but they are intensely felt issues. Political extremism, that's a Biden advantage. Abortion rights, which has been all over the news recently, that's a big Biden advantage. So, I think that gives you some sense of the kind of things Biden wants to be talking about versus the kind of things Trump wants to be talking about tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rick. We know we have Martha Raddatz here. She's just back from the, the, actually the edge of Gaza. You were on the shore of Gaza reporting for "World News Tonight" on the pier that the US has built. We'll talk about that later in the hour. \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  here, they both do "This Week" on Sunday mornings. And I'm dying to get your thoughts on everything here. But I wanted to show you something that I, these, these two have not seen before tonight, nor have Donna Brazile, Reince Priebus, and the rest of the team.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is the kind of material, though, we know that both campaigns study, they want to get their hands on. They want to know what the voters want to see tonight, what they need to hear in order to change their minds about the candidates. And we sent our team out to the key battlegrounds, Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, Georgia, these double haters that Linsey was talking about, American voters out there who say, you know, I'm not sold really on either one of these guys, and how, how is this our choice? So, we asked them a very simple question. Going into tonight's debate, what do you want to hear? What do you need to hear? And I want to get all of your reaction on the back side. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I would like to hear strength, someone that's strong, powerful, honesty, backbone of America.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm looking for a strong economy. My husband owns a business. Self-employed people are really struggling in this economy to make sure their customers are taken care of even if they're doing okay business-wise. So, it's the middle class person that's struggling.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I would need to hear from the candidates that they really understand what the average person is going through.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The rampant inflation that we're in right now is critical. I mean, it's, it's eating us alive.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you know, we just want to hear from the candidates and see what they're gonna do to boost the economy, put money back into Americans' pockets and stabilize those interest rates.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Here in Scranton, like, like the price of gas. And with inflation, like, filling up my car every week and spending so much money on gas is something that takes up my whole paycheck.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I feel like I just need to hear something that, like, gives me hope for the future in general. I feel like sometimes it's tough to listen to either side.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I just don't want false promises. You know what I mean? Like, if you guys are saying that you're gonna do something, actually follow through with it.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm not looking to have anybody that wants to be in America not be here. But I think that everybody needs to be properly vetted and pull their weight once they get here.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think this election is, is, is critical to the country. I really do think that our democracy as we know it is at stake.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm just not very excited about the choices that we have for, for president. You know, we got two old white guys running against one another. Both of them could just about be my grandfather. The most important issue for me is the environment and climate change.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I have never felt worse about the options for presidential candidates this year. I really feel like it's not a valid choice.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, I'm at a point where I just can't really, you know, have a decision because of what my choices are, you know. I'm going to vote, but I'm just not comfortable with who I'm voting for.\par \par \uc2 POTENTIAL VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We have two people who have been there before, and, you know, some people feel in one way or the other, especially here in Pennsylvania. So, the debate is gonna do a lot for them to, I think, lean one way or another.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, and it's really incredible to hear, isn't it. Forty-three minutes now until this debate. We will carry it here on ABC News. Martha Raddatz, you have been on the other side of the table in these debates. You and I have both been across from both Mr. Biden and Mr. Trump in various, different scenarios and debates along the way. These debates can turn on a dime. But you heard these issues over and over again. And we heard it everywhere, inflation, the economy, immigration.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and these are the faces behind the poll numbers. This is what people care about. We can talk about, we can look how we think the debate went. But they're looking for very specific things that matter to them. That's what matters to them. That should be important to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They want to reach those people. They want to answer those questions. They want to give those people faith in them as a candidate. And that is very tricky. And, and as you say, David, turning on a dime. You've moderated debates. Linsey, you've moderated debates. I've moderated debates.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can go in there as a candidate or a - or a moderator and things change. You can study, you can, I, I always say it's like studying for a college entrance exam and taking it live in front of 70 million people. But things can go wrong. You can know all you can about the, about the Pentagon, about foreign policy, about anything. But the body language up there, I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was looming over \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  at one of the debates. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was much more aggressive in a debate than I thought he would be. So, things are very, very, it's a tightrope up there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we always feel it across the desk but we always are reminded that it's, it's the candidates up there that the people at home are really, truly studying. And \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , you covered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the White House, chief White House correspondent for many years during that era.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is gonna show up to